EINS

Was für eine Absteige! Gut, ich hatte gewusst, dass es nicht das Grand Elysée   sein würde. Aber diese Unterkunft ist wirklich das Letzte. Eine Zumutung.   Muffig und dunkel. Und dreckig. Ich gebe mir Mühe, mich nicht genauer   umzuschauen, aber der Schmutz meiner Vorgänger ist unübersehbar. Es ist   offensichtlich, dass hier schon ziemlich lange nicht mehr saubergemacht wurde.   Mir ist zum Heulen zumute - wie konnte ich nur in eine solche Lage geraten?   Heute Morgen noch im Salon von Schloss Eschersbach, und jetzt das. Nun fange ich   wirklich an zu heulen.

»Schnauze, du nervst!«, kommt es keine zwei Sekunden später von links.

Richtig: Das Schlimmste hatte ich noch nicht erwähnt: meine Zimmernachbarn.   Fünf an der Zahl, die meisten von ihnen unglaublich verwahrlost. Und das nicht   nur optisch. Ungebildeter Pöbel, der in einem Adligen wie mir natürlich gleich   ein willkommenes Opfer ausgemacht hat. Mein Stammbaum reicht bis 1723, diese   Ignoranten neben mir kennen vermutlich nicht einmal den Unterschied zwischen   Markgraf und Markklößchen.

Vor meinem inneren Auge taucht mein Großvater auf. pflegte er zu sagen. Ach Opili, wenn du   mich jetzt sehen könntest - ich bin definitiv ganz unten angelangt. Bei diesem   Gedanken heule ich noch lauter. Irgendjemand muss mich hier einfach   rausholen!»Wo ein von Eschersbach   ist, ist oben. Vergiss das nie!«, 

»Komm, Süßer, beruhige dich.« Eine Hand greift durch das Gitter und krault   mich hinter den Ohren. »Gleich gibt es ein leckeres Fresschen, und dann sieht   die Welt ganz anders aus. Der erste Tag ist für alle schlimm.«

Hm, eine nette Stimme. Interessiert gucke ich zur Seite, um festzustellen, zu   wem sie gehört: Neben dem Zwinger steht eine junge Frau in einer Latzhose und   lächelt mich aufmunternd an. Ihre Hand riecht nach gewöhnlichem Dosenfutter,   trotzdem hat die Berührung etwas Tröstliches. Ich schlecke ihre Finger ab, sie   beginnt zu kichern.

»Ja, ja, das schmeckt dir, nicht wahr?«, flüstert sie mir zu.

O je, wenn die wüsste - an meinen verwöhnten Dackelgaumen habe ich bisher   eigentlich nur frisches Herz und Pansen gelassen. Fertigfutter war die absolute   Ausnahme, das gab es wirklich nur, wenn Emilia, unsere Köchin, krank war oder   Urlaub hatte. Bei dem Gedanken an Emilia krampft sich mein kleines Herz   zusammen, und ich muss ein bisschen winseln. Als ich mich heute Morgen von ihr   verabschiedet habe, hat sie geweint. Weiß der Teufel, wie Menschen das immer   hinbekommen. Aber zum ersten Mal in meinem Leben hätte ich viel dafür gegeben,   auch ein paar Tränen vergießen zu können.

»Du Armer, noch so schlimm?«, erkundigt sich die Pflegerin mitfühlend. »Mach   dir keine Sorgen, du bist so süß, wir finden bestimmt bald ein neues Frauchen   oder Herrchen für dich. Versprochen!« Dann streicht sie mir noch einmal über den   Kopf und zieht ihre Hand wieder durch das Gitter.

Ich drehe mich um und trotte in die andere Ecke des Zwingers. Dort wirft ein   Sonnenstrahl noch ein einladend helles Fleckchen auf den Boden, und ich   beschließe, es mir ein bisschen gemütlich zu machen.

Offenbar bin ich nicht der Einzige, der auf diese Idee gekommen ist: Bevor   ich mich hinlegen kann, tritt mir ein riesiges schwarzes Irgendwas direkt auf   die Pfoten.

»Kleiner, ich glaube, du verziehst dich besser auf die andere Seite. Hier ist   mein Platz.« Um seinem Wunsch Nachdruck zu verleihen, kommen die letzten Worte   nur als heiseres Knurren.

Was für eine lächerliche Töle! Meint die ernsthaft, sie könne mich in die   Flucht schlagen? Mich, dessen Ahnen noch mit dem letzten Kaiser zur Jagd   gegangen sind? Ich schüttle den Kopf.

»Ich glaube nicht«, entgegne ich so würdevoll, wie es mir unter diesen   widrigen Umständen möglich ist, »dass in diesem Etablissement mit Reservierungen   gearbeitet wird. Ich war vor Ihnen da, also werde ich mich auch auf diesen Platz   legen. Sie gestatten?« Mit diesen Worten schiebe ich Mr. Irgendwas zur Seite und   lege mich schnell hin. Er starrt mich völlig fassungslos an. So viel zivilen   Widerstand hat er wohl noch nie erlebt. Zufrieden räkle ich mich. Opili hatte   doch Recht - ein von Eschersbach ist eben auch oben, wenn er unten ist.

Während ich noch darüber sinniere, wann hier wohl mit der zweifelsohne eher   bescheidenen Mahlzeit zu rechnen ist, verfinstert sich mein sonniges Fleckchen.   Nanu, eine Wölke? Ich blicke nach oben, um festzustellen, was in aller Welt hier   auf einmal diesen unerfreulichen Schatten wirft - und schaue direkt in das   Gesicht eines ziemlich ungemütlich aussehenden Boxers. Er schiebt seine Nase   ganz dicht an meine und verströmt dabei einen Geruch, dass es mir regelrecht den   Atem verschlägt.

»Pass auf, du aufgeblasener Zwerg: Wenn du nicht neu wärst, wärst du jetzt   ein toter Hund. Hier gelten unsere Regeln, besser du hältst dich daran. Also   wenn mein Freund Bozo sagt, dass du dich verpissen sollst, dann ...«, er kommt   noch ein bisschen näher und schnappt blitzschnell nach mir.

Aua! Ein stechender Schmerz fährt durch mein rechtes Ohr. Hilfe! Der ist ja   gemeingefährlich! Ich belle aufgeregt - offensichtlich bin ich unter militante   und gewaltbereite Straßenköter geraten. Aber sosehr ich auch belle - niemand   kommt. Nicht einmal die junge Frau in der Latzhose. Boxer und Bozo grinsen   selbstzufrieden.

»Spar dir die Mühe. Die hört dich momentan nicht - ist zu den Katzen   rübergegangen. Wir könnten dich jetzt richtig plattmachen, und niemand würde   dir helfen. Ein toter Hund mehr in der Statistik dieser Bude. Wen interessiert   das schon?«

Ich merke, wie sich meine Nackenhaare sträuben und es mir eiskalt den Rücken   herunterläuft. Bozo, die schwarze Töle, baut sich wieder vor mir auf.

»Also, was ist jetzt? Wenn ich sage verpiss dich?«

»Dann verpisse ich mich?«, ergänze ich seinen Satz.

»Richtig. Hundert Punkte. Braves Hundchen.«

Bozo verpasst meiner empfindlichen Nase noch einen kräftigen Stüber mit   seiner ungepflegten Pfote. Erschreckt springe ich zur Seite und laufe auf   zittrigen Beinen in die andere Ecke des Zwingers. Wahrscheinlich machen sie sich gerade über mich lustig. Eigentlich bin   ich sehr gerne ein Dackel, aber in diesem Moment wäre ich viel lieber ein   Kampfhund. Staffordshire, Pitbull oder irgendetwas anderes in Richtung meinem 

Lizenz   zum Töten.

"Don't be sad," Fritz pats me on the side, "the nurse just said: You're such a real human type, someone will take you out of here soon. And then you'll show those two idiots over there the middle finger, because no one wants that."

I look at Fritz thoughtfully. Hopefully he's right.

The next morning I feel like a rag doll. I barely slept—and when my eyes finally closed for five minutes, I had horrendous nightmares. Of boxers and pit bulls chasing me through the kennel, and vast amounts of canned food that tasted absolutely disgusting. Tired, I hobble over to Fritz, who's wagging his tail at the cage door.

"Morning," I ask him, "why are you so awake and in such a good mood already?"

"Well, today is visitor day," he replies. "And if someone comes looking for a dog, I want to make a good impression. I'm not getting any younger, so it's important to seem dynamic and cheerful."

Is he right? Honestly, I have no desire to put on the trained dachshund act. But the thought of settling in here for an extended stay is terrifying. So I stand next to Fritz, unmotively wagging my tail a bit. And would people really fall for such a cheap trick? Unbelievable.

"By the way," Fritz asks, "what's your name?"

"Carl-Leopold," I answer brusquely.

"Carl-Leopold? That's a strange name for a dog."

"I don't think so. It depends on where you come from."

Bane! What does he know about nice names? "I'm from Eschersbach," I proudly add.

"Eschersbach? Doesn't ring a bell," Fritz grumbles, continuing to wag his tail.

I sigh. Truly, he's a bane. A nice one, though—just a bane. Just as I'm about to start introducing Fritz to the basics of my family history, a door slams open next to our kennel. My senses are instantly electrified. Not because of the noise—after all, this place has a noise level that would make a dachshund's ears droop. No, it's the utterly indescribable scent wafting towards me. Even Fritz seems to have caught wind of it, stopping his silly wagging and pressing his snout through the bars instead.

"Do you smell that?" I ask him. He nods. "Amazing, right?"

"Yes, incredible!" I agree.

"That's the most beautiful scent I've ever smelled on a person," I conclude.

It's clear this scent belongs to a human. Every dog can pick it up immediately. But who is this human? Not some grubby meathead like sausage or chocolate cookie. No, more like...I scratch my head...exactly—a beautiful summer day. A happy summer day. Lots of flowers, a hint of strawberries, and a wisp of mint. Fantastic.

"Probably we'll be disappointed when we see the person," Fritz says matter-of-factly.

"Really?" I want to know. "I haven't noticed any connection there myself. Can't judge."

"Oh yes. Absolutely."

We eye the door warily. And then she comes, followed by the overalls. Fritz was completely wrong. Because for a person, she's gorgeous, like an angel. She chats with the other woman and laughs while doing so. Her eyes laugh—which looks especially beautiful and is quite rare among humans. Most of them just crinkle their mouths when they laugh. Which is a shame. So if I could laugh, I'd make my eyes do it too. It definitely looks better that way.

"Hmm," Fritz says, "so you want a smaller dog? And preferably a younger one?" The angel nods.

Fritz's ears droop immediately. He knows what that means: no lady for him again. Because Münsterlanders aren't small—and Fritz isn't young anymore. He hangs his head. "Good luck!" he whispers to me, then trots past me. Of course I feel bad for him—but maybe this is really my chance? I try Fritz's tactic again, wagging my tail excitedly and trying to bark as cheerfully as possible. In fact, the two women are now walking straight towards me.

"This is our junior," she says, gesturing through the bars. "We just got him. About six months old."

She extends her hand through the bars, and I lick it clean right away. Well, if that doesn't make a good impression, I don't know what does. The angel bends down to me.

"Well, what a cutie! Such a sweet guy!" I bounce up and down excitedly.

"Yes, he's a real beauty," she says. "A dachshund mix."

Ouch. Mix. Ugh. That stings. I immediately stop acting like the enthusiastic dog. Not that it wouldn't be true—on the contrary. Miss Overalls is right. And in doing so, she hits on my shame: I'm a mutt. The result of Mom's affair with a very dashing terrier. Precisely why I'm here. Because although Carl-Leopold von Eschersbach, I'm not a purebred dachshund—not suitable for hunting and certainly not breeding. That's what the old lord of Eschersbach said before he put me in a box and drove me here. Emilia cried, but she already had my sister, and two dogs were more than enough for her.

Apparently I started whining, because now the angel extends her hand through the bars and pets me.

"Oh, poor thing, what's wrong? Are you sad?"

How embarrassing. An Eschersbach doesn't cry like that. And then in front of such a beautiful woman! Heaven help me, where will this end? But apparently it was just the right thing to do because now she stands up, points at me, and says: "I want him. Definitely. Can I take him with me?"

Overalls nods. "Come on in, then we'll take care of the formalities. He's been vaccinated, he comes from a very careful breeder. A little accident, that's all."

She chuckles at her own joke. And I'd love to give her a good lick right now. But I don't. Otherwise I might have to stay here.

Twenty minutes later, I'm safely tucked away in the box on the backseat of Carolyn's car. Carolyn—that's what I've found out when saying goodbye. A nice name. So noble. Probably from a noble family, just like a dog like me can tell. Carolyn is in good spirits. She whistles a tune and occasionally checks the rearview mirror to see how I'm doing.

"So, my sweetie," she says, "now you'll get to know your new home. I'm very curious to see what you think."

And I am too! Will it be as nice as Schloss Eschersbach? With a big park? And lots of rabbit hutches? The car slows down, and finally comes to a stop. Carolyn opens the door and pulls out the box. Now I have strawberries and mint right in front of my nose, and I'd love nothing more than to lick Carolyn from head to toe. But I have to be patient while getting out of the swinging box.

My surroundings grow darker and sway more violently; Caroline carries me up a flight of stairs. I try to sniff out my new home through the bars of my box with my nose. In any case, it seems to be a place where different people live—and different animals as well. At first glance, I can make out at least one cat.

Caroline sets down the box, and I hear her unlock a door. She pushes the box gently with her foot. Then she fiddles with the lid, opens it, and carefully lifts me out.

"Here we are! You'll be living here from now on. Take your time to look around, little guy."

At first, I see nothing—it's so bright in here. I blink cautiously and try to get used to the light. Dimly, I recognize that we're probably standing in a human living room. In front of the window sits a large couch, which looks like a tiny dachshund could very comfortably take a nap on it. Does Caroline allow that? Anyway, it was strictly forbidden at Schloss Eschersbach. Which naturally led to my sister and me jumping up onto the sofa in the salon—just because it was so hilariously ironic when the old lord of the manor, despite his cane, shot past us like greased lightning and wildly waved his stick around to shoo us away.

I bound for the couch and sniff its lining. Hm, strawberries and mint as well? But something else too. No animal. More like a human. I immerse myself deeply in the scent. Hm, not only do I have a new mom, but also a dad? This definitely isn't a woman's scent. While I'm still pondering, Caroline lifts me up and sets me down on the couch—right next to her. Enthusiastically, I lick her hands—this woman clearly knows what dachshunds love. She laughs and pulls her hands away. Then she looks at me thoughtfully.

"Well then, little guy: I've taken care of everything for you—a crate, a leash, a bowl, food. The only thing left is... " I shake my head; in my opinion, that sounded pretty complete. "You need a nice name."

I squeak surprised—I already have a name! Or did Caroline just drop me off at the animal shelter without telling me anything about myself? Such heartlessness!

Apparently noticing my indignation, Caroline lifts me onto her lap, and we lock eyes.

"Hm, so what kind of little guy like you should I call you? What do you look like?"

I try to puff out my chest and look as dignified as possible. Maybe she'll then come up with something like Carl-Leopold? To emphasize this appearance, I bell majestically twice. Come on, Caroline, think hard!

"In any case, you're not an ordinary dog—you seem to have real character."

Yes! Exactly! She's got it! With a majestic toss of my head, I declare, "I'm Herkules!"

What? HERKULES? An ancient Greek instead of old aristocratic?

TWO

Herkules! Okay, Caroline may not have much taste when it comes to dachshund names, and I'll need some time to get used to this strange new name. But she does have a knack for finding the right neighborhood. In fact, the house I'm living in now is almost as big as Schloss Eschersbach. My hunch that Caroline comes from good stock seems to be correct. The neighbors don't exactly live in modest cottages either. Right behind our house starts a park. However, I'm not sure if it belongs to Caroline alone because it's enormous. When we take a short walk there, I can't even make out where the park ends—fantastic!

And it's not just big; adventure awaits within it as well. After just a few steps, I sense the first rabbits and squirrels. Immediately, I want to run off, but then a rough tug on my neck reminds me that Caroline has something for me that is completely unfamiliar: a kind of rope she attached to my collar. Ouch! What's this for? I turn around, take the thing in my mouth, and tug on it a bit. Caroline kneels down next to me.

"Well then, Herkules? Don't you like your new leash? Or have you never gone for a walk like that before?" She seems unsure if a small dog like me can actually do this. "I'm not sure if I should just let you run around freely."

At the word "leash," I bite down even harder on the rope. I don't exactly know what it means, but it definitely sounds like something aimed against dogs.

"Oh, come now!" Caroline says and then strokes my head gently. I let go of the leash and look at her.

"I guess I'll have to buy a book on dog training or perhaps sign up for some classes with a trainer? After all, you're my very first dog ever. But yesterday, I suddenly felt like it would be nice to have such a friendly, loyal companion around."

Okay, the training part is obviously unnecessary nonsense, and I hope Caroline figures that out on her own. But the friendly, loyal nature definitely applies to me—and all dachshunds from Eschersbach. I even go so far as to say we're famous for it. "Listen, Opili says," I think gloomily in my mind. What happens if the person suddenly leaves their dachshund? That's something Opili would never have thought of, I add mentally. A momentary bad mood threatens to take hold of me, but then Caroline scratches something in her pocket. Hmh, not just anything—I recognize the scent! It's sausage meat. She actually holds a piece under my nose. "A dachshund from Eschersbach never leaves its person." Never! Remember that, Carl-Leopold!

"Here you go, my darling," she says. "Let's start our first training session with something enjoyable. I hope you like this."

I snap up and down excitedly when she offers me the sausage piece. Caroline should know that was the right idea.

"That makes you happy, doesn't it?" She suggests we visit Daniel instead of continuing leash training. It's time you meet him. He's probably working hard at this hour and might appreciate a little distraction."

I'd have preferred to stay in the park with the leash—even without it. Maybe we would've met another dog I could ask about the neighborhood. But if Daniel is that important... well, be it!

Caroline takes the same route back as we came. I do my best to trot along bravely behind her. Perhaps I'll get another sausage piece if I give her a successful demonstration now. In fact, she turns around briefly to look at me.

"Good boy, Herkules! You're learning quickly!" She praises me—unfortunately, without reaching into her pocket again. That's okay; I'm not really hungry anyway.

We're back in front of our house now. Does Daniel live here too? Caroline bends down and picks me up onto her arm.

"Time for the workshop!"

Workshop? An interesting word. What could it possibly mean? We actually go into the house, but not up the stairs, but down four steps. Then Carolin opens the door - and we stand in a room that smells incredibly of wood. I snort in surprise. Do people also have forests where houses are located? And do foxes and rabbits still live there despite this? However, I don't see any trees at all. Strange.

I hear someone whistling from a corner of the workshop thing. Could it be Daniel? Carolin carries me towards the sound. We enter a room with two large windows through which the warm afternoon sun shines directly. Just behind the windows begins a meadow that looks very pretty. In front of the windows stands a large table, and behind the table is the person who can whistle so loudly. He holds a long thing in his hands that resembles a branch with long hair. When he sees us, he puts the thing aside and stops whistling.

"Oh, hello! Did someone get lost to us? Or do we have visitors?"

Carolin shakes her head. "Neither: We have a new roommate. Let me introduce: Hercules - Daniel. Daniel - Hercules." With these words, she sets me down on the table next to the thing.

"Wait, did you buy a dachshund?"

"A dachshund mix, yes."

I can't help but shake my head vigorously and bark in indignation. The two look at me in surprise.

"Maybe he doesn't like men?" Daniel wants to know.

Carolin shrugs her shoulders and strokes me soothingly behind the ears. "I hope not. They didn't mention anything about it at the animal shelter, and I actually wanted to bring him with me to the workshop during the day."

Daniel smiles. "Well, maybe he's a proud little guy and doesn't like when you question his purebredness."

The two laugh, and Carolin takes me back into her arms.

What's so strange about that? Even though I don't know many people yet, one thing is clear: these bipeds are definitely less sensitive than we dogs. They clearly have no idea what disturbs us at the moment. All of a sudden, I get the feeling that living constantly with such a person might not always be fun. After all, Daniel was already on the right track. We'll work on the rest!

"May I hold him too?"

"Of course!" Carolin passes me over. Daniel has a firm but not unpleasant grip. He is only a little taller than Carolin, and from up here I can see that his bright hair wildly curls over his entire head.

"Well, little guy? Don't you like me?" To make it clear that I don't agree with the "no men" statement, I lick Daniel's face with my tongue.

"So much for that," Carolin rejoices. "You're getting along with him just fine."

"That makes me relieved! Because if we spend our days in threes from now on, anything else would be bad. A dachshund who constantly pinches me in the shins would have seriously disrupted our harmony."

Ah, I understand. Daniel is my lady's master. Probably, I also smelled him up in the apartment. I've heard before that people like to pair off and then stay together for a long time. I always thought it was a strange idea. But when I see the two of them, I can understand it. They seem so... familiar with each other. Almost like my Opili with the old Eschersbach. And they have been going hunting together for fifteen years. That's as good as it gets. Do Carolin and Daniel also hunt together? Or do couples do something else in pairs?

"By the way, has Mrs. Brolin called again?" Carolin asks Daniel. "She actually wanted to come by today with a cello for an appraisal. It needs restoration if it's worth it."

Cello. What a beautiful word. So soft and yet somehow... fiery. What is that? And is there any connection between the thing Daniel was holding earlier and this cello? Well, I'll find out, as of today, I'll be in this area more often.

"Yes, she called briefly and left the instrument here. It wasn't urgent for her. It's enough if you call her on Monday."

"Okay. To be honest, I'd like to take the rest of the day off and show Hercules his new home and surroundings. We've already tried a little walk in the park, but Hercules doesn't like his new leash." Maybe I'll practice a bit more with him.

"Go ahead, there's nothing urgent here anyway. At least not something I wouldn't manage without you." Daniel smiles again and passes me back to Carolin.

It's amazing what different facial expressions people can make. Of course, it's also much easier when you don't have so many hairs around your eyes and nose. Right now, Daniel looked at Carolin as if he wanted to lick her face.

But apparently, that's not something people do. At least, I've never seen them do it. By the way, they don't jump up and down either. Strange, right? But it feels so good when you're happy.

"Daniel?"

"Yes?"

"Is this okay with you about the dog?"

"Of course, don't worry."

"I mean, it was a pretty spontaneous idea, and I actually wanted to ask you first. But then I went to the animal shelter out of pure curiosity and fell in love with this little guy at once."

"He's really cute too. I can understand why you took him right away. Those big brown button eyes alone. By the way, I think he's not a mix at all. His floppy ears and relatively short legs - he has a pretty long back, though. So, in my opinion, he definitely comes from a real dachshund family, no other breeds are likely to be involved."

Daniel, you're my man! I'd love nothing more than to jump straight from Carolin's arm to Daniel and lick him all over again - his compliment feels amazing. I feel like I've grown an extra paw. Proudly, I start barking.

"That seems to make you very happy!" Carolin says, stroking my ears one last time. "Well, my sweetie, then I'll do my best to make sure you're comfortable here."

As I lie in my new basket at night, exhausted but happy. We spent an hour walking in the park and practicing with the leash. To do Carolin a favor, I usually trotted behind her, only occasionally sitting down and growling when I thought I had passed by a rabbit hole. After all, I also have a reputation as a hunting dog to uphold. But with some convincing and a few slices of meat sausage, we managed to complete a pretty large leash walk in the park. We even met a few other dogs, but I wasn't in the mood for talking. After all, there's always tomorrow.

Before I drift off to sleep, Carolin comes by one last time and tucks a soft blanket into my basket. She nuzzles me for a bit, then whispers in my ear, "You know, my sweetie, it's actually a shame I haven't gotten myself a dog yet. Here, it would be ideal for someone like you. During the day, you can come with me to the workshop or wander around our garden. And whenever I take a break or have some free time, we'll go for walks here. How does that sound?" She wants to know, and finally - finally! - I get to lick her across the face. Carolin giggles, strokes me one last time, and wishes me goodnight.

Wow, I've hit the jackpot: a nice lady, a nice guy—actually, a truly beautiful family, just like at Schloss Eschersbach. Okay, there's no Emilia, and instead of fresh panse as promised for dinner to celebrate, Carolin opened a can. But who cares? If that's what civilian life is like, I'm game. At least it seems honest here, and I haven't met anyone as cold-hearted as the old Eschersbach yet. Yes, the luck of the little people has a calming effect all around. The nobility can do without me. Just thinking about that delicious sausage makes my eyes close.

THREE

I'd rather plug my ears or hide under the sofa. What I'm experiencing right now gives me the creeps. A man I've never seen before just walked into our—or rather, Carolin's apartment, and he started yelling immediately. I'm speechless. Who is this horrible guy? And where is Daniel? Won't he protect his lady? Maybe I should look for him quickly so he can help Carolin. But that would mean passing by the screaming man, and frankly, I don't dare. Is it really a bad habit to hate people who shout so loudly? My ears are top-notch, if I may say so myself. In my opinion, humans shouldn't have to speak so loudly or—which they seem to love doing—listen to incredibly loud music. But that's beside the point.

This man reminds me of Bozo and Boxer in his aggressive way. He's huge, a whole head taller than Carolin. And his hair is just as black as Bozo's fur. He makes the same proud movements and has the same unrefined tone. Apparently, he's a proletarian through and through, just like Bozo-Boxer.

He waves his arms around wildly and points—at me! Oh no!

"Did you buy a dog? I was on a business trip for three days, and when I came back, you had bought one?"

Bozo-Boxer's face is bright red. Something tells me I don't quite fit with him the way I do with Daniel. But that can wait. The main thing is my master likes me. And hopefully, that will happen soon. Even with usually bad human ears, this shouting can be heard in the workshop. Now Carolin bravely steps in front of the man. Obviously, she wants to defend me. A noble gesture, but it's just not going to work like this. If anyone needs defending here, it's my lady through her brave hunting dog. It's no use—I have to jump into the fray.

Just as I'm about to take off, ready for a daring leap maneuver to get the best bite position on that guy's sensitive spot, the unbelievable happens: Carolin moves even closer to the nasty type and starts petting him.

"But, dear—we agreed that getting an animal was a good idea. And yesterday I just spontaneously went to the animal shelter. Please, don't be mad! Hercules is so cute!"

I hope I'm mishearing. As far as I know, these things are said by couples to each other. For example, the gardener always tells Emilia everything is fine because they're a couple. So Carolin has two men? And one of them just happens to be this proletarian? At least he seems to have calmed down a bit; he's not shouting as loudly anymore. Dear?

Dear

Dear

"You and your spontaneous ideas! Buying a dog without asking me—what nonsense!"

"Well, I thought since you're often away and we have such a big garden, Hercules can come to the workshop during the day. Daniel had no problem with it."

"Of course he wouldn't. He's the weakling here. If you suggested standing in the store in strapless dresses tomorrow, he'd say yes and amen."

"Come on, Thomas—stop picking on Daniel. He may not be as assertive as you, but I couldn't imagine a better partner than him."

Aha, so the man's name is Thomas. And apparently, he knows Daniel. What an interesting dynamic. Do people sometimes live in threes? It's said that dogs used to live in packs in ancient times, with several females for each male. But here, one male had multiple ladies. Maybe it's different for humans—if there even are human packs—and maybe every woman needs several men? There's still so much for a young dog like me to learn. That much is clear.

"Well, of course you wouldn't find a better partner," sneers Thomas. "There aren't many luthiers around. But just because your work colleague lets you have your way in the workshop doesn't mean I do."

Thomas laughs mockingly. Carolin starts to cry, and it slowly dawns on me that Daniel and Carolin are apparently not a romantic couple. They're just work partners, or so it seems. So Carolin must have chosen Thomas out of some ununderstandable reason. How did this even happen? Is there an institution for matching up men and women in human society? Sort of like a breeder? That's what I always thought was excluded when it came to humans—I saw them as beings with free will. But if I'm seeing this here, then humans must have some other way of doing things. And—it goes without saying—it's not working well.

"Carolin, you're making a mistake. This just isn't going to work between you and Thomas. It never has, and it never will."

"How do you know that? Just because I'm a psychologist doesn't mean I can see into the future."

"No, not because you're a psychologist. It's because I'm your best friend Nina, who's been watching this whole mess for four years now."

We sit, or rather Carolin and Nina sit, I lie down, in a café. Carolin came with me after the argument with Thomas. A short time later, Nina joined us. And since then, it has been quite interesting, as Carolin and Nina are talking about exactly what has been worrying me today: What does Carolin actually want from Thomas? Nina obviously doesn't like Thomas either. But unlike me, she doesn't judge him by his unpleasant smell or loud voice, but has listed many other reasons, most of which I didn't quite understand. But anyway - in the end, Nina and I come to the same conclusion: He's not good enough. Carolin, however, bravely defends him, but Nina continues to argue.

"I mean, honestly, Carolin: You've already bought a dog out of desperation. What's next?"

Hey! Does that mean me? I growl cautionarily. Carolin leans down towards me.

"That's not what she means, Herkules."

Nina rolls her eyes. I can see this clearly from my spot next to Carolin's chair. "Yes, I mean exactly what I say! What you're missing is a man who loves you just as much as you love him. That stupid dachshund is definitely no replacement!"

Stupid dachshund? Does she even know who she's talking to? It's clear I'm not going to get away with just growling. I jump up and bark at Nina energetically. She raises her eyebrows in surprise.

"Wow, it seems like he actually understood me. Okay, I take back what I said about him not being a stupid dachshund. But the first part stands - you will never be happy with Thomas. Keep the dog but break up with that guy."

Exactly, let's do this! I lie down again.

"Herkules is no substitute for love. It has nothing to do with Thomas. I've always wanted a dog."

"Nonsense. That was typical sublimation."

"Yes, yes, the psychologist knows best."

I'm not sure what that means, but it seems dangerous. Carolin has already told Nina this several times, and it sounded like Nina had a serious illness. At least kennel cough. Poor thing, she looks so healthy - rosy skin tone, big clear eyes, I bet her nose is cold too. And her brown hair shines. But if it is an illness, I hope Carolin doesn't catch it and also become psychologically affected.

"So let's get to the point: You're not happy with Thomas and you never will be. Keep the dog but break up with him."

That's right, that's what we'll do! I wag my tail. Unfortunately, Carolin is not as enthusiastic about this advice as I am - she starts crying.

"You don't understand. Thomas and I are meant to be together. I know it for sure. Just the way we met was fate!"

Aha - fate! A mysterious word. Should that be the force that brings people together? And if so, how could fate have been so off with Carolin? I try to imagine fate in person. Perhaps it looks like the old Escher. Strict. Intimidating. A little too proud. But if fate is indeed like Escher, then it might also make mistakes. After all, even the Old Man got my character wrong when he brought me to the shelter.

Suddenly, I'm no longer interested in Nina and Carolin's conversation. My thoughts are back at home: With Mom, my sister Charlotte, and Emilia. How are they doing? For the first time since those exciting past three days, I feel a strange longing. Is my family missing me? Or have they stopped talking about me? Does Charlotte sleep well without me in her basket? Oh, Charlotte, will I ever see you again?

"Hey, Herkules, what's wrong with you? Aren't you feeling well?"

Apparently, I've started to whine. Carolin and Nina stop talking, and Carolin picks me up onto her lap. Surprised, I look straight into her eyes. They're all red - people do cry easily, but obviously it doesn't agree with them. I lick her hands quickly. Does that mean...? But Carolin looks quite worried.

"Hmm, what's wrong with him?"

Nina shrugs. "Maybe he's not happy with Thomas either? After all, Thomas would like to kick him out."

WUFF! What? Thomas wants to kick me out? So I'm going back to the shelter tomorrow? With Bozo and Boxer?

When we get home, I'm still quite worried. Will Carolin really bring me back? That would be terrible. Maybe I won't have a choice but to make up with Thomas. I decide to show my best dachshund side when I see him next time. Although it goes against the grain for an Escher to crawl on their belly, this was not exactly a successful act of civil disobedience and ended with a painful bite in my ear.

However, at the moment, Thomas doesn't seem to be home. Carolin doesn't call out for him either; she only briefly puts her bag down and then grabs the apartment key.

"We're going back to the workshop, Herkules." Then she opens the door. "Come on, sweetie!"

Nothing I'd rather do! I'm happy to see Daniel again and wag my tail behind Carolin as we go downstairs.

Upon arriving below, however, I realize that it still smells a lot like wood but not necessarily of Daniel. Strange, where is he? While Carolin goes to one of the tables and reorganizes things a bit, I run around looking for Daniel. I find out that these rooms in the workshop are arranged quite similarly to Carolin's apartment: two adjoining spaces on one side, a third behind them, then a long hallway, and at the back, another room. The wood smell is strongest here, and when I stick my nose inside, I see that there are entire piles of logs stacked up. Odd - what could Carolin possibly want with so much wood? Escher collects bottles in his basement, and Emilia's man collects these small colorful square pieces with notched edges, but wood? There seems to be nothing people won't collect.

"Herkules, where are you?" Carolin calls down the hall. I run out of the wood room. "Well, what are you doing in the wood pile? It smells nice, doesn't it?" I lie down in front of Carolin's feet, and she briefly pets me. "Or are you looking for Daniel?"

When I hear that name, I wag my tail. Let Carolin know right away who I consider a good master.

"Ah, so that's where the wind is blowing. Daniel is nice, isn't he? But it's the weekend, and we usually don't work then. I also have a few things to take care of that have been left here since your move in. Then we'll go for a walk, promised. You can look around in the garden while I'm working, okay?"

A good plan, because I haven't inspected the garden yet. In general — apart from the apartment and workshop — the house is still completely unknown to me, and I'm already looking forward to exploring it step by step. Carolin goes to one of the windows in the second room and opens it. Only now do I see that there are two steps leading up into the garden from there. I spring them quickly and sit down immediately in the grass. Wonderful — how ticklish it feels on my belly! The sun is shining right into my nose, and I have to sneeze. Carolin laughs.

"So, then enjoy yourself — I'll leave the door open, so you can come in if you get bored."

No worries, Carolin, that won't happen! I run off and sniff at the massive tree standing on the side of the house. Hm, interesting. Obviously no dog has been here for a long time, because there's nothing marked on this trunk. I mark it right away and lift my leg. This is something new for me — I've never done this before, especially not on such a wide trunk. But anyway, I can practice here in private. Until I get it just as well as the adult males that I've secretly been observing. They're so cool: they walk past a tree and lift their leg — as if it's no big deal.

I try again on the other side; after all, it's important to master both legs. Not easy at all! Luckily, no one is watching.

"Hey there, little guy?" comes a voice from directly above me at that moment. "That looks pretty wobbly. You're not staying long, heh heh!"

Who on earth is that? I look up and see a thick, black cat in the tree crown. Oh no, what a disgrace! A secret observer and now just a cat!

"By the way, this is the garden and the tree — so I'm asking you to stop peeing here. It's disgusting and stinks."

My...

With these words, the cat slowly climbs down from the thick trunk and stands in front of me. For a cat, she's quite large. Especially her fatness. I growl at her.

"What? Is that supposed to be a proper greeting? You dogs have no manners. You just come barging into my living room without knocking and don't even introduce yourself." But okay," the cat sighs, "let's start over: I'm Herr Beck."

Aha, a tomcat.

"I'm Carl-Leopold von Eschersbach. Pleased to meet you, Herr Beck. Finally, someone who doesn't judge me for not knowing what's appropriate."

The cat chuckles. "Carl-Leopold? That's strange, I just heard Carolin calling you Hercules. And 'von Eschersbach' sounds a bit pretentious."

What a nerve! I'd love to bite this fat creature right in the heels — but from dealing with palace cats, I know that can be quite painful for a small dog like me. These creatures are really fast and have sharp claws. Although I'm seething inwardly, I try to keep my cool.

"A woman as lovely as Carolin can call me by any name she pleases. But not you, an ordinary cat like you. By the way, this isn't your living room anymore, it's my new garden. So please, in future, don't climb on my also new tree. You're damaging it with your claws."

The cat's tail starts twitching, but unfortunately not out of fear, rather into hysterical laughter.

"Wonderful — you were just missing! I was getting a bit bored — but with a clown like you, this summer will definitely be entertaining."

Beck starts laughing on the floor. It's clear he's enjoying himself immensely. As for me, I could pull out my tail hairs. No one takes me seriously. Slowly, Herr Beck calms down, stands up and shakes himself off briefly.

"Now seriously, little guy — what do you think you are?"

I'm about to answer when Beck's paw zips by my nose, missing by millimeters. "Stop asking that question! Just don't start with your noble talk again."

I growl. Beck shouldn't think he can just insult me without consequences — claws or no claws. Unfortunately, he completely ignores this and continues unabated: "I've been living here for a while as the only animal in this house, if you exclude that stupid parakeet on the second floor. And just because you were brought here by the very attractive Carolin, you don't get to clear my territory. This was garden, it is garden, and will always be garden! So be happy when you can occasionally bask in the sun here and stay away from the tree."

With these words, he turns around and apparently wants to leave me standing there. That's finally enough for me. I take a leap forward and grab Beck's tail. Actually, with the intention of making it just as narrow an escape as his clawed swipe earlier. Unfortunately, at that very moment, Beck lowers his tail tip towards my teeth — and before I know it, I bite right into it. Ouch. That definitely hurt a little bit. Maybe quite a lot. But not on purpose!

Beck roars angrily and wants to get revenge, but I take off. On the second day of being pummelled by a cat, that's certainly not how I envisioned my successful introduction. Before he catches me, I leap through an open window in the workshop.

I almost land on Carolin's feet, who looks at me in surprise.

"What are you doing there, Hercules? Doing acrobatics?" She looks out the window and sees Beck just managing to stop short. "Did you have a fight with the cat?"

I try to look as innocent as possible and wag my tail.

"So really, Hercules! Herr Beck is a nice older gentleman. Plus, his wife always waters my flowers when I'm not around. So you need to behave yourself."

How embarrassing! She knows the cat better than me. I pretend to be looking at something very interesting on the ground. However, I can't help but think that Carolin's taste in men — both human and feline — is far from exquisite. After Thomas, it's Herr Beck — it's almost a wonder she didn't take Bozo from the shelter instead of me.

The thought of Thomas reminds me that I still need to make a good impression on him today. He shouldn't even think about bringing me back. Besides, I have enough enemies close by, and Herr Beck is no longer someone I need to be friends with. I resolve to seize the first opportunity to bond with Thomas over Bozo's tail.

That's exactly what I'll do, Opili. Boldly and fearlessly. Ein von Eschersbach doesn't hesitate — he acts when the opportunity arises, boldly and fearlessly.

FOUR

Indeed, the opportunity for brotherhood with Thomas comes earlier than thought. A night later, I lie in my basket and can't sleep. Too much goes through my head. Thomas. The small incident with Mr Beck. The conversation between Carolin and Nina. Even Fritz, the Münsterlander from the shelter, I have to think of. Restlessly, I roll over and over.

Suddenly, I hear a sound. It's a murmuring... or rather a... whimpering? I scramble up, climb out of my basket, and trot out of the living room towards the hallway. There I can hear the sound even better. It is indeed a whimpering, and it comes from the bedroom! Horror - isn't Carolin not feeling well? The door is only propped open, so I can quietly slip in. Unfortunately, it's completely dark, I can't see anything.

And again the sound. The whimpering has now turned into a groan. To my great relief, though, it's clearly Thomas who isn't doing well. My first thought: Carolin is probably fine. My second thought: Here she is, my chance! Now, I have to act boldly and fearlessly. Because quite obviously, Thomas is lying in bed and winding himself up in pain. Carolin appears to be sleeping - at least, it seems she doesn't hear him, because if she did, she would help him. So I'll make sure she wakes up and Thomas doesn't have to suffer any longer. Then he'll realize what a great dog I am and how good it is that Carolin got me.

With a bold and fearless leap, I jump into bed right next to the groaning Thomas. It's really amazing that Carolin doesn't hear him, because she lies more or less under him. As I mentioned before: humans have really bad hearing. But don't worry, Thomas, you now have a new loyal friend. In his pain, he winds himself up, turning his face down. I lick him quickly on the neck, so he knows help is near. He shivers. Then I start barking as loudly as possible. After all, Carolin should finally wake up.

The next thing I remember is flying across the whole room and landing very roughly beside the door. Then suddenly, it gets bright. Thomas - miraculously healed - stands over me and glares at me angrily.

"You damned mutt! What gives you the right? I'll put you down!"

He swings at me - does he want to hit me? I try to duck away. But where? In panic, I start yapping. Help - what's going on here?

At that moment, Carolin suddenly stands behind Thomas. From all the noise, she finally woke up. She grabs Thomas by the shoulder and pulls him back.

"Submit yourself, Hercules! He didn't intentionally disturb us. "

Thomas turns around. "Please? The dog jumps into our bed when I'm just getting started, and you defend it? To that stupid creature, I'll show soon enough what I think of his little act."

"Thomas!" Carolin says sharply. "You leave Hercules alone right now! Now!"

She bends down to me and picks me up on her arm. By now, I'm shaking like a leaf. This is just too much for my delicate nervous system. And anyway, I don't understand anything anymore: Why should Thomas be happy that at least I recognized his critical state? Instead, he had serious plans to beat me up. And my Dackelpo hurts from the kick he gave me in bed. I start whining. Never have I been treated so unfairly. Against this psychopath, old von Eschersbach is nothing but a person of mildness!

"You poor thing, you're shaking so hard!" Carolin presses me against herself and presses her face into my neck. "Don't worry, I'm with you. I'll take care of you."

Thomas snorts in disdain. "So really, Carolin. Do you now have an erotic relationship with a dog? You seem not to mind the interruption at all. Maybe it was even right for you. You don't even have to say you have a headache anymore."

Hold on: Thomas groans, and Carolin has a headache? What interruption? I do my best, but I can't make any sense of it. One thing is clear though: My attempt to get along with Thomas has completely failed. And I don't even know why. Can I take my new chew toy to the shelter? Besides, it doesn't matter, Bozo and Boxer will probably take it away from me anyway.

I spend the rest of the night in my basket. Although I'm exhausted, after all this chaos, of course, I can't sleep either. From time to time, I raise an ear and listen into the darkness. Complete silence. But even if I heard another noise, there's no way I'd go back into a room where Thomas is present again.

The sun shines through the workshop windows, creating inviting patches on the old wooden floor, calling me... This morning, the last night still stings in my bones. I hesitate between choosing a spot in Carolin's room or lying down next to Daniel's worktable. "Come on, Carl-Leopold, lay down and rest a bit!"

Finally, I lie down next to Daniel. I feel pretty small and am afraid that Carolin might also blame me for everything. Even though she didn't say a word, it still feels a little uncomfortable. Because something must have gone completely wrong. Even after careful consideration, I still don't know what exactly. But the fact that Carolin had to defend me from Thomas again is really embarrassing for me.

"So, how was your weekend? How does life with your new dog go?" Daniel asks Carolin.

I tuck my ears down and lower my head between my front paws. The story that will inevitably follow, I don't want to hear.

"You - great! Thomas is also very excited about the little guy. Well, you know how much he loves animals."

Huh? Was there something in his food this morning? Obviously, I'm hallucinating.

"Really? No, I didn't know that he likes animals so much. But all the better, then the two of them will get along great. They can go hiking together or do survival training or whatnot - the usual stuff a real tough guy does with his dog."

Do I imagine it, or do I hear a hint of irony in Daniel's words? This is actually not easy for me as a dog - humans often use the same words to mean completely different things. I remember well when von Eschersbach first said that after I jumped onto the sofa in the living room with wet paws, and then proceeded to swing his walking stick at my hind legs. I couldn't calm down for two hours until Mom explained that people often say the opposite of what they mean to make it clear that they don't actually mean it. Crazy, right? In a human's head, there must be several impractical and unnecessary twists. Probably because they carry their humans high above the ground with their upright posture. This is obviously not good for their brains."Fine dog, good idea!"

In this context, it's also interesting to question how Carolin came to tell such a story? Why won't she admit that our start into a shared future was a total failure? Could animal love perhaps be something that increases the value of a man? Just like wildness, drive and shooting accuracy distinguish an outstanding hunting dog? I know a lot about outstanding hunting dogs: Mom was three times in the final of the National Championship, the shelves at Schloss Eschersbach are bending under her trophies, and she never received a mark worse than "Excellent 1", or short V1. So if animal love is basically listed, and Carolin wants to demonstrate that Thomas is a candidate for V1, then her story makes sense. But on the other hand, even a small dog like me can see at first glance that Thomas would get no better than a "Satisfactory" - even with animal love. If it were actually present.

But back to the topic of irony: honestly, I hope Daniel didn't mean it that way. Because the combination of words "with" and "waken" evoke in me entirely different associations than those of a great friendship between man and dog. Instead, I see Thomas in my mind's eye as he throws me over a rocky ledge into a very deep abyss or ties me to a tree in a lonely forest and simply goes away. Then again, animal shelter. Perhaps I can found a dog-sharing with Fritz, who is unfortunately still there, and we'll get our own little kennel? Maybe even next to the cat enclosure - looking back, I actually find it quite funny that I bit Beck's tail.

Thomas alone

Survival training

"So Daniel, you don't have to laugh about it. I really believe Hercules and Thomas will become good friends."

Ah - thank God! So indeed irony. No dangerous solo ventures with Thomas.

"I'm not laughing. I just doubt that your dear Thomas will soon be jogging through the park with a cute little guy like Hercules. It goes too much against his carefully cultivated image as a hard guy."

"What's wrong with Thomas?"

"Nothing at all. I sometimes wonder what he has against me."

Carolin bursts out laughing loudly. Sounds quite inauthentic.

"Please - Thomas doesn't have anything against you. On the contrary, he finds you very nice."

Carolins usually warm voice has a tinny undertone. Does Daniel hear that too? He sighs.

"Sure, sure."

"You're just very different. But that doesn't mean you can't be friends."

After that, Daniel says nothing more but takes a deep breath. Apparently, he doesn't want to talk about this topic with Carolin. A pity, I would have liked to know more of his opinion about Thomas. Perhaps I have an ally in him? That would be nice, because by now I could use another friend in this house.

At noon, Carolin briefly takes me into the apartment to give me my meal. She has indeed bought a book about dogs - I saw it on the sofa in the living room - and maybe as the first chapter she read the one about healthy nutrition. In any case, she is now off canned food and brought fresh heart for me during our walk this morning. As she cooks it, a tempting scent spreads throughout the apartment. Yummy! A very pleasant development.

"Okay, little guy, that needs to cool down a bit first, then you'll get it. Let's wait five more minutes; I need to make a quick phone call anyway, then I'll give it to you."

She puts my bowl with the heart pieces in the fridge and goes into the room next to the living room. I stand around uncertainly for a while, then trot into the hallway. While I'm still wondering what to do until dinner, I see that the bedroom door is open again. For two days, I've made a big detour around this room, but now my curiosity wins out. Perhaps I'll find something there that explains what really happened on that terrifying night recently? Although I don't know what it could be, at least I want to take another look in the light of day. From the other corner of the apartment, I hear Carolin talking with this black plastic thing. After everything I now know about people in general and women in particular, this is a sure sign that she's completely distracted right now. It was the same with Emilia. You could steal the most amazing things from the kitchen while she was on the phone.

Carefully, I push my nose through the door crack. Yes, the air is clean. In a flash, I'm inside. At first glance, it looks entirely unremarkable. But as they say, it's the second look that makes things interesting. Better said, the moment when a dog precisely sniffs. And that's why I decide to conduct a thorough sniffing inspection of everything here.

I start with the bed: Everything is the same as usual. To the right, it smells like Carolin; to the left, more like Thomas. Well, what did I expect? That's just how they sleep. I'm almost about to jump back down when I notice a different scent. Not directly on the sheets, but rather underneath, on the mattress. I pull the sheets apart and sniff again. Strange. While Carolin's side has that fantastic Carolin smell and Thomas' side smells unsympathetically from his bed, there's a third scent hanging over this bed. It's... hmm... I'm not sure... somehow... no, or maybe yes... really hard to say! So I sniff again and again all over the bed.

At that moment, the bedroom door swings wide open. "Herkules, no! What are you doing in our bed again?"

Carolin stands before me, wagging her index finger in disapproval. Ashamed, I look down. How am I supposed to explain what exactly I'm looking for? I don't even know myself. I just know that I've discovered something very strange.

"Dogs don't belong in the bed, Herkules. You have a very comfortable crate, and please stay there when you want to sleep. On the sofa, you can sit with me, but not in the bed. Thomas was really angry at you earlier, and I promised him I'd teach you better. I want you two to become friends. And that's not how it works!"

Now Carolin looks really sad. Ugh. I tuck my ears down and jump off the bed. It was a bad idea with the bedroom. And smarter I'm not either.

"Don't look so sad. Every now and then, even such a sweet dog like you has to learn something new. And now come - your meal is probably ready."

Of course, I don't let that slip twice, and I rush straight into the kitchen. Carolin takes the bowl out of the fridge, stirs it once, and sets it in front of my nose. Mmm, delicious. A proper portion of heart, and the biggest sorrow quickly forgotten.

Back in the workshop, I doze off for an hour. Daniel has fashioned me a second bed out of an old box and a pillow – very resourceful of him. I wake up because I have the feeling that someone is tormenting Mr Beck. In any case, horrible sounds are reaching my ears. Very high-pitched and shrill, a wretched howl. I jump out of the box and run towards the noise. There, in one of the front rooms, stands Carolin holding something on her arm. Not Mr Beck, though, but one of those small wooden boxes that seem to be scattered all over the workshop. They look strange. They come in various sizes; they're not angular, but round, as if two circles had been glued together. Plus, they have a long neck. And Carolin is swatting at the neck with her stick. To be more precise, she's tracing patterns on it. It seems to hurt the box, because afterwards it emits that terrible noise.

Brr, my blood freezes! I can't help but start wailing. First timidly, then loudly. Carolin lowers the box, and Daniel enters the room. He sees me still heaving a final, powerful sob, then breaks into thunderous laughter.

"Oh my goodness, tell me, does Hercules not like music? Well, you're in the right place here!"

Music? That's supposed to be music? That's just not on! I know music from Schloss Eschersbach. In the drawing room, not only was my favourite sofa, but also a so-called piano. Sometimes Eschersbach would play there, and it wasn't exactly my personal choice, but it wasn't nearly as bad as what I just heard. And when I went shopping with Emilia, the car played music, of course much too loud, but otherwise quite nice – with a clear rhythm and very fast. But this... this was simply terrible. And so shrill. It wasn't even music in the slightest. I shake my head vigorously.

Carolin and Daniel exchange bewildered glances.

"Maybe he finds the notes too high? Get the cello, maybe that'll suit him better than the violin?"

Daniel sets off and returns with one of the larger boxes. Ah, so this is what they call a "cello", I suppose. Hopefully it sounds a bit like music. Daniel sits down on a chair and wedges the cello between his legs. He also takes the stick in hand. Then he moves it slowly back and forth. Indeed, notes are coming out. And they sound much better. I grumble contentedly and lie down in front of Daniel, resting my head on my forelegs.

"Well, Hercules isn't the type for violin," Carolin observes. "But generally, it seems he has no objection to music." "That's good to know. But I have to get back to work now. So, Hercules, if you don't mind your ears, maybe you'd better go into the garden."

Fine by me; I can practice peeing a bit more.

FIVE

The garden is peaceful and quiet. I scan around to make sure there are no unwelcome spectators this time, then approach the big tree and lift my leg. Wait a minute? It's actually getting better! That stupid Beck can go to hell. Besides, this isn't even his garden; it's mine. After all, he's just visiting Carolin's workshop. There's nothing wrong with that; a guest from Eschersbach is always welcome. But if this stupid cat thinks I'm going to dance to his tune now because he's been here longer, then he's got another thing coming! I won't give in. A dog from Eschersbach never gives in!

I try switching legs a few times, left and right, but eventually get bored. Time to check out the other corners of the garden. Behind the tree starts a large lawn bathed in inviting sunlight. Otherwise, it's rather dull for my taste; no sign of rabbits or moles. The occasional squirrel seems to run across the meadow from time to time; I can smell them, at least. But squirrels aren't much of a catch; they jump onto trees too quickly on the run, even for such a formidable hunter as Opili.

The lawn is framed by a flower bed on both sides. It smells sweet and summery, and a few bees buzz heavily from bloom to bloom. I sniff around the edge but can't find anything interesting. Just as I'm about to turn around to trot towards the front garden, I hear someone calling.

"Hercules! Hey, come over here!" Could that be Mr Beck? Demanding revenge? I decide not to respond.

"Come on, Hercules, come already."

I slowly turn around but still don't move from my spot.

"So, if it's so important: Carl-Leopold, could you please come?"

Oh, a whole new set of sounds. Beck obviously wants something important from me. But where in the world is he? I can't see him anywhere; not on the lawn, not on the tree, nowhere.

"Beck, where are you? I can't see you." "I'm up here."

"On the tree?" "No, on the table."

On the table? A table? I have no idea where to look; I scan around in confusion.

"Up there on the lawn. Run behind the flower bed, and you'll see it!"

I trot over to the left flower bed and indeed spot a large garden table – or rather, the legs of one such table. Not easy for a Dackel to spot from behind these tall bushes, but that must be it.

"Exactly, now get on the chair; then you'll see me."

What kind of game is this supposed to be? I look around for a chair and find one right next to the table. Whoa, that's high! Hopefully I can actually make it up there.

"Listen, Beck, I don't know if I can do that. It's pretty high for me. Why don't you just tell me what you want, or even better, come down here yourself?"

"That's not possible. You'll see why soon enough. So please, go ahead and jump!"

I sigh and take three steps back to get some momentum, then leap onto the chair. Success! Just barely, but still. A little proud of this feat, I look around with my head held high – and spot Mr Beck sitting right in the middle of the garden table. To be more precise: in a bird cage placed on top of the garden table.

"Well, now you see why I can't come."

Beck looks unhappy. I, on the other hand, have to fight hard not to burst out laughing.

"What are you doing? That looks ridiculous! A fat cat like you in such a tiny cage!"

"Yes, thank you too for your sympathy. What am I going to do here? I had the historical chance to grab this annoying, old-fashioned budgerigar. Unfortunately, I didn't think about the fact that the cage door opens inward and now I can't get it open because I'm blocking it with my size."

"I mean: you're fat!"

Beck ignores this objection and looks at me intensely, as a cat might through bars.

"You have to help me, Carl-Leopold. If old Meyer sees that I've caught her bird, there will be serious trouble."

"She can think so even if you're not in the cage."

"Yes, maybe she can think it, but she can't prove it. My first master was a lawyer, and let me tell you - between belief and knowledge, people make a huge difference."

"Anyway, why should I help you? I'm happy to see you go to the animal shelter or somewhere else."

"Hey buddy? Is that solidarity among pets?"

"Solidarity among pets? I don't know, I'd like to ask the budgerigar first."

I turn to leave, but Beck makes one last attempt: "Okay then, call it however you want. But if you've ever thought about burying the hatchet between us, now is an extremely favorable time. Think about whether it wouldn't be useful to have a friend in this house - someone who knows humans very well!"

Fine, he's got me. I sigh. "So what should I do?"

"Come next to the cage. You can also open it from above, but first you need to chew through the knots in the string, and that's something my teeth can't do alone."

I look at what he means. Indeed, the cage has a top flap secured with a kind of ribbon. The knots of this ribbon are outside the cage and look like a solvable task.

"I think I can manage that. But we'll have to flip the cage over, otherwise I won't be able to reach the knots."

"No problem. Flip the cage off the table - better a few scratches than sitting here anymore." "Alright then, watch out!"

With a strong push, I slide the cage off the edge of the table. It falls with a loud thud and lands on its side.

"Ouch!" Beck exclaims and shakes himself vigorously. "Well, that's how you should get to it."

I jump down from the table onto a chair. Then I stand next to the cage and examine the situation again. Yes, this could work. The knots are right at the level of my snout. And for my skills as a thing-chewer, I am almost legendary. Legend, I would say, to Emilia's great misfortune, has cost her one or two pairs of shoes. But somehow this skill does need training.

In less than three minutes, the ribbon falls to the ground, and the flap opens - fortunately outward. The opening is quite small, but Beck forces himself through with all his might. Amazing how flexible cats are. In fact, they would make great construction workers - but probably they're too afraid to face a badger face-to-face. So this budgerigar was an easier catch.

Sniffing, Beck finally sits next to me. "Thanks, my friend."

"No problem. But tell me, did you really kill that poor bird?"

"Well, so to speak, the budgerigar is still alive. I didn't eat it."

"It's still alive? You mean you were in its cage and it's still alive?" But where is it?"

"It's not in the cage, as I was chasing it. It was just a plastic bird."

I look at Beck with wide eyes.

"Yes, I know what you're thinking. But here's what happened: The stupid bird wasn't even in the cage. This morning I went for a walk in the garden. When I saw the cage on the table, I thought this was my chance. So up I go and straight into the cage - grab the buddy, bite down - and suddenly I have this stupid plastic thing in my mouth."

"Please? You mean you actually killed the plastic bird?" I burst out laughing. "That can't be true! How could you mistake a real bird for that piece of plastic? That's just being blind, ha ha!" I roll around on the lawn, enjoying myself.

"Well, the plastic thing does look like a bird from far away," Beck retorts angrily.

"Yes, maybe it looks similar. But it's completely different!"

Beck remains silent. Clearly, my new friend is deeply hurt by my laughter. Okay, maybe I shouldn't have gone overboard.

"Hey, I'm sorry. I didn't mean to make fun of you. I just couldn't imagine how that happened."

Dejected, Beck looks at me. "I can tell you exactly how it happened: I'm a damn old cat now, with not the best eyes and even worse sense of smell. That's how it happened. It's no wonder a youngster like you can't fathom it."

Oh dear, someone is pretty down in the dumps here. But that's a silly story: to break into a cage without prey and then not be able to get out alone. I try to cheer him up.

"Come on, there are lots of things you're good at that I have no clue about."

"Like what?"

Gloomy, Beck stares ahead. I ponder for a moment. But only briefly, because something immediately comes to mind that I genuinely envy him.

"Well, you said it yourself. You know humans very well. But I propose this: now that we're friends, I'll help you understand them."

Something's just not right, little one. Does your ear hurt? She strokes my head. Then she runs her hand over my right ear, and I shiver. "Indeed, you have a small lump." She now precisely touches the painful spot, and I let out a loud yelp.

"Daniel, can you come here? I need your expert advice. Hercules has a knot on his ear, and it seems to hurt him too."

Daniel pokes his head out of his room. "I'll be right there, I just have to finish something quickly."

Hopefully Daniel can help me because the more I think about it, the more my ear hurts. By now, the itching has almost completely given way to a continuous throbbing. I rest my chin on my forepaws and mumble a little in front of myself. It can't hurt to let people know how bad I feel.

"Okay, Hercules, let me take a look."

Daniel bends over me and gently pushes my right ear forward. I mumble a bit louder. When Daniel feels the bump, he carefully parts my fur.

"Ah, I already suspected as much," Carolin looks at him with great concern. "Something serious?"

Brr, now I'm worried too. Will my ear fall off? Besides, good hearing is important for every hunting dog, and if it did fall off, I would surely be the ugliest dachshund in the world.

Daniel shakes his head. "No, no, don't worry. It's just a tick."

Phew, I'm relieved. I've heard about ticks before, they can survive. Although I've never had one myself, Emilia has always carefully searched us after our play sessions in the park.

"However," Daniel continues and gives his voice a tone that makes the idea of an ear falling off not seem so far-fetched, "it seems to be infected. The bite site is quite warm and slightly oozing. Of course, we can now pull out the tick with a tweezers, but I'd better take Hercules to the vet."

No, please not the vet! It makes me shiver, and I feel my fur stand on end.

"Man, Hercules, you look so angry," Daniel finds amusing.

I wouldn't know what's so funny about it.

"Apparently our new roommate understands us perfectly well, and he definitely doesn't want to go to the vet. Look, he's stiffening up."

He hands me over to Carolin, who takes me onto her lap and gently strokes my head. "Oh Hercules, you don't have to be afraid. A trip to the vet isn't so bad."

So, with all due respect, I know that better. Of those present, I'm the only one who has had this experience as a patient. Even twice. The first time, I was still fooled by the friendly chatter until this figure named vet suddenly pulled a fold of skin up and stabbed me with a needle. It's amazing what kind of nonsense people spout with such conviction. How I would love to talk to them in these moments!

While I'm still debating that all my important and relevant thoughts could only result in a loud bark, the doorbell rings. Carolin sets me down and opens the door.

"Hello everyone!"

"Hello Nina! Man, I almost forgot about you. I'm afraid our plan for today at noon will change a bit."

Nina looks disappointed. "Oh, why? What happened?"

"Hercules caught a tick, and it's infected. I need to take him to the vet right away. Later on, I won't be able to fit it in as I have too many client appointments."

"Too bad! But maybe the procedure won't take long, and we can go out for something to eat afterward? Then I'll just come along. Where should we go?"

"Well, I didn't expect to need a vet so soon. Maybe you could give me a tip, Daniel?"

Daniel thinks for a moment, he furrows his brow, which is usually a reliable sign. Anyway, I need to get into the habit of looking people in the face more. You learn a lot about their current mood that way. Although I've already learned quite a bit about "understanding humans," Beck could still give me some additional lessons.

"So, our old vet was great, but I'm not sure if he's still practicing. He was actually the best and well-known. Wagner was his name, his practice was just around the corner on Hellmann Street. Call the directory, maybe."

Less than half an hour later, we're already sitting in Dr. Wagner's clinic. The Escher'seah vet always came to the castle, so my vet visit today is a bit of a novelty for me. And I have to admit: If it weren't for my aching ears, this would be quite interesting. From my spot on Carolin's lap, I can see that there are two rabbits in the transport box under her chair. Crazy - I've never been so close to them before. I feel a warm tingling in my nose. I'd love to hop down and take a closer look at them. Maybe I could chase them around the waiting room? Just for fun, of course. The motto among pets is solidarity. A little nibble on their hind legs wouldn't kill them. I notice that my ear hurts less when I think about it.

Slowly, I slide off Carolin's lap and take a quick look to see if she's watching me. Nope, she's engaged in an animated conversation with Nina about their shared favorite topic - Thomas. Delicately, I let myself glide down and land right in front of the rabbit cage. Carolin just briefly strokes my head then turns back to Nina. I look around - the air is clean because the responsible rabbit person stands at the counter talking to a young woman. So, friends, shall we play a little?

I press my nose against the cage. What a wonderful smell! I could bark loudly with joy! But I leave it out, because then I'd probably fly up, and the fun would be over before it started. Instead, I try to push open the latch that secures the small gate on one side of the cage. The rabbits stare at me, not looking thrilled. But that doesn't bother me, so I grab the latch with my teeth and turn it. With a quiet "click," the gate swings open. Fantastic! It works! Immediately, I stick my head into the cage and try to catch the larger of the two rabbits. Fearfully, it squeals, clearly not enjoying the fun. Before I can really get a hold on it though, something incredible happens: Its colleague shoots forward with lightning speed and bites me right in the nose. I yelp loudly and pull my head out of the cage. Such a rudeness!

The rabbits take advantage of their chance to escape and jump straight out of the box. I bark furiously and want to follow them, but don't realize that I'm on a leash. Nina gets caught in the harness as one of the two rabbits slips through her legs. She stumbles, sways - and falls directly into the arms of a man in a white coat who just opened the door next to our row of chairs and innocently calls out, "Mrs. Neumann with Hercules?" As I see him, a strange feeling washes over me. But I don't have time to think about it any further because the rabbit's owner has run up to us from the counter, calling, "Bobo, Snow White! My poor dears!" and tries to give me a targeted kick. I dodge and duck under the next chair. Carolin is frantically tugging at my leash, and the grandmother sitting diagonally across from us has an asthma attack from excitement. The man in the coat - clearly Dr. Wagner - still holds Nina in his arms, and I wait for him to let her go now and also start hunting me.

But nothing like that happens; instead, Dr. Wagner starts laughing loudly. A very dark, cheerful laugh. A laugh that sounds familiar to me. Suddenly, it's as if I'm back at Schloss Eschersbach, sitting with Charlotte on the cold tiles in the antechamber of the stable. I can almost hear the voice of the old man from Eschersbach talking to the vet. Dr. Wagner is not just any vet; he's my vet! Excitedly, I start barking - out of fear or joy, I don't know.

"Well, little guy, you've caused quite a mess here."

Dr. Wagner kneels down in front of the chair where I'm still hiding and strokes my head. Now Carolin leans over, unties my completely knotted leash, and pulls me out from under the seat.

"Oh my God, Dr. Wagner, this is so embarrassing. Please forgive me, I hope nothing happened to the rabbits."

Pah! Traitor! What does it matter what happens to the rabbits? She should be more worried about me - first, I get struck by insidious ear infections, and now they're bringing up my past like a brutal flashback. But at least Dr. Wagner knows his priorities. He shakes his head, then takes me in his arms.

"Don't worry, you shouldn't feel embarrassed. This little guy can't be held responsible for anything. A mix of Dachshund, right? You must know that Dachshunds have been bred for centuries to hunt these rabbits." And against three hundred years of breeding, even the best-behaved dog can't compete. He strokes me, and I lick his hands. "By the way, Hercules sounds familiar to me. Have you ever brought him here before? We don't have a patient file for him yet."

Carolin shakes her head. "No, I'm a new dog owner. A friend recommended your practice to me."

"Well, then I must be mistaken. I took over the practice from my father just last year, and I admit that I sometimes mix up patients." He turns to Nina. "I hope I didn't get too close with my little rescue maneuver? Thank you very much."

Nina lets out a very strange giggle and says in a voice completely different from her usual one: "But no, quite the opposite. You came just in time, thank you."

"Alright then, let's take a good look at Hercules. Please come with me?"

He opens the door he just came through and gestures invitingly. Carolin goes first, and Nina doesn't seem to want to miss out on my examination either. She doesn't simply sit back down but immediately follows Carolin.

Dr. Wagner nods briefly to the young woman at the counter. "Sinje, please help Mr. Riedler catch Bobo and Snow White; they shouldn't become the next victim of a hunting dog."

Behind the door is a bright room with a table in the middle. Dr. Wagner sets me down on it and examines me.

"So, Hercules, where does the problem lie?"

"He has a tick behind his right ear, and I'm afraid the bite has become infected," Carolin explains.

Dr. Wagner runs his hand along my ear until he feels the lump. As he touches it, I flinch. In all the commotion, it actually didn't hurt that much before, but now the throbbing is almost unbearable.

"You're right, it has become infected. I'll remove the tick and disinfect the wound. Then I'll prescribe antibiotics for Hercules. It's possible he was exposed to some germs from the animal." He also gives me a plastic collar so he can't scratch the spot anymore.

Great! What a wonderful prospect for the next few days. I bet Beck will be laughing his head off when he sees me with this thing on.

"Now, I suggest you hold Hercules' front paws steady while I put a muzzle on him. Just in case he bites any of us when I remove the tick."

"Can I do something to help?" Nina asks.

Is she trying to make a good impression with Dr. Wagner? She's usually not so sweet.

"Thank you, Mrs... uh..."

"Bogner. Nina Bogner."

With the muzzle around my snout, I can't see as well, but I still notice that Nina is beaming at Dr. Wagner. All her teeth are visible. For a human, it's not an unpleasant smile. Her voice sounds like a flute, though, and unpleasantly so.

"Thank you, Mrs. Bogner, that's very kind of you," Wagner says. "But we'll manage here."

"I'm just worried about our little friend here. I love dogs, you know."

Ugh, this babbling is driving me crazy. Hopefully Dr. Wagner will be done soon, and we can go home. From the corner of my eye, I see him holding a kind of plier in his hand. He bends over me - AUCH! I knew it; visiting the vet always ends in pain. And today is no exception. I would love to snap at Wagner, but the stupid muzzle prevents me from doing so.

"Just a moment, Hercules!" Wagner reassures. "We're almost done."

He opens a small bottle and pours out a liquid with an intense, stinging smell. Ew, that's disgusting! Wagner dabs some of the stuff onto a cotton ball. Then he applies it to the wound. AGAIN AUCH! It burns like hell, and this time I manage to break free from Carolin and jump up. Angrily, I bark at both of them.

"Hercules!" Carolin scolds, "Dr. Wagner is just trying to help. Now be good and lie down again!"

"Not needed, Mrs. Neumann," Dr. Wagner says. "I've removed the tick, disinfected the wound. Your helper will give you the collar and antibiotics. Hercules will get the medicine in his food for the next seven days. That should take care of everything, and your Hercules will be back on his feet soon."

Ten minutes later, I'm sitting on Carolin's lap, and we're both back in Nina's car. Nina is obviously in a good mood; she's whistling happily.

"So, happy as a clam?" Carolin asks her.

"Oh yes, that was quite interesting today. A different kind of lunch break. I've never been to a vet before."

"Well, why not? I always had the impression you weren't exactly a big animal lover. Your newly discovered love for dogs surprises me honestly."

"Why? Hercules is such a cute little guy. So, if we ever need to go back, I'd be happy to come along."

"Hmm. Are you sure it's not about another cute little guy?"

"What? I don't know what you're talking about."

Here, Nina and I are on the same page. I still can't figure out what Carolin is talking about. Which other cute little guy? I couldn't find anyone but myself who deserves that description.

"Come on, now do stop acting like that. Didn't you see him flirting with Dr. Doctor?"

Nina says nothing, she just keeps humming.

"Okay, admit it, he caught your eye. I get it. He does look quite handsome - and how he grabbed you right away: old school."

Carolin giggles, Nina remains silent. Human communication is mysterious.

Back home, I'd rather curl up in my basket immediately. But that's not an option because before Carolin can even unlock the front door, Beck happens by, seemingly by chance, and whispers to me, "We need to talk, right now!" Is there ever any peace around here? On the other hand, Beck is making such a big deal out of it that my curiosity wins.

"Alright, in the garden? I give up." I sigh resignedly.

Beck nods and disappears. I look at Carolin, start to chirp, and pace back and forth.

"What's wrong, Hercules? Do you have to go?"

I bark briefly and run towards the garden. "Hey, not so fast! I actually need to get to the workshop right now."

I pause and chirp again.

"Alright, if it's that urgent..."

In the garden, I see Beck sitting under a tree. I squat down next to him.

"So, what's up?" I want to know.

Beck takes a dramatic breath. "I've made a sensational discovery."

SEVEN

"Where? I don't see anything!"

"Over there! (He gestures vaguely across the street.)

I strain my eyes but can't make out any sensation that Beck seems to have noticed. Fine, maybe it's because I'm wearing this oversized plastic collar around my neck which restricts my vision, but there's nothing we can do about that now. Beck sighs impatiently.

"We're here to solve your big problem."

"My biggest problem? Say what?" The cat is getting on my nerves.

"Ah, forget it - no big problem, it's your biggest problem."

"My biggest problem? You mean that I'm not a purebred and Escherbach has been hallucinating the whole time?"

In my mind's eye, I see a pedigree certificate as long as a roll of kitchen paper, issued in my name.

Beck grunts. "Nonsense. Not this pedigree nonsense. It doesn't matter to anyone." Your biggest problem is Thomas."

"Okay... So you're saying that Thomas wants to get rid of me?"

"Right. And what follows from that?"

"That I need to behave better?

"Nope. That's not it at all. You need to get rid of him. Before he can send you back to the shelter."

"I need to get rid of Thomas? Unbelievable! How am I supposed to do that? Should I attack him and bury him secretly? I think you're overestimating me a bit here, I'm not exactly a fighting dog."

Beck shakes his head. "My God, you're hard to understand. Not like that at all! You have the historic chance right now to get rid of Thomas once and for all. But not if you just stand there like a statue. So come on, follow me!"

I sigh. When will this day finally become a bit calmer?

On the other side of the street, I still can't figure out what Beck is so excited about.

"Excuse me, obviously I'm not as sharp as you today. What's there to see?"

"Right in front of you."

"Huh?"

"In front of the evidence."

"Evidence? I don't see anything."

"You only see two cars and a power box. I, on the other hand, see a BMW, black metallic. That's the first piece of a brilliant, seamless proof that ends with Thomas at your door and you on his couch." So let me explain: we're here for a site visit."

Did I mention Beck used to be a lawyer? One very unpleasant consequence from that time is the arbitrary use of legal jargon. Tragically, how humans can rub off on their animals. I wish it were the other way around. The world would be a kinder place.

"Come on, Dackel! Climb up on the power box!"

With one bound, Beck is at the top.

"Now what? How am I supposed to do that? I can barely get up there under normal circumstances - and with this collar, it's completely out of the question." So either you tell me right now what all this is about or I'm going home."

Beck looks offended. "I expected a little more enthusiasm from you. After all, I'm doing this only for you. It could be entirely irrelevant to me what Thomas does with his time. But since you're a friend...

"Hold on, what do you mean? What about Thomas?"

Beck jumps down and lands right next to me. Cats can do that, you know.

"Alright, let me write this down: This morning I went for my usual little walk. I always like to be on the other side of the park; there's better air here, more mice, quieter... You'll see."

"Beck," I interrupt impatiently, "what about Thomas?"

"As I was walking by, the aforementioned BMW pulls up right next to me. And who gets out? (He gives his voice a dramatic weight) Thomas! He opens the car door and - he's immediately embraced by a young woman! Right there in the hallway of that house we're standing in front of!"

"What nonsense, you stupid little dachshund!" Beck interrupts me rudely. "No, not at all! They kissed! I mean, Thomas kissed another woman! "

"Why not? I've seen it happen before. Carolin and Nina kiss each other on the face from time to time, that's just a ritual among people."

"With their tongues?"

"What do you mean?"

"Do they kiss with their tongues?"

I'm bewildered. Kissing with tongues?

"So, they lick each other?"

"Excuse me?"

"Well, do they lick each other's faces?"

I'm at a loss. I wag my tail uncertainly. "So, you mean... licking? That's not something I've seen people do."

"It is indeed," Beck exclaims triumphantly. "They do it! But not always. Only when they want to mate. And that's what I saw, see for myself: Thomas was licking this woman with his tongue, and she was licking him back. First, they kissed normally, then they stuck their tongues in each other's mouths. An undeniable sign! There's a big fraud going on here, and I've uncovered it!"

This is clearly too complicated for a little dachshund. My ears are twitching, both of them, not just the one near the collar. Apparently, my confusion is evident, because Mr. Beck moves closer to me and whispers conspiratorially. 

"Carl-Leopold von Eschersbach, I'm delivering Thomas right into your hands. He's cheating on Carolin with another woman. As you know, men and women like to form pairs. And when they do that, they stay together. Anything else is fraud. So, having sex or kissing someone who doesn't belong to you is fraud. And licking each other the way Thomas and this other woman did... it's often a sign of fraud."

My tail stops wagging. My head tilts to one side. "What do you think will work?"

"One hundred percent. A sure thing. We just need another piece of evidence."

I stop moving altogether.

"Darn."

"What's wrong?"

"The evidence. How are we supposed to prove that? We can't take a picture and show it to Carolin. And Thomas certainly won't kiss her in front of her eyes."

Beck nods thoughtfully. "You're right, that is a problem. But I'll think of something. Until then, I suggest we intensify the observation. At the appropriate time, we'll enter the proof-gathering phase."

I groan inwardly. Jurists and their jargon...

"But you don't even know where Thomas is with this woman. And even if you did, how would we get in? "

"You're being overly cautious. And you're wrong on both counts. First: the apartment is on the ground floor. I saw her close a window. That makes things easier for us second."

"Well, maybe you're right," I decide to ignore him, determined not to be goaded into breaking the law and intruding into a stranger's home, led by a cat who clearly isn't in his right mind.

"Come on, hurry up! I can't carry you any longer!"

Beck groans and sways unsteadily. I hesitate for a moment. The window sill is at least a meter away, and if I fall, it will hurt. But we've come this far; giving up now would be a shame. In fact, we'd managed to sneak past the postman unnoticed into the house through the back door. Now all I have to do is get from the back door stairwell to the window of the apartment in question. So I close my eyes, take a deep breath - and leap.

Maybe it was more like half a meter. Either way, I landed almost effortlessly right in front of the large window. Phew! Two seconds later, Beck joins me. "Wow," he gasps, clearly impressed. "You're not as bad off as I thought!"

We both peer through the window. There they are: Thomas and this woman. And they're... doing what Thomas recently called "going full throttle." It was hard to tell from the dark bedroom, but now it's clear as day: we're witnessing a love-making session.

"I knew it!" Beck exults. "Sex! Call me Sherlock Beck! I may be old, but I still have it!" He jumps up and down excitedly, almost knocking us both off the window ledge. I'm afraid we might fall or get caught. The latter seems less likely, though, since Thomas and his companion are too engrossed in each other to notice anything else.

"So, that's sex," I say dryly after Beck has calmed down.

"Exactly. And people make such a fuss about it! All the who with whom and when and why... you can't believe it."

That's true enough among dachshund breeders. The bitch's mating is always a big deal: the right stud must be found, perhaps even the approval of the German Dachshund Club's breeding registrar obtained, then there's waiting for heat, his readiness to mate... and on and on. But I digress.

The point is: while dachshund breeders have good reason to get all worked up about stud selection and registration with the club, it seems that humans are more concerned with something else entirely. Something weightier than a pedigree or a record in a breeding book. Because if this weren't the case, why would their affair be worth breaking up Carolin and Thomas?

"Dreaming?" Beck asks.

"No, just wondering why love is such a big deal for them."

"Love? What does that have to do with it?"

"Apparently not much," I reply. "It's all about who loves whom and why... "

"Well, duh! That's an elementary question in human relationships!"

"Elementary? You mean love is just... physical attraction?"

"No, of course not! It's..." Beck pauses, searching for the right words. "It's... complicated."

Fine by me. I have a better idea. "If we can't take a picture, then we'll need to find something else that proves it conclusively. Something Carolin will instantly recognize as evidence of what happened."

Beck looks at me in surprise. "You mean... bring something with us? "

"Exactly! Something obvious!" I think for a moment. "I've got it! "

"But, darling, let me explain..." Thomas stammers, "This is a complete coincidence, nothing more!"

"I found a black lace panty in your jacket pocket, and that's a coincidence? Do you really think I'm stupid?"

She should rather ask how stupid Thomas is. It didn't even seem odd to him that she had just dragged his jacket through the hallway. That's just how it goes when you ignore pets. It comes back to bite you bitterly. Carolin, on the other hand, immediately wondered and looked at me. Well, then she discovered the edge of the panty.

"Believe me, Carolin, I have no idea how that panty got there. Not even a little!"

Thomas sounds desperate. But it doesn't soften Carolin's heart.

"I'm tired of your lies, Thomas. The whole time, I've had this feeling something wasn't right. Those strange calls, all your supposed business trips."

Carolin was right about that feeling. It became clear to me in that moment when Herr Beck emerged from the half-open terrace door with the panty in his mouth. After a quick sniff, I knew where that smell came from: Thomas and Carolin's bed. Exactly what I couldn't place at the time was the scent of this woman. She had clearly also slept in Carolin's bed! Can it be? Without having dealt with the matter for a while, I'm pretty sure that's another form of 'deception'. I hope Carolin doesn't give in to Thomas. He really deserves to fly out of here. The chances are excellent. Carolin's voice isn't even remotely conciliatory.

"I booked the hotel back then on Herr Brodkamp - apparently a mistake by your secretary. The scent of a foreign perfume, which I supposedly imagine. Forget it now, it's over! I want you to leave. And immediately! I'm going to see Nina. When I come back, you're gone."

She turns around and walks towards the door.

"But, but... Carolin!" Thomas grabs her arm. "You can't just kick me out like this. I thought we loved each other!"

Carolin looks him straight in the eyes and says with a very firm voice: "I thought so too. But apparently I was wrong. Goodbye, Thomas. Go find Hercules. Nina is waiting for us."

Carolin, I'm proud of you. She handled it perfectly. Not even flinching. Almost icy cold. From my spot under her car floorboard, I can't see her well, but she definitely has a smile on her face. Finally, she's rid of the conman - if that isn't cause for celebration!

I'm happy too. In my mind's eye, I see Carolin and me lounging around on the couch during a cozy TV evening. Will I maybe be able to sleep in the bed soon? After all, it's much too big for one person. A little guy like me would do just fine - oops! The car suddenly stops abruptly, and I'm pressed down very roughly into the floorboard. Ouch, did we have an accident? I struggle to get back up. Suddenly, I realize why Carolin braked so hard: She's lying with her upper body on the steering wheel, face buried in her arms - she's crying. No, she's not just crying; it's shaking her whole body. Her shoulders are trembling, and I hear a sniffling sound that scares me. What's going on? Carolin isn't happy, that much is clear. Silently, I sit next to her, wondering what I can do now. How does one comfort someone?

Slowly, I wiggle my nose under her arms and get to her face. It's warm and wet. I start licking it, first gently, then a bit more aggressively. Mm, nice and salty. Normally, Carolin wouldn't appreciate a dog licking her face, but in this case, she doesn't react at all, which is surprising since usually everyone has an opinion on dogs that slobber on them, often not a good one.

Eventually, Carolin sits up, turns to me, and strokes my head. "You want to comfort me, don't you? That's sweet. I'm really glad I have you."

I try to make some sort of affirmative sound, but it doesn't come out right. So, I lick her hands again. She giggles a little. At least that!

"It's okay, sweetheart. You probably don't understand, right? Don't know what happened, poor thing."

Well, I wouldn't put it quite like that, but maybe it's for the best that Carolin isn't exactly up-to-date on all the details of this whole affair. She wipes her tears away.

"Everything will be alright, don't worry. It will get better."

Is she talking to me or herself? In any case, she stopped crying and drove off again.

Carl-Leopold von Eschersbach, Hopefully it was a good idea to get involved in this mess.

EIGHT

"Hercules, old buddy, I wish you could talk." Daniel lifts me up onto his workbench and looks at me. "I'd really like to know what happened between Thomas and Carolin." He scratches my neck. "But she won't tell me - and you can't either."

To be honest, even if I could speak, I wouldn't tell Daniel what happened. Because now that I think about it, I wish we had never come up with the stupid idea of the panty. When Carolin and I returned from Nina's, Thomas was already gone. But nothing else happened as I'd hoped. We're not sitting comfortably on the couch together, snuggled up. I'm not sleeping in Thomas' side of the bed either. No, since Thomas left, Carolin has changed completely. She cries a lot. She doesn't talk to me anymore. She barely talks to anyone. And she hardly sleeps. She walks around aimlessly in the apartment and listens to music loudly. Sometimes so loudly that it gets on the nerves of the other residents - and that's saying something. But when the neighbors knock and complain, Carolin just stares at them wordlessly and shuts the door again. Although she does turn down the volume a bit, nothing else changes. She continues wandering around aimlessly in the apartment.

For four days now, she hasn't gone to work either. She's been taking me down to Daniel in the morning. She said very little, just asked if Daniel could look after me during the day. So right now, I spend my days with him, and in the evening, he brings me back up. While doing so, he tries to get Carolin to talk, but unfortunately it never works out.

"Honestly, Hercules, I'm worried. That she's taking Thomas' departure so hard is terrible. I mean, you'll agree: The guy was a complete idiot, and we don't need to mourn him. Especially not when you have such a fantastic woman like Carolin."

The only nice thing about the current situation is the conversations between Daniel and me. Well, maybe "conversation" is a bit strong, but Daniel talks to me quite a lot. It's probably no wonder, after all we're mostly alone now. But I learn a lot about people in general and Carolin in particular, and of course about Daniel. He knew Carolin before Thomas came along. They learned together how to build these wooden instruments—violins, cellos, that sort of thing. It was somewhere far away, in a place with a wonderful name: Mittenwald. It must have been an amazing town if it had such a name. Right in the middle of the forest.

In Daniel and Carolin's violin-making class, there were lots of girls, but none was like Carolin. Daniel noticed that right away, and soon they became best friends. They even lived together. Many things that each of them did for the first time in their lives, they experienced together: the first big house cleaning, the first homemade Sunday roast, the first Christmas without parents. Only the first great love was individual to each person. That had the advantage of allowing them to comfort each other afterward.

When Daniel tells his stories, I almost feel like I'm a human myself. At least, I think I'm starting to understand how bipeds tick. Of course, Mr. Beck has explained some things to me as well, but hearing it from the subject itself sounds more... credible. With Beck, I'm not always sure if he's not making parts up to make the story more interesting.

Daniel pats me on the head again, then sets me back down on the floor. "Alright, now I need to get a few things done or we'll sink into chaos here for sure. A special customer is coming in shortly. She probably won't be easy to recognize—a dog, not a human. But as a man, I can assure you: a sight to behold! An excellent musician, too. And temperament—oh là là! Not from bad parents, that lady. Sometimes you have to rein her in a bit, but it's always nice to see her."

He starts humming a melody and tidies up his workbench.

This is really boring. And if this excellent musician also plays the violin, then that's definitely not for me. I stroll toward the terrace door. Maybe I'll run into Mr. Beck in the garden. A nice chat between pets would be nice right about now.

But there's no sign of Beck, neither behind the house nor in the front yard. Instead, I make an interesting discovery: Right on the little wall that surrounds our front yard, a young woman has taken a seat. She sits there doing something with her face. I approach closer to see better. She doesn't even notice me, so absorbed is she... what exactly? On superficial observation, I'd say she's painting herself. At least, she holds a sponge in one hand, on which bright paint is applied, and then smears that paint onto her nose. A moment later, she takes a pen and applies some red paste to her mouth. Hm, strange.

The woman packs her art supplies back into her bag and stands up. Then she quickly bends down and shakes her hair over her head. It looks very much like a dog shaking off water after swimming. That humans do that without water—another proof that bipeds are irrationally acting creatures, devoid of sense and reason. She throws the completely dry hair over her shoulders. They're long, black, and curly. Reminiscent of the Hungarian shepherd dog who once visited our castle at Eschersbach. I suddenly wondered how he could see the sheep he was supposed to guard.

Now I see that the woman has a suitcase by her side. Obviously a violin case, as I now know. So this must be the musician mentioned earlier. Whether she's particularly beautiful in human eyes, I can't say. It's hard to tell, since she's painted her face so that it's no longer recognizable in its original form. The most beautiful woman in the world is Carolin, and the rest don't interest me.

The painted woman approaches the entrance, I walk back through the garden to the terrace door, and stand next to Daniel when he opens the workshop door.

"Daniel, my dear!"

She wraps her arms around him and kisses him. I do my best to see if it's with or without tongue. I've learned a thing or two. Unfortunately, I can't really tell, her voluminous curls hide both faces. In my own interest, I hope this is just a normal greeting, because right now I don't need anything else. Even though I wasn't innocent in the recent developments. So far, the workshop has been a good retreat from human emotional turmoil, and that should remain the case.

"Wow, Aurora, you look fantastic as always! Come on in, I've been waiting for you. Carolin is unfortunately sick and won't be at the workshop this week."

"Poor thing! What's wrong with her?"

Do I imagine it, or does this concern sound somewhat insincere? I'd wager a large slice of sausage that Aurora is actually relieved not to see Carolin.

"She has a bad cold. She has a pounding headache, and I advised her to take some time off."

"Good idea." Aurora raises her hand and makes a threatening gesture with her index finger. "Just make sure you don't catch it from her, my dear." Now she notices me too. "Since when do you have a dog?"

"Carolin brought him home from the animal shelter last month. A sweet little guy, not? I'm helping out as a dog-sitter while she's sick."

"That's nice of you. I'm not really a dog person, I prefer cats. But he is quite cute."

Grrr, she prefers cats? Maybe I'll give her a little tap on the heels to justify her cat love.

"So, let me see this beauty, I'm curious." Daniel helps Aurora out of her coat and leads her into his workshop space.

"You're right, Daniel. She's absolutely gorgeous."

She hands him the violin case, he places it on his workbench, carefully takes the violin out, and spins it around. Then he whistles in approval.

"Impressive! Cremonese school, no doubt about it!"

"I was so excited when the broker called me. I'd been looking for such an instrument for a long time. Last week the appraisal was finished, and yesterday it arrived by express from London."

"Well, there's a crack in the lid, the curves are warped—but overall, it doesn't seem too bad. I'd say there's hope."

Aurora lets out a whoop of joy and wraps her arms around Daniel again.

"I knew you were the best! Thank you, thank you, thank you!"

With a certain satisfaction, I notice Daniel gently pushing her away.

"No thanks, it's my job."

"When can we start?"

Daniel looks at the calendar hanging on the opposite wall.

"Hm, wait a moment. This week is out of the question because I'm all alone right now. But for next week, I had you scheduled prophylactically; I'll definitely start then. How long it will take, I can't say yet. It also depends on what else I discover when I open it."

Aurora nods and puts her hand on Daniel's arm. "Call me if you see more clearly. Do you plan to come to my concert at the Music Hall next week?"

"I'm not sure. There's so much going on here..." He raises his hands in a defensive gesture.

"Well, I just hope that poor Carolin gets back on her feet soon. You'd really be missing out. We could go for dinner and celebrate afterwards. The new violin needs to be toasted." How does that sound?

"Man, Aurora, that sounds amazing. I'll see what I can do. Well, I have to go now." With a friendly but unmistakable gesture, he leads Aurora to the exit and helps her into her coat.

"So, see you next week! I count on you; please try your best!"

Daniel smiles. "I will. And that's why I'm going to be working hard from now on."

He opens the door for her; before she leaves, she gives him a quick kiss on the cheek without tongue.

Carolin greets us at the door and looks somehow strange. She also smells strange. A scent I've sniffed once or twice with the old Escherbach.

"Good evening, both of you, come in."

"Is everything okay with you?" Daniel asks.

"Of course it is, don't worry about that. I'm just a little tired."

Hard to believe: Even Carolin's voice sounds strange. It's dragging and muffled. All of a sudden, I feel very uncomfortable.

Daniel goes behind me into the apartment while I run to my basket. He sits on the couch in the living room.

"Aurora Herwig was here today," he reports.

"Oh! The beautiful violinist! How is she doing?"

"She's doing great; she got a cheap old Italian master from London, I think. Cremona, if I remember correctly. I haven't read the expert opinion yet. Aurora was over the moon."

Carolin starts giggling. "Well, that's good to hear that Aurora is happy. Then everything's fine."

"Say, Carolin, are you sure everything is okay? You look a bit off. I'm really worried about you; apart from that, I miss you terribly in the workshop."

Carolin sits next to Daniel and puts her hand on his shoulder. "Don't worry, it'll all be fine. Next week, I'll be back to my old self, I just need some rest. Now go home. I'm tired too and going straight to bed."

"Okay, goodnight!"

Daniel bends down to hug Carolin but she dodges him.

"Good night."

Daniel leaves; I'm left alone with Carolin. I can't quite say why, but the uneasiness has turned into fear. Something's not right here. I'd love to chase Daniel and bring him back, but how? Ugh, something tells me that Carolin shouldn't be alone right now. Not 'alone' in the sense of 'without other people'. I don't want to underestimate my company, but this place needs more than a little dog. Definitely.

Carolin sits on the couch for a while then stands up, goes to the stereo and turns on music again. It's driving me crazy: This music has been playing for almost a week; I could rip out my ears. I run to her and tug at her jeans. "Hey, now pay attention to me! I'm here too!" But she just looks at me with glassy eyes for a moment before heading towards the kitchen. I follow her. Although Daniel has already fed me, I wouldn't mind some reconciliation sausage right about now. It would be nice if Carolin thought of me once in a while; I'm starting to feel a bit offended.

Indeed, she opens the fridge - but only to grab a bottle. She takes a glass and pours something into it. Ah, that's where the smell comes from! Apparently, she's been drinking more of this stuff. As she makes her way back towards the living room, she almost steps on my paws. Ow! I bark loudly. This can't go on like this! I decide to retreat to my basket.

A while later, I hear a rumble. Curious, I jump up and run towards the noise. In the living room, Carolin is just getting up. Ouch! Did she fall? I rush to her and lick her hands. She's not that angry with me after all.

"Thanks for asking, Hercules. Everything's fine; I was just trying to reach something on top of this chair, but it was wobbly." She looks at the board above mentioned and there are more bottles on it. Carolin stands up, puts the chair back in place, and climbs onto it again. This time, she succeeds, and she retrieves one of the bottles. The liquid has a nice golden-brown color, but as Carolin opens the bottle, a sharp smell reaches me. Ugh, this is probably better left for external use - Carolin wouldn't drink that, would she?

She does. She pours the liquid into her glass and takes a large gulp.

"Want to try it too, Hercules?"

I shake my head and growl. "No way!"

"Then cheers! I'll pour some in your basket; it's cozy." She laughs and pours herself another glass.

That can't be right, that must be a joke. Even though there's now a light brown stain on the carpet, I wouldn't suggest turning it into my basket. In fact, she goes to the closet, takes out scissors, and kneels down on the carpet.

"Let's see if we can make something interesting out of this mess." She lifts an edge, takes the scissors, and starts cutting. "Darn, it's pretty tough." But with grunting and straining, she continues to work on the fabric - I'm really amazed. Soon, the carpet has lost its round shape and looks like a very large, very angry animal that bit into it several times. Carolin takes a small break and pours herself another glass. The bottle, which was almost full earlier, is now nearly empty. Carolin looks at me.

"Will you stay with me, sweetie?" She whispers.

I assume so because right now, I would definitely stay by her side even if my sensitive dog nose is suffering from this overwhelming scent. She leans back and stares at the ceiling. Almost mechanically, she scratches me behind the neck. Then she mumbles "I need to get some more" and tries to stand up - but she falls down unexpectedly. She struggles to get up but can't quite manage it.

What's wrong with her? She tries again to stand but fails miserably.

"She's not doing well at all," she murmurs, then starts to gag. Her whole body contorts, and it looks like she's in pain.

I suddenly feel an overwhelming fear. What should I do? What's going on here?

Carolin keeps gagging, and I see her spitting up onto the bright carpet—or what's left of it. Now everything is clear: Carolin has poisoned herself! Probably with that stuff from the bottle! The last time I saw someone throw up was when Mama's sister Luise did, and a bad neighbor had mixed something into her food. We need a doctor immediately, or things could get much worse!

I run back and forth, finally returning to Carolin's head, where she now lies motionless next to her vomit. I bark loudly so she'll wake up—but she doesn't move. What should I do? Carolin needs help, right away.

Maybe another neighbor will hear if I make more noise? After all, they did complain about the music. I bark and growl, jumping up and down. Three minutes, five minutes, maybe ten. But nothing happens. Exhausted, I take a break. Damn, is nobody else in the house besides us today? Not even Beck?

Carolin remains unconscious, her face slowly turning pale. I crawl over to her and listen intently. Thank goodness, she's still breathing. I lie down at her head end, my snout on my front paws, listening to her breath. Sometimes it stops for a moment, and Carolin lets out a groan. What a terrible situation. And I've gotten us into this mess. It's all my fault—if I hadn't set Thomas up, he'd still be here, and Carolin wouldn't have poisoned herself.

I take another run at it, jumping back and forth in front of the balcony door while barking. The door is slightly ajar; maybe someone outside will hear me? I'm so busy jumping and barking that I almost miss the phone ringing when I finally notice it. Does one of the neighbors call? No, I know to this day I don't understand how people manage to talk on the phone! But maybe this is my only chance to alert someone. I have to try, quickly before it stops ringing. At least I've figured out something about phones.

The phone is on a little table in the living room. Carolin always picks up when she's talking on the phone, so I run over and try to lift it with my snout. But that's not easy; the phone is quite large. On my first attempt, I don't quite get it, and on the second, it falls out of my mouth. Grrr, nothing seems to be working today. Hopefully I haven't broken it. Carefully, I sniff at the black object lying on the floor in front of me. Can we still use this phone? And if so, how? As I look closer, I hear a voice that sounds very far away. I bark excitedly! If I can hear the voice, maybe the voice can also hear me. Although I'm not quite sure if the voice knows where I am, I give it my all: barking, growling, whining, yowling, and heaving—all in the direction of the phone. Occasionally, I listen for the voice again: It seems to still be there. Unfortunately, I don't understand what she's saying, but I think I've heard my name once. Does the phone actually know how I'm called?

Suddenly, the voice is gone, replaced only by a buzzing sound. Frustrated, I bark at the phone. Probably all my barking didn't do any good. I trot back to Carolin and lie down next to her. If she's in such bad shape, at least she shouldn't be lying alone.

The apartment is completely silent. For the first time in a long while, I would very much like to be back at Schloss Eschersbach.

NINE

Was I asleep? I'm not entirely sure. Now, however, I'm wide awake because finally something happens. First, the doorbell rings, then after some time, a key turns in the lock and the door opens. "Carolin, are you there?"

By all that's holy—it's Daniel! I immediately run to him, jumping up and trying to lick him.

"Hey, hey, Hercules! That's quite a greeting. Where's your lady? We're worried about her."

We? Now I realize Nina is also standing in the hallway of our apartment building.

"Daniel, I have a bad feeling. It just doesn't seem right that Hercules answered the phone and we can't reach Carolin anymore. And how this smells—disgusting!"

"Okay, let's take a look."

He enters the apartment, and I run ahead to the living room. Come on, follow me! I stop next to Carolin and bark loudly. "Oh my God, Carolin!"

Daniel is behind me, kneeling down next to Carolin. Nina also comes into the living room. As she sees Carolin lying there, she covers her face with her hands. "Oh no, what happened?"

Daniel takes Carolin's hand. "At least she has a pulse," he says, shaking her shoulder, "Carolin! Wake up."

She doesn't move. He turns her to the side, away from the vomit, and wipes her face with a tissue he pulls out of his pocket.

"I'm calling an ambulance right now."

He stands up and goes over to the phone that's still lying where I dropped it. After a brief conversation, he comes back to us. Nina also sits down on the floor next to us.

"What does all this mean? Carolin unconscious, the carpet torn apart. How long has she been here?"

"Well, I came home two hours ago. Before that, I left Hercules with her. She did seem a bit tipsy—but okay, that can happen. Especially after a breakup. However, she hadn't been to the workshop for four days because she was so down. But she promised to come next week."

"Darn it, I should have prodded more," Nina says.

"And thank goodness you called right away," Daniel adds. "If not, we wouldn't be here."

"And thanks to you for having a key!" Nina pets me and says, "You're quite the smart dog. You realized Carolin needed help, didn't you?"

"Absolutely," Daniel agrees, "if you hadn't made such a racket on the phone, we might not have gotten here in time."

The doorbell rings again, and Daniel lets in three men. The three look like they've been disguised; they're wearing jackets that strongly remind me of garbage collectors — only I'm pretty sure these aren't garbage men. One goes straight to Carolin. Before he kneels down, he briefly turns to Daniel.

"What's her name?"

"Carolin Neumann."

"Your wife?"

"No, a good friend."

The man does essentially the same thing as Daniel — he first shakes her awake.

"Mrs. Neumann, can you hear me?"

Of course not! We've already come this far. He takes her hand and feels around her wrist, just like Daniel. Damn it, why did we call? This guy doesn't even have a new idea. I try to get as close to him as possible. He should know he's being watched. But now he does something we haven't considered: he opens her eyes with his fingers and looks inside, then pulls something out of his jacket pocket, at first looking like a pen.

"Hmm, there is a pulse, but weak. Quite dilated pupils."

He opens one of her eyes again and points the pen in that direction. Ah, a flashlight! Strange, what's he doing?

"Hmm, very slow reaction. She also threw up. Do you know what your friend drank?"

Daniel shakes his head. Ha, but I do! I rush off and find, under the pulled-out carpet pieces, the empty bottle, grab it, and bring it back properly. The man with the flashlight nods approvingly.

"Well, if that's not a thinking dog, I don't know what is! Very good! Let me see: at least she has good taste. But do you really have to drink an entire bottle? That's a different question. To be honest: Does she usually drink anything else?" Hennessy VS.O.P.

Now Nina intervenes.

"Of course not! What do you think? Mrs. Neumann usually only drinks a glass of wine in the evening. But right now she's not feeling well, she just threw her boyfriend out, that filthy pig!"

"Nina, please," Daniel interjects, "this doesn't really matter here."

Mr. Garbage Man Jacket smiles and shakes his head. "No, it's all good. And actually does matter — do you think your friend might have taken something else besides alcohol? Maybe pills?"

Daniel and Nina shrug their shoulders.

"I don't think so," Daniel finally says, "but I'll take a quick look around."

Shortly after, he's back and shakes his head. "Nothing found. But I actually also exclude that."

The man nods. "Alright, my colleagues and I are taking Mrs. Neumann now. She's definitely had quite a bit of alcohol.

Is that very dangerous? Alcohol poisoning?

"I'll give her an IV right away in the ambulance to lower the alcohol concentration a bit; we'll see more at the hospital. She'll probably stay there for the next three days." So, guys," he addresses the other two men, "let's go."

The two men grab a stretcher from the hallway and place it next to Carolin, lift her onto it, and then walk away with her. The third Garbage Man Jacket briefly says goodbye to us, then disappears as well. I notice a sudden feeling creeping over me: sadness and loneliness. A little guy like me needs his mom! Will I have to go back to the shelter?

"What do we do now?" Nina looks at Daniel questioningly.

"I think one of us should also go to the hospital. So someone's there when Carolin wakes up."

Nina nods. "Good idea. She really shouldn't be alone in this situation. What about you going ahead? I'll clean up a bit here, then come after."

"Okay. What do we do with Hercules?"

The two look at me. I'd like to bark loudly, but only a pitiful yes comes out. Not the shelter!,

"Look how miserable he looks! He must have been terrified too. We can't leave him alone. Besides, he's Carolin's savior, so he deserves a reward."

Finally, a sensible thought from Nina.

"I suggest I take him with me later. From my side, he can also stay at my place tonight; then I'll bring him to the workshop tomorrow."

"Sounds good. I'll do night duty with Carolin — if necessary. And I'll take care of Hercules tomorrow."

"See you later!"

After he leaves, Nina starts cleaning up the mess in the living room with a bucket and scrubber. Once she's finished, she stands there bewildered in front of the remains of the white carpet.

"Can you maybe explain to me what happened here?" She wants to know from me while holding up one of the carpet pieces. She turns it this way and that, then starts grinning. "I recognize this carpet — Thomas wanted it badly, but Carolin thought it was ugly. Plus, it was really expensive. It almost looks like someone cut her into little pieces here." She puts the piece back down. "That's good news for the patient's recovery."

What that has to do with sick Carolin, however, doesn't make any sense to me. But it's reassuring to hear Nina consider it a good sign.

Before we leave, Nina goes through the whole apartment once more to check if everything is in order. In doing so, she discovers something I had already long forgotten: She comes out of the bedroom with the plastic neck brace Carolin had left there on the windowsill.

"Look what I found here, Hercules!"

Yes, I see it. Great. What do you want to do with that? You don't even have a dog, and it's definitely not going to fit around a human neck.

"We need to take that back to Dr. Wagner; he'll probably be missing it."

I don't believe that, but if Nina is such an orderly, conscientious person — then fine. The main thing is I don't have to come along and get tortured again.

We don't go to the hospital after all. Daniel calls and says everything's okay for now and he can stay longer. A huge stone falls from my heart. Just thinking about Carolin being seriously ill is terrifying. What was she drinking? I decide from now on, I'll have to be more careful with her; otherwise, this won't happen again.

Nina's apartment is much smaller than Carolin's and smells completely different as well. Almost a bit dusty, but still quite good. It must be due to all the books that are everywhere. Almost every wall has a shelf, and each one is filled to the top with books. Big ones, small ones, thick ones, thin ones. Hard to believe she read them all. It's even harder to imagine, especially since I can't read myself. I still don't quite understand how it works. One thing is clear, though: you need an incredibly long time to look at a sheet with a strange pattern, and something happens to people during that time.

In her head, I mean. Because from time to time they start laughing when they look at a piece of paper — even though nobody said anything and nothing else happened either. Or they even cry. That's what I've observed with Emilia from time to time. She also reads a lot. So, what does paper do to the human head? Does it produce some kind of hallucination or dream? Should I ask Beck again when I see him next, I definitely have to.

The next important question is obviously where I'm going to sleep. Because I'm realizing that I'm incredibly tired. Nina wouldn't let me sleep in her bed anyway. As I assess this woman, even my most beautiful dachshund stare wouldn't soften her. Or should I give it a try? After all, she said herself: I deserve a reward.

So I get up and tug at Nina's leg a bit.

"Well, sweetheart, are you tired too? I'm trying to figure out where you can sleep. I don't have a basket for you."

Now is the perfect time to tilt your head and look incredibly cute. I try it. Nina looks at me in surprise.

"Do you want to tell me something? You look so...so strange."

Strange? Impudence? I look absolutely adorable, bite-sized, irresistible! Now take a closer look! I lean my head even further and pimp a bit.

"Hmm, are you sick? Or do you want something specific?"

I bark briefly and run off. Somewhere in this house, the bedroom must be, it's not exactly spacious. Behind the next door, I find it. I rush inside and sit on my doggy poo. Nina follows.

"So, that sounds a bit crazy and like...but do you want to sleep in my bed? Prince Charles talks with plants."

I cast her another loyal look and now present a trick I haven't demonstrated very often: I make myself up! And it even works, I stand at least a minute as if made of stone. So, if that doesn't work, I don't know what to do.

Nina looks — and erupts into peals of laughter. "That's just delicious! Hercules, where did you get that from?"

Offended, I sit back down. This woman clearly has no idea about art. And she obviously doesn't know how difficult it is for a dog to go vertical. Yes, as a human, that's nothing. Everyone can do it. But for me, that was quite good. Sneaky! I don't even want to get into bed with her anymore. I'd rather sleep on the doormat and anyway...

"Come on, you rascal! Hop in!" Nina quickly flips back the duvet at the foot of the bed and invites me invitingly onto the mattress.

I said sneaky? Nonsense. Nina is a nice girl.

"Good morning, both of you. I don't know who looks worse — you or Daniel."

The next day, Nina and I didn't go to the workshop, but straight to the hospital. Daniel had been guarding Carolyn's bed all night. Accordingly, he looks wrinkled. That's just a disadvantage when you don't have fur on your face: an unhealthy lifestyle is clearly harder to hide.

Carolyn is very pale around her nose, but at least she's no longer unconscious. She sits in her bed and even manages to crack a smile.

"Hello, Nina, thanks for coming. And thank you for bringing Hercules along."

"Well, animals are actually banned on the ward, but when I explained to the head nurse that Hercules is kind of your life saver, she gave me a pass."

Carolyn nods. "Daniel told me. Come here, Hercules, let me scratch you a bit."

Only too happy! Nina sets me down in the chair next to Carolyn's bed and then we cuddle for a while.

"Oh people, I'm so embarrassed! How could this have happened? Unfortunately, I can't remember anything either — although that might be better like this."

Daniel takes Carolyn's hand. "Come on, you really don't need to be embarrassed. We're your friends. Besides, we of course expect you to hold up your end too when we once crack open a bottle of cognac and cut strange holes in carpets."

Carolyn blushes. "Just stop it, I can't hear myself think!"

Daniel laughs. "Alright, my dears. I'm heading home. There's a lot of work waiting for me on my workshop bench, yesterday Aurora brought an important restoration project, I really don't know where to start. But first, I need to take a quick nap, otherwise I might accidentally drill holes in Aurora's artifact where they don't belong."

When Daniel leaves, Nina and Carolyn sit silently for a while. I have my head on Carolyn's lap and enjoy being scratched behind the ears.

"Carolyn, I'm seriously worried about you," says Nina finally.

"Well, I'm not used to this kind of thing. It wasn't intentional. But I wasn't feeling good, so I drank a bit too much."

"Come on? You know what I mean. 3.2 promille — one more cognac and you might have fallen into a coma. That didn't just happen by accident."

Carolyn stops scratching me. "What do you mean?"

"You know exactly. Daniel told me that you hadn't been to the workshop all week. And that he takes care of Hercules during the day because you're not doing well. "

Carolyn remains silent.

"It's clear I'm worried about you. Thomas was an asshole, right? I was so happy when you finally got rid of him. Of course, you don't feel good now, but that's normal and you'll get over it. Please don't think your life will be sad forever. That's not the case."

Carolyn starts to cry, Nina gives her a tissue.

"Since Thomas left, I've been so lonely. I'm afraid I'll never be happy again. I always wanted a family, children. But that seems even further away than I thought. In essence, I only have Hercules."

What do you mean by "only"? A loyal dog is better than a deceitful scoundrel! I will never let Carolyn down like that. Quickly, I nuzzle her hands. Nina nods at me.

"You get it, Hercules, right? But she's so cute — I can understand Carolyn. A dog isn't a human."

I want to shout, because at least on me you can rely. Luckily! "You know, Thomas wasn't perfect either. But who is? I think now that maybe I should have forgiven him. Maybe I was too hard on him."

Nina snorts in disgust. "So please! That sounds like a lot of self-pity. I don't understand how such a great woman as you could sell yourself short like this. You'll meet the right man, I'm sure of it. And then you'll realize that today was your lucky day, the day everything turned around!"

Carolyn looks doubtful. "Well, if fate wants it, I guess I can meet my prince in the park behind our house. I wouldn't have to look far."

Of course, that's it! A brilliant idea comes to mind. We need a prince! And who better than Carolin? She saved me from dire straits before. Now I'll save her. Even if I have to dig up the whole park for it.

TEN

Hopefully we're heading home right away. I absolutely must tell Beck about my sensational plan. The basic framework is already in place, but I need a deep people-reader for the crucial details. Someone like Mr. Beck.

Finally, Nina pulls over and walks around the car, opening my door. In that moment as I hop out, I see it: We're not home. Worse yet: Nina pulls out my plastic collar from the back seat. Oh no - I sense something bad is about to happen. We're back at the vet's!

The car door isn't even closed when I quickly jump back onto the passenger seat. That stupid plastic piece, Nina can handle that herself; she doesn't need me for it.

"Hercules, what's wrong with you? Get out, we're going to Dr. Wagner."

I growl and bare my teeth. There's no way I'm leaving here. Plus, I feel fantastic. My ear doesn't hurt anymore, I don't have any other issues. What's going on?

"Now come on, sweetie, jump out!" Nina urges.

I firmly shake my head. Nina sighs, then rummages in her handbag. Finally, she pulls out my dog leash. Does she want to drag me there by force?

She does - because shortly after, she secures the leash to my collar and gently but resolutely leads me towards the sidewalk. Clearly, she means business. I briefly consider whether I should stubbornly refuse to cooperate, but given our size difference, I'd lose. It's heartbreaking!

At the clinic, Nina marches me to the counter and hands over the collar to the receptionist.

"I'd also like to speak with the doctor. He should take another look at Hercules. He's such a delicate, sensitive little guy, and I'm always so worried about him."

"That's not really necessary," the assistant replies, smiling.

Right, good girl!

"Still, if you insist. But you'll have to wait for a moment. There are still some patients ahead of you."

Just then, Dr. Wagner pokes his head out from behind the treatment room door.

"Oh, hello Mrs. Bogner!" he greets Nina. "Back with... uh...?"

"Hercules," she helps him out.

Ugh, he still remembers my name, but has forgotten mine. Maybe Wagner should switch from veterinary to gynecology.

"Right, Hercules. The dachshund mix." Grrr!

"So it's not exactly a problem," he says, "but I'm taking care of the little guy right now because Mrs. Neumann is sick. And I don't want to make any mistakes. That's why I'd really appreciate it if you took another look at Hercules, just to be sure everything is okay with him."

Dr. Wagner grins. Yeah, how weird that is... ha, ha!

"Alright then, come on in."

A moment later, I find myself on the examination table, and Dr. Wagner holds my ear in his hand. He smoothly runs his hand over my fur and passes his finger over the spot where the tick had been attached.

"Let me assure you, everything looks very good. Hercules is back to his old self."

"Hmm, are you sure? What about Lyme disease? I read recently that dogs can get it too."

Lyme... what? My ears perk up.

"Then close monitoring would be advisable," he explains, "but not to worry, Hercules doesn't seem to have anything. And I wouldn't want to subject him to unnecessary stress. Perhaps we can take some blood next time you're here."

What?! In one bound, I leap off the table, glower at both of them, and give a short but loud bark.

Wagner laughs. "See, Mrs. Bogner, Hercules doesn't like your suggestion at all. And frankly, I don't either. Lyme disease is rare in Hamburg, and Hercules seems perfectly healthy."

"Oh?"

Nina looks very disappointed. She's clearly a sadist.

"But here's another proposal: Before you keep dragging this poor dachshund to me, why don't you simply ask me if I'd like to grab a drink with you when you see me again?"

Nina gasps and looks completely shocked. Why? I think this is a brilliant idea.

"So really... I... I..." Then Nina breaks into laughter and can barely control herself. "Alright, you've got it. Tonight at 8 o'clock at Cavallol."

Wagner nods. "Very much so, Mrs. Bogner. Very much so."

Nina drives and sings loudly while driving, laughing all the while. Then she turns to me briefly.

"My goodness! It's incredible. I asked him if he'd like to go out with me tonight, and Hercules... brings me luck. That's for sure."

Cavallo

It's nice to hear that, but I'm still not entirely clear what the big deal is about this whole thing. Wagner said she could just tell him when she wants to see him. And why did Nina get so scared? It seems communication between men and women is even more complicated than a dog would think at first glance. There must be some secret rulebook I'm still unaware of: "He says this, she says that."

We pull up in front of our house. Finally, home! Although I wasn't gone for long, I can't wait to consult Mr. Beck on how to put my brilliant plan into action. Nina and I walk through the garden to the back door of the workshop. She knocks on the window, two seconds later Daniel opens it.

"Up and at 'em?" Nina asks.

"Yeah, more or less," I reply. "I'll just go to bed a bit earlier tonight, and that should do the trick."

"Can you take Hercules with you today? I have a date this evening, but it's definitely a no-pets event."

"A date?"

"Sort of."

"Details later?"

"Maybe."

"Well then, Auf Wiedersehen!"

"Auf Wiedersehen danke!"

I'm buzzing! How many times have I heard that phrase at Schloss Eschersbach. And every time it was the prelude to a great adventure I was too young to join. Only Mama and her sister were allowed to go. Opili was too old, but he stayed home with us and told stories of the grandest thing a dachshund could ever experience: hunting. He always made his tales so vivid that I always felt like I'd been right there myself. The scent of rabbits, the trail of red deer, the smell of excitement and joy - wonderful! How excited I was for my first hunt. I knew it would be my true calling: side by side with my hunter through the forests! Auf Wiedersehen!

Through my nose, a fine tingling - Nina and Dr. Wagner are thus going hunting! I'm so excited that all of a sudden, my conversation with Mr. Beck is utterly indifferent to me. Dr. Wagner suddenly appears in a whole new light. A hunter - no wonder Nina wanted to see him again! But why don't they take me along? I lie down right in front of Nina's feet and whine. I want to go too! Absolutely!

"One thing is certain: Hercules, the former owner, was a hunter. Look how he reacts to the 'halali' - that little guy is really worked up!" Daniel crouches down to me and scratches my belly. "But unfortunately, you misunderstood. The type of hunt Nina is going on tonight is completely boring for small dachshunds. You wouldn't miss anything if you stay with me."

My communication problem rears its head again. And it's not just between men and women, but also between women and dachshunds. Disappointed, I decide to look for another four-legged friend in the house. At least one who understands me. And more importantly, someone I understand.

"Come on, don't hang your head! I don't think Nina is really going hunting. Anyway, she's definitely not hunting hares. Rather, this veterinarian." Beck grins.

I groan internally. Now he's also started speaking in riddles! Humans just don't get along with animals.

"When men or women talk about the other gender in terms of hunting," Beck lectures now, "it doesn't mean they're planning to go into the forest together and bag the next wild boar. Rather, it usually means they're talking about the art of courtship. You understand? Men hunt women, and women hunt men. But not literally meant."

I shake my head in disbelief. "Why don't they just say what they mean?"

Beck shrugs. "No idea. For some reason, the one who the other person wants as a partner must under no circumstances find out. On the contrary - one has to act as if one doesn't want anything to do with them."

"Got it. That's like hunting then. Moving slowly and silently. Weighing the prey until the end. The smartest way to bag even the wisest."

"You're right," Beck concedes.

"So, this whole courtship thing is more like a stalk than a drive," I muse. "Which fits, by the way, with Nina's discomfort when the veterinarian immediately noticed she wanted to 'bag' him. Using the metaphor, of course."

"Exactly. A man can't even suspect that a woman has set her sights on him or it won't work."

As Beck says this, I suddenly remember what I actually wanted to tell him. "By the way, have you heard about Carolin? She's not doing well in hospital!"

"Ah, yes, heartache. It'll pass."

"But she's in the hospital! That's serious!"

"Oh, I didn't know humans could go to the hospital for something like that. Of course, I'm sorry about it."

"You should be - after all, it was your idea!"

"Hold on: What do you mean by 'it was my idea'?"

"If you hadn't meddled with Thomas and Carolin, Thomas would still be here, and Carolin wouldn't be so unhappy."

Beck snorts. "Listen, we did that for you! You were afraid Thomas would kick you out, right? Plus, Carolin was already unhappy before. She just didn't realize it."

Alright, the cat has his paws in this one, and I don't want to start an argument. So, I take a conciliatory tone.

"Calm down - I'm not trying to start an argument with you. I have a brilliant idea."

Beck looks at me skeptically but says nothing. Instead, his tail tip starts twitching frantically. To give my words more weight, I stretch and take a deep breath.

"So here's the plan: We find a new man for Carolin. And not just any man - a prince! At least someone really great."

Ta-da! I'm curious about Beck's reaction. Unfortunately, none comes.

"Hey, still mad?"

"No. But that idea is ridiculous."

"Why? I think it's fantastic."

"Well, because you have no clue about people - or women."

Now I'm the one looking offended.

"Hercules, how do you even suggest this? I'm pretty sure Carolin's taste in men isn't compatible with young dogs. Plus, her taste is terrible!"

"But that's exactly the point! Carolin's taste in men! It's not just bad; it's catastrophic! If we wait until she chooses again, we might soon have Thomas back in the house. She simply doesn't know what's good for her."

"Oh, but you do, right?"

"Absolutely. I know. We're looking for a man I can go hunting with without worry. Someone stand-up, of course, since Carolin isn't just anyone. But also someone who'll treat his dog well - feed him regularly and take him on walks. Because if you treat your dog like that, you'll treat your wife the same way. But Carolin doesn't seem to notice those basic things."

Mr. Beck sighs. "Of course she doesn't. She's a person, not a dachshund. Forgotten?" He adds, "Also, women don't like nice guys."

"What?!"

Poor Beck - I think it might be old age.

"Exactly: Women don't like nice guys. Because if they did, Carolin would already be with Daniel. He's head-over-heels in love with her, and she likes him too. But he's just too nice to her. Way too nice. It won't work." Thomas, on the other hand, "basically" did everything right. Well, almost everything. Remember this: Nice guys don't get taken seriously by women - and especially not by attractive ones."

"Do you mean that women deliberately choose unpleasant types like Thomas?"

"Exactly."

"But that's terrible!"

"The fact is: If Daniel or anyone else wants to land Carolin, he has to treat her badly."

I'm stunned - and confused. That can't be right! It would ultimately mean women are mistreated. Mr. Beck must be mistaken; otherwise, my prediction for Carolin's love life looks bleak. But on the other hand... In one point, Beck is right: Daniel is really nice, and I get the feeling he adores Carolin. Everything seems to fit. So there must be a reason why Carolin fell for Thomas instead of Daniel.

For a while, Beck and I sit silently side by side. I need time to process what I've just heard. My beautiful plan - sadly, I rest my head on my front paws.

"However," Beck says after a moment, "if I think about it, your idea might not be so bad after all. It's true that we animals have a much better understanding of people than humans do themselves. Perhaps we can indeed save Carolin from her next disaster."

"And how would we do that? You just said yourself it wouldn't work!"

"Well," Beck says, tilting his head, "that's the interesting part about our new task."

"Also los! Hier ist das ideale Revier!" Beck looks at me skeptically. "How do you even come up with that?"

"Nina said there's a whole swarm of princes in here."

"Did she really?"

"Not exactly. But something like that. So, maybe she didn't say 'swarm', but there are definitely some guys around."

We're standing in the park, scanning for men. We don't quite know what we'll do when we find one, but we've decided to let the situation inspire us and then improvise. However, Beck's pessimistic attitude is really getting on my nerves today. He thinks a rainy day isn't a good time to find a man in the park.

But we have to start trying, because Carolin has been back home for three days now. She doesn't look particularly happy, but at least she's working in the workshop every day again. I take that as a sign that things are slowly getting better.

"There! I see one! Over there!"

Excitedly, I run towards the direction where I saw a pair of legs under an umbrella. After two meters, I realize Beck obviously has no intention of following me. I stop and turn to him.

"Hey, what's up? Why are you just standing there?"

"Hercules, you crazy little dachshund! That's clearly a woman!"

"How do you know that? You can only see the legs. And with this rain, I can't smell if it's a man or a woman. We have to take a look. Come on, at least make an effort!"

The hangover is really bad today. He doesn't even seem remotely guilty; he grins at me widely.

"You've got a lot to learn, my dear. Under the umbrella is definitely a woman. I don't have to run through half the park for that."

"Well, how do you know that? For someone who can't tell a plastic camera from an actual bird, you're being pretty presumptuous."

Beck completely ignores my side-comment and instead signals towards the target person with his paw.

"Take a good look. The umbrella has a very distinctive flower pattern."

Hmm, that's right; big and small flowers form neat circles.

"So, lesson number two: flowers are a woman's pattern. You don't even need to bother looking any further. I've never met a man who would go out with a flowery umbrella. So let's save our energy for the real case."

I understand people: an interesting concept, patterns for women and patterns for men. I wonder why that should be. Don't men and women recognize each other anyway? After all, their noses are almost deaf, so they might need to rely on auxiliary criteria.

We loiter around in the park for another ten minutes without incident. It's quite large, but also pretty round, so you have a good view from the center. You can see: nothing. Not a single person is out and about. I start to feel soft despite my thick fur. Maybe Beck is right, and we should head back home. Just as I'm ready to admit defeat, an intrepid two-legged creature appears. And this time, it's definitely a man - he doesn't have an umbrella in his hand but jogs quite casually from the park's right corner straight towards us.

"Huh, does he want to join us?" I wonder.

"Seems that way. He might be trying to cut through," Beck taunts. "After all, it's no fun running around in this weather."

So, really, he's just being a downer. Why did he even come along if he thinks everything is stupid? As a hunting dog, Beck would probably have been shot by his master for defeatism long ago. On the other hand, the jogger is already here. And yes, I still don't have a great plan. Frantically, I brainstorm.

The jogger almost passes us by when I suddenly and decisively leap in front of him, barking pitifully. It definitely looks like I'm in terrible pain and need urgent help. Let's see if this Mr. Animal lover will take care of me. We can figure out the prince thing later.

Two seconds later, I'm not so sure my idea was that great: The man tries to avoid me, stumbles, and falls right on Beck's nose. He stays down for a moment, then gets up, shakes himself off, and rubs his right arm. As he stands back up, he walks towards me, looks at me briefly - and lets out a roar: "Watch where you're jumping, you stupid piece of shit!"

Okay, that's clearly not a gentleman in the strictest sense. He swings his right leg for a kick, but before he can connect, Beck and I take off running and hide behind the next bush. Ouch! What a failure! Beck says nothing at first until we've both caught our breath, then he shakes his head slowly.

"Really, what was that all about? It could have gone so wrong. "

"I did at least something," I defend my unconventional approach to master-seeking.

"Ha! Pure operational haste! I wonder what would have happened if the guy had taken you out right away or landed on top of you. Then you'd be flat as a pancake. It's too silly for me, I'm leaving."

I hang my head. There's something to what Beck says. Although I didn't imagine it would be this difficult. When Nina told about looking for princes in the park, it sounded so simple. Ugh.

Apparently I look very dejected because Beck nudges me and offers some comforting words.

"Now, now - the world won't end if it doesn't work out on the first try. You underestimate the effect of rain on people. Most just don't like it very much and stay home. Look at them - no signs of movement. Apart from a few joggers, a person can easily spend several days on a sofa. Even I, as a hangover, would find that too boring! But you'll see: As soon as the sun comes out, the park will be packed again. Then we'll stroll casually from bench to bench and pick our best candidates. And then you can bring back your 'I'm such a poor, sick little dachshund' act."

I look at Beck in surprise. "Really? You didn't think that plan was bad?"

"No. Acceptable. At least for someone who came up with it as a dog."

I look up in surprise. In fact: sitting on the next park bench is a man. Although the bench must still be quite wet, he has made himself comfortable and obviously prepares a small picnic, as he already has a bottle by his side and now rummages in a bag that he brought with him. I approach the bench closer to get a better look at the man. He doesn't exactly look like a prince. Somewhat wrinkled, perhaps. His hair is gray and slightly longer, unruly strands constantly fall into his face. Plus, he has a beard that almost looks a little like a Wire-haired Dachshund's fur.

"Hmm, do you think this is the right one? I have serious doubts."

But Beck doesn't buy it. "And if so! It's at least an opportunity - shouldn't we take advantage of it? If it doesn't work out, we'll at least gain some experience. Then we'll be well-prepared for the real top candidates."

That's something I've never seen before. Cats are true strategists. And Beck has thought even more deeply.

"So, let's approach that guy. Then your 'sick dog' act will come in handy. Give it a little gas, rolling around, barking, the whole nine yards." If he pays any attention to you, try to make him understand that you want to go with us to our house.

"And how do I do that?"

"Just like you. Improvise!"

Upon reaching the bench, I look for a strategically good spot for my show. The man hasn't noticed me yet, too busy with his bag. Occasionally, he brushes his half-long gray hair out of his face and tucks it behind his ears. I lie down on my side next to his feet and turn over onto my back. Then I start whining loudly, kicking my legs and writhing around. A picture of misery and distress - if anyone doesn't react to that, they have a heart of stone and don't deserve our Caro!

In fact, the man puts down his bag and bends down towards me.

"Excuse me, what are you? And what are you doing here?"

A strong smell wafts over to me, sweat... and... yes, exactly: and the stuff that Carolin drank in that terrible night. Unpleasant memories flood my mind, and I would love to cancel this whole event. But from the corner of my eye, I can see Mr. Beck sitting just a meter to our right. It seems like he's having a great time. If he thinks I'm giving up now, he's mistaken. Woof!

I wriggle around a bit and try to add more drama to the situation by squinting and wiggling my snout.

"Oh, you poor creature, you're in bad shape! Come on, Willi will pick you up."

With these words, the man gently places his large hands under my neck and back and lifts me onto his lap. Instantly, I stop writhing. Not that I want to fall off here and hurt myself. He scratches me under the belly, which is actually quite pleasant. However, the smell up here is even stronger than Carolin's drink. Hrks, this must be something really bad. Hopefully, the man doesn't throw up right away, because I'm currently in a very unfavorable position.

"Well, little guy, you're not shaking anymore. That means it'll get better for you now, right? But what will Willi do with you?"

Indeed, that's a very good question. This is precisely the moment for Beck's intervention. I hope he doesn't mess it up and has thought about how to lure Carolin with the man. Even though I'm now completely convinced that he isn't even remotely a prince or an acceptable candidate. Come on, Beck, where are you?

"Whoa, there's another little friend! Where did all of you come from all of a sudden?"

So, communication among us four-leggers works. I quickly turn onto my stomach and see Beck stroking Willi's legs. Now he jumps onto the bench and sits right next to us.

"Meow, meowuuu, meowuuuu!"

Okay, cats can't even properly meow. To be precise, they can't meow at all. Beck sounds like one of the violins Carolin plays every day. Actually, even worse, I wonder what he's trying to achieve with that.

"Oh my, you're not doing well either. What's going on here? Are both of you sick or have you gotten lost?"

Willi scratches Beck's head with one hand and looks at him thoughtfully. Even if he isn't a prince, he's a kind person. Maybe that's enough? Beck places his paw on Willi's arm and pulls a bit.

"Ouch!"

Apparently, he uses his claws, because Willi withdraws his arm in surprise. Beck hops off the bench again and grabs one of Willi's legs. As well as possible with one paw, he pulls on the trouser leg and meows repeatedly.

"Now I wish we could talk to animals. I'd love to know what you want from me. Should I come with you?"

Enthusiastically, I immediately lick Willi's hands. They taste - well, let's just say they're peculiar.

He laughs. "Hey, my friend, you're going wild. Was that your answer to my question? You want me to come along?"

It's so easy to talk to humans! I had imagined it much more complicated. Or maybe Willi is particularly sensitive. But in the end, it doesn't matter. I jump down from his lap and approach Mr. Beck, who looks at Willi expectantly while wagging his tail excitedly. Willi stands up a bit wobbly. As he gains balance, Beck circles him twice, then runs towards Carolin's house. Following suit, I do the same.

"So, should I follow? A dachshund and a cat want to go for a walk with me. If I tell the lady from the Salvation Army this, she'll just push it off." Then let's go!

At this command, Mr. Beck and I approach the park exit directly in front of our garden. Throughout, I cast occasional glances over my shoulder - Willi follows us dutifully. Only when we reach the garden gate does he pause briefly.

"So here? It's a nice house. Not that they'll think I'm an intruder."

Ha! What a funny thought! A watchdog who brings in the intruder himself! I wonder how Willi came up with that. A moment later, we're standing in front of the workshop's patio door. I scratch on the glass, Willi stands right behind me and peeks curiously through the window, then finally knocks. Daniel opens the door a crack.

"Yes, please?"

Willi clears his throat. "Well, to put it briefly - these two here below practically dragged me here."

Daniel looks down, only now does he seem to notice us.

"Oh, Hercules and Mr. Beck - what are you doing here?"

"So, the little dachshund seemed to be having severe cramps, so much so that he collapsed on the park bench where I was sitting. Then his friend here wanted me to come with him."

Daniel looks through the door crack and raises an eyebrow, which looks quite amusing.

"Ah, yes. The cat wanted you to come with him. I understand.

"Good, I know that sounds strange, especially from someone like me. But that's how it was, so please believe me." And then they brought me here."

At that moment, Carolin appears behind Daniel. "What's going on here?"

"The... uh... gentleman here claims that Hercules and the cat brought him to us after Hercules had a fit in the park."

Carolin steps next to Daniel and opens the terrace door wide.

"Well, you two? What have you been up to? Attacking peaceful park visitors?"

She smiles encouragingly at Willi. Hm, maybe he does like her after all? Daniel, however, rolls his eyes, but Carolin can't see that. Willi can, though. He fidgets nervously with his messy hair.

"Yes, so as I told your husband - the two of them did indeed lead me here. So, first they jumped onto my bench, then the cat pulled on my pants leg, and...", Willi hesitates, suddenly feeling embarrassed about the whole situation. "I don't want to bother you any further. The dog seems to be doing better now, I'll go."

He's about to turn around when Carolin takes a step towards him in the garden direction.

"Thank you for bringing them here. It seems they wanted something from you, unfortunately they can't speak. Maybe I should take Hercules to the vet later on. Better safe than sorry."

"Absolutely," echoes Willi. "That's definitely a good idea. Have a nice day." Then he leaves.

Oh no! What a blunder! To the vet. I should have known that this whole incident would bring me back to Doctor Wagner. Disappointed, I hang my head, with Beck grinning beside me.

"Hercules, come in," Daniel finally says and gestures for me to enter. "And you, Beck, stay outside. I think you've had enough adventure for today."

Daniel seems angry. I quickly make sure to retreat into my box.

"Do you believe the story?" Carolin wants to know. "Do you really think they brought him here? Or did he just want a closer look at our back entrance to break in?"

"He didn't seem interested in houses, more like someone who'd break into the next kiosk for his booze," I say.

"But why would he make up such a story? Can you seriously imagine Hercules and Beck dragging him here? And if so, why?"

Daniel raises his hands. "Honestly, no idea! Hercules doesn't look sick to me either. Maybe Old Man Eschenbach had a few too many and imagined things."

Daniel smiles, Carolin turns red. She abruptly turns around and goes back to her room without another word. Daniel hesitates for a moment before following her.

"Hey, I'm sorry about that," he says.

Carolin doesn't answer. She's clearly angry at him, even a blind dog like me can tell. Why, though, I don't quite understand. But Daniel seems to have figured it out right away. He stands close to her and considers what to do next. Finally, he chooses the option I would've picked in his situation: physical contact. He embraces Carolin tightly.

Suddenly, a tension fills the air as if Old Man Eschenbach had cranked up the voltage on his transformer at the horse stable.

TWELVE

"He's so sweet! Honestly! I think I've finally met a great guy," Nina gushes, her eyes sparkling with joy. Her hands are talking just as much as her words. Even though our "Prince for Carolin" campaign hasn't been successful yet, at least her best friend seems to have found someone. For over an hour now, Nina has been lounging on our couch, rambling about Dr. Wagner. Brrr, the mere thought of Nina voluntarily spending time with this dog trainer is adventurous enough. A man whose hands are likely stained with blood! Maybe even mine! But it doesn't really matter to me; all that matters is Carolin's well-being.

As Nina continues her monologue in the background, my thoughts wander. If the weather improves, Beck and I will definitely pick up where we left off. You can't call our attempt with Willi a complete failure. Beck was right - it was a great exercise, and we've made significant progress. Still, I'm not sure why Carolin thought Willi might be an intruder. Was it his peculiar smell? But Carolin wouldn't have noticed that, her nose is too bad for such things. Plus, Willi never stood right next to her. There's something Beck and I need to work on there. I'll go look for him in the garden. Assuming Nina comes to a conclusion soon, and I can talk to Carolin again. Unfortunately, that doesn't seem likely at the moment; Nina is still babbling away.

"And his looks! Just gorgeous! Such a great figure! And then those full brown hair! A little like Hugh Grant," she gushes.

I perk up my ears. Maybe this conversation isn't as boring as it seems. Dr. Wagner is good-looking, that much is clear. But for dogs, such matters are rather obscure: When does a human look good to others? Does he need lots of hair or less? Is height important - or perhaps the opposite? Do men and women have different standards? Ugh, if I really think about it, I've taken on quite a challenge with this "Partner Matching" project. Which makes Nina's current tale all the more interesting.

"You didn't notice his incredibly blue eyes?" she exclaims.

Aha! The eye color. Apparently, that's an important factor.

"Nina, I don't think you remember - but when we were at the clinic, Hercules was in a very bad mood, constantly barking and then trying to kill two cute rabbits, causing complete chaos. Sorry I didn't get a chance to look into Dr. Wagner's deep blue eyes."

Carolin sounds slightly annoyed. Is it because of her unpleasant memory from the vet visit? Because I behaved badly there? Embarrassing.

"Oh, come on, you're in a bad mood. I thought you'd be happy that your best friend had a date," she chides.

"Sorry, I'm not in the best mood. I promise to improve. So, apart from those gorgeous blue eyes: How does he kiss?"

That seems to be an important question; otherwise, Carolin wouldn't have asked it as the first thing. But how do we even find out a man's kissing skills? Beck and I need to figure this one out.

Nina giggles. "You won't believe this, but I don't know. We've been talking for hours, but we haven't kissed yet."

Got it. It's not something you can see in people. You have to experience it yourself.

"You and your boyfriend haven't kissed? But you spent the whole evening together?"

"We did, and we had a great conversation. Marc was charming and funny. But we didn't kiss. That's okay though. I want to take things slow."

"Hm," Carolin says, sounding skeptical. "You? That's a completely new tactic. When did you start using it?" In his practice, nothing like this had been noticed. It seemed more like... I mean, you were already in his arms.

"Slow down and make sure of the situation first?" Nina snorts indignantly. "Are you saying I just tripped over your stupid dog? And what do you mean by new tactic? That sounds like I always take them down with a shotgun."

"Well, to be fair, you've never been exactly delicate when it comes to someone you like. More of a shotgun kind of girl. "

"Carolin, it's clear you haven't been out there for a while. I'm young, single. If I like a guy, I don't wait around for him to ride up on a white horse; I take matters into my own hands. Call it the Shotgun Principle - but what's wrong with that?"

Market? Horse? Shotgun? Nina speaks in riddles. Carolin, however, seems to understand what she's talking about.

"Fine, fine. You're right, I haven't been out for a while. I need to get used to things again."

Nina nods vigorously. "And let me tell you - don't hold your breath waiting for the perfect guy to just show up! That only happens in fairy tales."

"Wrong, my dear! It does happen when Carl-Leopold von Eschersbach takes personal interest. Then you don't even have to deal with a vet." So, ladies, let's wrap this up so I can share my latest insights with Mr. Beck!

"Say, do you think Hercules needs to go?" Nina looks at me thoughtfully.

"Why? "

"He's been pacing around here all of a sudden. Not that he'd actually pee under your sofa."

"I took him for a walk before you arrived. But maybe we're just boring him. I can let him out in the garden; I have to go back to the workshop anyway."

"Too bad, I thought you'd take some time off and get together for a cup of coffee. "

"I'd love to - but I've been neglecting Daniel lately. I feel terrible about it. "

"Come on. You weren't in the best shape, and Daniel was happy to help." Nina grins at Carolin and pats her shoulder.

"You don't have to smile like that. Of course, Daniel would be happy to help - but I don't want to overdo it. He has enough on his plate as it is. With Aurora's violin reconstruction, for instance. That'll take forever."

"I'm about to cry. To be honest, I'd much rather Daniel took care of you than some stupid violin from a even stupider violinist."

"Aurora is an excellent musician."

"She's a dumb cow who constantly flirts with your boyfriend," Nina insists.

"Daniel is just my business partner. He can flirt with whoever he wants in his free time."

Nina rolls her eyes and sighs. "Fine, fine, believe what you want. Daniel and I are friends, nothing more. Hercules, let's go!"

"Our ideal candidate has blue eyes and knows how to kiss well." I sit next to Mr. Beck in the sun and report on my latest insights regarding people and partner selection.

"Then it's a done deal. It'll be easy. I'll take a closer look at his eyes, and you kiss him. Though I hope you can judge if he's any good at kissing."

I shoot Beck a bad look. "Why do I get the feeling that you're not taking me seriously? "

"Because it is. Your babbling isn't getting us anywhere. Plus, we don't even know if Carolin likes blue eyes. She might prefer brown or green? And then there's the fact that a human eye isn't exactly huge. From our perspective, we'd barely be able to tell a man's eye color from five meters away. And as for kissing... well, we're not quite there yet. If we get a guy close enough to Carolin that she shakes his hand, then we'll be doing pretty well."

"So, if I am indeed this stupid little dog, I suggest you take over here and let me follow along." "Fine, the cat will have its way."

"Don't get so upset! Our plan is good. We go back to the park, then we pull off the same trick as with Willy. Eventually, the right guy will show up. Shotgun Principle."

"Weird, that's what Nina just said too."

"See? And she knows her stuff. Since I've been living in this house, I've seen Nina bring home at least five or six different men. We'll do the same - drag as many guys as possible. Like Nina does. Believe me, she's an expert."

I look at him skeptically. "Oh really? And why hasn't she kept any of them then? Her selection process doesn't seem to be working too well. Now she even comes with this horrible vet."

"Who knows? Maybe she doesn't want to keep them around forever? Maybe they just... wear out after a while, and then a new one is needed."

"That's the first time I've heard such things about human relationships. I thought the concept was eternal fidelity. That's why Carolin got so worked up over her underwear incident, right?"

Mr. Beck shakes his head. "Hmm, you're right again. Yes, who knows? I'm just an old cat trying to understand humans." He pauses, then adds, "So, what now? Go to the park?"

"Fine."

Action Shotgun can begin.

The weather is beautiful today, so there are more people in the park than we can comfortably take in. We decide to try the benches near our house first; that way, we don't have to drag guys across a field if they actually follow us. Unfortunately, the two closest benches are either occupied by women or Willy with his plastic bags. He waves at us cheerfully, however. No point in trying them then.

Finally, on the third bench, we find our man: A young guy is sitting there tying his shoelaces. I can't tell what color his eyes are, but we had planned to overlook that anyway. Mr. Beck and I edge closer, then start our performance.

That is, we want to start. But before I even get going properly, a young woman approaches the bench, leans down, kisses him, then sits next to him. I scramble back up, shake myself off, and sit down next to Beck.

"Darn it, he already has a girlfriend."

Beck chuckles. "But we could ask if the guy is any good at kissing. That was one of your new criteria, remember?"

"Ha ha! Very funny."

I'm a bit disappointed because the guy looked really nice for my taste. The last thing I need right now is some snide comments from an overweight cat.

"How about you pick the next guy?" I suggest, sounding a bit more testy than I intended to admit.

"Of course, my dear, of course. I've already spotted one - look over there!"

He runs a bit along the meadow, then stops in front of a bench next to a beautiful flower bed. Okay, I have to admit, the man doesn't look half bad either. He's reading a newspaper, which seems to be a sign of some education anyway, and it also allows us to sneak right up to his feet unnoticed. So I roll back onto my stomach and start barking. And not just any barking, but wailing pitifully. To the point that foam starts forming at the corner of my mouth. Despite this, the man only looks at me with boredom and moves his feet a bit to the side before getting up. Then he turns around and walks away. I am speechless. Unbelievable! Beck comes running over.

"Hey, what was that? Did he just take off? And left you to your sad fate? It's incredible how heartless these people can be sometimes!"

We watch the man. Now he stands still and looks in our direction. Perhaps guilt finally seizes him? He scratches his pocket. Maybe some sausage meat? No, he takes out his phone and starts dialling. I sneak a bit closer to him because my intuition tells me it's about me now.

"Hello, police? Yes, Diekamp here. Well, you might be surprised, but I want to report a suspected case of rabies. In Hamburg." He pauses. "Yes, yes. Right, here in Hamburg. Okay, connect me."

Another pause, the man stands there intently listening into his phone. 
"Good day, Diekamp speaking. I've already told my colleague about it - I want to inform you about a suspected case of rabies. In a dachshund. In Hamburg. Right... mhm... "

The man walks back and forth and stares at us. When he sees that I've come closer, he takes a few steps back. 
"Well, how does it manifest? I'd say sudden, uninhibited attachment, cramps, almost some foam in the mouth. Hmm... Hamburg is not a rabies area? Is no part of Germany a rabies area? I understand - but maybe they should still send someone by?"

Now Mr Beck stands next to me. 
"Tell me, what's that guy so excitedly talking to?" he wants to know.

"I think he's talking to the police," I reply. "He told them I have rabies. I'm a damn good actor - if people even think I'm rabid! You know, normally only foxes get it. Opili told me that. It's very dangerous, and a good hunter must always be very careful not to be bitten. A good hunting dog of course also has to watch out."

My voice has taken on a slightly smug tone, but that's okay, after all I do know a lot about hunting, at least theoretically.

"What?!" Mr Beck shakes his head and laughs. 

"Yeah, strange, isn't it? And now he wants them to send someone especially."

Beck stops laughing. "Honestly? Ugh. We should leave here right away then."

"Why? This is finally getting interesting. Apparently I'm not just a number to him, and maybe he wants the police to find out where I live."

I would say Mr Beck is simply jealous of my acting talent and the effect I have on him. 
"Nonsense, you silly dog. What do they do with an animal that might be suffering from a very dangerous disease like rabies? They don't bring it home, they put it down! Maybe even sedate it right away!"

"Put it down?" I echo uncertainly. 

"Exactly. Put it to sleep."

I hear a startled whimper and realise that I am the one making these strange sounds. Mr Beck looks at me intently. 
"Exactly, my friend. You heard it right. And if you ask me, we have exactly one option left."

We both shout "Run!" simultaneously and sprint towards the park exit, past Willi who is sitting on his usual bench, staring at us in surprise. 
When we arrive home, I am drenched in sweat. Not from exertion, but fear. What a terrible word! We slip through the garden gate and lie down in the shadow of the big tree. We remain silent for a while, exhausted. Then I sit up. 
"I don't know, Mr Beck. My plan was not so great after all. Or at least not in this form. Not suitable for man-hunting."

Beck shakes his head back and forth. "Don't give up so quickly. The basic idea is right. We just need to narrow down our selection a bit more. So, not every man sitting in the park on a nice day is automatically a candidate."

"But that was our approach... and by the way, your suggestion. I say shotgun."

"Well? What does my yesterday's rambling have to do with it? You can be smarter. I think our success will depend on targeted pre-selection. And then we strike!"

"I don't know," I mumble, "how are we supposed to do that with pre-selection?"

Beck ponders - but only for a short moment. 
"We have to see the men through Caroline's eyes."

"Great, how am I supposed to do that?!"

Instead of answering, Beck jumps up. 
"Come on!" he calls over my shoulder. "I just had an inspiration."

"Yes, that's definitely clear. But I can't blame him. The weather is really nice, actually too beautiful to spend the whole day in the workshop. I'll go for a walk with him right now. It's better for my mood anyway."

Daniel nods. "You're right. What do you say if I accompany you?"

"Oh no! That doesn't suit me at all. After all, I'm doing all this just to find a new man. With emphasis on 'new'. It would be more of an obstacle to bring along a man already."

But nobody asks me, and so it happens that Carolin finally hooks the leash to my collar, but Daniel also reaches for his jacket, and we leave the workshop together, three of us. In the garden, we pass Mr Beck who had already positioned himself for shade.

"Hey, what's this? Is he coming along too? You don't take beer into a pub!" he whispers to me.

"Do you think that was my idea?" I whisper back. "But tell me, how could I have prevented it?"

Beck shrugs and seems to want to say something else, but we've already passed him by.

As we walk towards the park, I wonder if my plan can still be implemented under these circumstances. Will Carolin notice other people if she's constantly talking to Daniel? It's so frustrating - I can't pretend to be a sick dog because Carolin is here, and keeping an eye on Carolin doesn't help because of Daniel. Grrr!

"It's strange - I work right next to such a beautiful park, and yet I'm hardly ever here."

Exactly, Daniel, I want to say, and why exactly today?

"Yes, you don't do it often enough. Although I must say that with Hercules, I'm out in nature much more than before. But lately, I've been neglecting him. I feel guilty about it, and just now he even came with his leash to remind me of my 'lady duties'." She bends down to stroke my head. "Come on, Hercules, you haven't made it easy for your lady either. But things will definitely get better, you'll see. I hope you don't miss the animal shelter!"

Me? Longing for the animal shelter? What an absurd thought! Even though the search for a master isn't going well - Carolin doesn't seem to realize how uncomfortable it can be in ten square meters with guys like Bozo and Boxer,

Because the weather is really nice and we've already got Daniel along, I decide to simply enjoy this walk as it is. In fact, Carolin hasn't been out with me much lately. Not at all, actually. We trot along one of the winding gravel paths, and I sniff each tree that stands by the side of the path in pure enjoyment. Wonderful! Important dogs have already passed here, I can smell them precisely. And thanks to my training in the garden, I can now add my scent mark skillfully. Which I do a lot. Carolin and Daniel stroll rather than really walk, so I have plenty of time for the important things in a dog's life.

Now, however, they're even going too slowly for my taste. Probably because they're so engrossed in their conversation. Annoying. People just need to talk all the time. I tug at the leash a bit. "Come on, keep moving!" I've already sprayed every bush here!

But Carolin and Daniel don't notice me. Instead, they steer towards the next park bench and sit down. Well, that's it! But then Carolin could at least take off my leash, then I'd go explore alone. So I jump up onto the bench and rest my head on Carolin's lap. I've neglected Hercules so much and need to change that urgently.

Hmm, am I imagining things or is there again this strange tension in the air? Daniel seems nervous, and Carolin looks excited too. It wouldn't be surprising, because if I hadn't been out for so long like them, I'd hardly be able to keep myself from getting restless. In a way, humans are just big animals. They don't want to admit it. I sniff Carolin's hands and try to lick them a bit. Maybe that will calm her down.

Before I can even get my tongue around one of Carolin's handrubs, Daniel's hand suddenly lands on my nose. "Hey - what's this?" My nose is sensitive, so I growl briefly. Daniel quickly withdraws his hand. Obviously he startled me. But I startled him too. What does he want with my nose? I blink up at him, but he acts as if nothing happened. Strange. A few minutes pass in silence; neither Carolin nor Daniel says a word. Actually, it's very nice. Then Daniel clears his throat.

"Say, what do you think about doing something together this week?"

What on earth is he talking about? The two of them do something together every day! Carolin obviously thinks the same. She giggles.

"And what had you in mind? A cello or a violin?"

"Ha, ha, very funny."

"Come on, a little revenge for your cognac comment from the other day."

"Okay, then we're even." No silence.

"Cooking," Carolin says, "we could cook something together. Like in our WG in Mittenwald. We haven't done that in ages and it was always so much fun."

She smiles at Daniel. That's exactly the smile that caught my eye in the animal shelter. Unmistakeable and beautiful. All of a sudden, it's much warmer on our bench. Wonderful! I snuggle up close to Carolin and enjoy the moment.

This time, I see Daniel's hand coming for my nose just in time, and I duck away. He's crazy! But actually, he doesn't seem to be targeting me at all, but Carolin. Because over my back, he reaches for her hand and pulls it towards him. Carolin looks surprised, but she doesn't pull her hand back. What does that mean? When does a man hold a woman's hand? Too bad Beck isn't here; he would definitely know. I decide this can only be a good sign - people are usually so stingy with physical contact. I'm curious to see what happens next.

"Hey, you!" a harsh, unfriendly voice booms out suddenly.

Carolin and Daniel jump in surprise, and he lets go of her hand.

"Yes, exactly you!" the voice continues.

Now we also see the owner of the unfriendly voice: He stands right in front of our bench. I bark briefly - doesn't he realize how rude he's being? But he just stands frozen like a statue and stares at Carolin and Daniel. Or is he staring at me? A bad feeling creeps up on me.

"Excuse me for interrupting you like this," Holger Diekamp, he introduces himself, "but the dog you have on your lap behaved very strangely here yesterday - I'm afraid he might be suffering from some evil disease. Frankly, I don't rule out rabies, even though the police dismissed it yesterday."

"The police?" Carolin and Daniel echo in unison.

"Yes, I immediately informed the police. But before they could arrive, the dog was already gone. Together with the fat tomcat who had been by his side the whole time. I was alarmed and then decided to keep looking for the dog. After all, rabies is a deadly disease. You might think that's excessive worry, but that little guy was writhing in convulsions and had foam at the mouth. Plus, he seemed attached, or even overly so — all classic signs. Have you been with the animal in North Africa recently?"

I realize I'm slowly turning to stone from fear — what if I still have to go to the police? And they really want to put me to sleep? No, and all over our stupid plan! I try to make myself as small as possible and squeeze tightly between Daniel and Carolin. Hunched closely against Daniel's thigh, I feel him start shaking. How terrible! Apparently he's terrified of me — my fate is sealed, the two will soon hand me over to the police. I lower my head and begin to bark.

"Rabies!" Daniel gasps, shuddering even more. "That's just the silliest thing I've ever heard."

Phew! Daniel isn't shaking from fear but laughing! Out of relief, I spontaneously jump onto his lap and lick his face.

"Whoa, Hercules! You see, Mr. Diekamp — this little guy is completely alert. Whatever he had yesterday wasn't rabies for sure. Maybe you mistook him for another dog."

Diekamp glares at me.

"No, there was no mix-up. But if you want to put your own health in danger with your carelessness, be my guest!" Diekamp lets out a furious snort and turns on his heel, leaving towards the park exit.

Daniel shakes his head. "Seriously, these people are crazy. Rabies — such nonsense."

"Well, I am starting to worry a bit," I say.

"Why? Hercules is definitely healthy. Just look at him. He has nothing missing."

"Yes, but do you remember last week? That beggar? He also said Hercules acted strangely. Maybe he's sick."

Carolyn sounds quite worried. Ugh — what have I started!

"I mean, seizures, foam at the mouth... I once read that dogs can also have epileptic fits. And if you know about it, you can treat them, just like with humans."

Daniel sighs. "Alright then, let's go see your vet if you feel better. It's a nice walk in this weather — so either way, a good idea."

Carolyn nods. "I'll call Dr. Wagner right away."

"To cut to the chase, Mrs. Neumann: At first glance, Hercules is in excellent condition. Clear eyes, a cold nose, good muscle tone — I'd like to rule out an infection."

I sit again on the cold metal table in Wagner's examination room and let him go to work on me. I feel so small that I don't even take the opportunity to check Dr. Wagner's blue eyes. But what can I say? It's my fault. Of course, Mr. Beck would also get his fair share if justice ruled the world. But that's not how things are. Wagner runs his hand over my head and then turns back to Carolyn and Daniel, who stand by the table, attentively watching the whole procedure.

"As for epilepsy," he continues, "it does exist in dogs. Unfortunately, it's not easy to diagnose. However, it's often hereditary. Do you know if there have been any cases of epilepsy in Hercules' family?"

Carolyn shrugs. "No, sorry. I got Hercules from the shelter. I don't actually know anything about his family, but he's said to come from a very meticulous breeder. He's not purebred, that's why he was given away."

Dr. Wagner contemplates me thoughtfully. "Hmm, a meticulous breeder... So I immediately had a feeling I've seen Hercules somewhere before. Must have been with one of the Dachshund breeds — and I know them all pretty well. Maybe at the old Escherbach place?"

ESCHERBACH!!! Just saying that name makes me nearly jump off the table. I spring up and start barking loudly. Exactly! That's me! A real Escherbach! And what a one!

"Whoa!" Wagner exclaims, gently pushing me back into the center of the table. "But you're excited! You know the name well, little guy. I'd say: Bingo!"

I have no idea what role Bingo plays in all this, but for the first time since our initial encounter, Dr. Wagner seems almost sympathetic to me. Finally, a person who recognizes my true origins.

"My father took care of the Escherbach breed. However, I've only been there a few times myself; the dogs are healthy, thank God. But I can ask Mr. Escherbach if he's ever had a problem with epilepsy in his dogs. The diagnosis is quite complex and expensive, so we should have a pretty good suspicion before making any assumptions. Perhaps these seizures have another cause."

"Well," Dr. Wagner looks questioningly at Daniel.

He shakes his head. "Me neither."

"Hmm," Dr. Wagner says. He turns to me again and scratches under my chin. "So, you're attached and peculiar? Have Hercules experienced anything traumatic recently that might have strongly affected him?"

Carolyn looks down sheepishly. "I was a bit sick lately." She whispers it.

"Right," Dr. Wagner says. "I remember Mrs. Bogner mentioning it when she brought Hercules in for his follow-up check."

"Oh, did she? I had no idea," Carolyn blushes.

Caught red-handed! I'm reluctantly admitting my guilt.

Carolin glares at Daniel with a wicked gleam in her eye. "I highly doubt there's any connection here."

"Come on, Carolin, it was just a joke," he retorts.

Then I feel relieved! It would be very embarrassing for me to get caught like this. Carolin wouldn't approve.

"Well," Dr. Wagner interjects, "I don't find the idea that absurd. Dogs do develop a strong protective instinct for their social pack. So if there's any suspicion in that direction, I'd definitely look into it." What exactly happened to you?

"I don't think that'll help much," Carolin dismisses him with a snippy tone. "Everything's fine on my end. But ask the breeder—that seems more promising."

"So what kind of audacity is this, questioning me like this?" Carolin fums, as we make our way back home. "This Wagner guy is impossible! I wonder what Nina sees in him."

I trot beside her and Daniel, hanging on every word with eager anticipation. After all, it also concerns me.

"Calm down, he didn't mean to offend you. He just wanted to make a well-informed diagnosis. He can't know that you're so sensitive about this."

"I'm not sensitive!" Carolin protests indignantly.

"Well, maybe a little," Daniel counters.

"And if so, isn't that amazing? Imagine my vet suspecting Hercules' behavioral issues because I'm such a challenging case."

"Now hold on, nobody claimed that. And anyway, it's completely far-fetched."

"Oh really?" Carolin turns to Daniel with a smirk. "I hope you're considering Hercules as your savior before he drags in some random guys."

Even Carolin can't help but laugh. "Yeah, I guess so!"

So that's how it is. Maybe Daniel isn't such a bad match for Carolin after all. I need to talk to Beck urgently. Our plan might require significant adjustments. No, our plan is likely obsolete soon.

"I think we don't need to look any further: We've found our man!"

The next day, with an important expression, I announce my new insight regarding Carolin's partner choice to Mr. Beck under our tree in the garden, enjoying the warm afternoon sun.

"How did you come to that conclusion? First, you were with Daniel in the park, then at the vet's, and today Carolin's out all day without you—how could you have found her prince?"

"Quite simply: We've always had the prince by our side."

"Huh? I don't get it."

"Daniel. I believe Daniel is the right one."

"Come on, I already explained that to you: Daniel doesn't work out because of his over-friendliness in a particularly severe case, plus good naturedness as a penalty. "

Mr. Beck, the lawyer. If he's like this, I don't really care for him much.

"Have you ever considered that your theory might be wrong? I observed them closely: First, there's a tension in the air when they're together. It's hard to describe, but it's definitely there, even if you can't see it. Like electricity on a fence."

Unimpressed, Mr. Beck stretches and relaxes. "Electricity on a fence? You're really country, my friend. I don't know what you mean at all."

Ignoring his objection, I present my additional evidence: "And second, Daniel held Carolin's hand—even over my back. "

"So what? They've known each other for ages. It means nothing."

"And third, Carolin herself said she'd like to have Daniel as her savior."

Alright, cat, your turn!

"You still have a lot to learn, my canine friend. What people say and what they actually think and do are two completely different things. Completely. Sometimes I even think humans waste their linguistic abilities since they never use them for anything meaningful. Honestly, if humans couldn't talk to each other, nothing would really change. They don't tell the truth to each other anyway."

"Nonsense. I think you just want to be right."

"I'm not trying to be right—I am right."

Ugh, he's being so stubborn today. I sigh and say no more. It doesn't matter what this cat thinks. The main thing is that Carolin gets back on track, and we're soon a happy family again with the master, mistress, and dog. Beck and I remain silent for a while before I decide to head back to the workshop. Although Carolin is out on some appointment, maybe I can get a few pats from Daniel.

I arrive just in time to witness Aurora's grand performance. With sweeping arm gestures, she recounts her latest concert. Clearly a huge success, she leaves no doubt about it. According to my understanding of human upbringing, excessive self-praise is impolite. The old Count von Eschersbach would have given Aurora a stern look for this behavior. He was known for his popular advice to all those who came and went at the castle: "Don't boast." However, of course, the count is much older than Aurora, so his views might be slightly outdated. Or perhaps the rule about boasting doesn't apply to artists directly.

"Enthusiastic—the people were just thrilled, Daniel," she gushes. "But I really outdid myself that night too. By the way, it's a shame you weren't there."

"Yeah, sorry. I'll definitely come next time—at least when your new violin is finished, promised."

Aurora furrows her brow, which looks very intriguing. "Well, I get the distinct feeling you're only coming for the violin and not because of me."

"Please, that's ridiculous! You know how much I enjoy listening to you play."

"And yet, lately, we've been having trouble getting a date," she teases.

"With all due respect, that's none of your business."

Daniel sounds almost angry now. Good!

"But I have a right to know! Since Carolin ditched Thomas, it's been chaos between you two. You're hardly ever available, and scheduling appointments is next to impossible... Nothing against love, but you run a workshop, not a rehab center for broken hearts."

"Aurora, we've known each other for five years. Have I ever delivered subpar work? "

"No, that wasn't what I meant..."

"Then tell me, has Carolin ever delivered subpar work?"

"You want me to say she feels neglected? That's exactly what I'm trying to convey."

"Daniel!" Aurora's voice cracks with sadness. "Don't get mad. I'm just disappointed that we don't have as much contact these days—that's all. I thought you cared about my art."

Oh, if only I could roll my eyes! Even without Mr. Beck's professional assessment, it's clear to me that Aurora doesn't lack interest in Daniel's artistry. But Daniel seems determined to ignore this comment, shifting gears towards a more conciliatory tone instead.

"Let's not argue. I promise solemnly to attend your next concert—with or without the new violin."

Aurora looks at him like a child and smiles, "Hm, do you think men would find that cute?" As a dachshund, I find it rather silly.

"Thank you so much, Daniel! That means a lot! And if this doesn't work out... In the fall, I'm looking for someone to accompany me on my next concert tour. It's going to Italy, and I'd like to take a look at some violins they've offered me there. What do you think?"

"Well, you just bought a masterpiece. Plus, I can't leave the workshop alone for too long."

"I see you're not ready to commit yet. But I'm not refusing either. I'll wait a bit longer." She pats his arm. "Anyway, I have to go. I have a lot on my plate."

She turns around and steps on the tip of my tail. It's probably not malicious. It doesn't hurt much, but she didn't catch me hard enough. This opportunity might not come again soon, so I whine pitifully and snap briefly but firmly. Zzzz. Grrr. Wonderful!

Aurora lets out a sharp scream and jumps into the air. "Ouch! Damn - are you pulling my leg?"

She stares at me, and I try to look as innocent as possible while whining prophylactically. Aurora rubs her thigh; you can clearly see the imprint of my teeth.

Daniel remains unmoved. "Well, it seems you've climbed onto his tail pretty hard. He's usually quite gentle."

Aurora gasps, trying to say something bad but changing her mind.

"A real sweetheart, for sure. Greet Carolin, see you soon."

Then she rushes out. Daniel looks at me. He bends down and strokes my head.

"Good job, fella."

"How do I look, Hercules?"

Carolin is wearing a knee-length flowery dress and spins in front of me. Very pretty, I must say. Plus, I'm naturally happy that she's chosen a floral pattern for the joint cooking evening with Daniel. If Mr. Beck is right, it's definitely a feminine pattern. I combine: Carolin wants to look like a woman. A good sign! I sit in front of her and wag my tail.

"So, you like it? Great, I'll keep it on." And he tucks his hair up into a bun. I grumble briefly. It's much prettier loose - that way, you can see the hair better, and no dog would ever think of hiding its beautiful fur. Especially not mine, which shines silky smooth like Carolin's hair. As a dachshund pup, I can only be envious. In this respect, Carolin is a true setter, or rather: a Golden Retriever. She lets her hair down again.

"I see that makes more sense. It looks better, but it's not very practical for cooking."

I tilt my head. No, loose is much prettier!

"Alright then, how about a compromise: I'll put it up while we cook, and then take it down again? Exactly. Good idea. Thanks, Hercules!"

You're welcome. I'm happy to help.

Cheerfully, Carolin goes around the apartment, tidying things up. She sets the table, opens another of those disgusting bottles, and pours its contents with a flourish into a larger, rounder bottle. A splash of red liquid lands in the container. It looks pretty, but I have no idea what it's for. However, I've seen this ritual - or magic? - at least once before with old Mr. Eschersbach. Maybe it's for a successful evening? As a dachshund, I'm not overly superstitious, but if it helps today... It would be too nice to see the look on Beck's face if I could tell him that Carolin and Daniel have become a couple.

The doorbell rings. That must be Daniel. I'm so excited! Carolin obviously is too, because she rushes to the door, then makes a sudden stop in front of a mirror next to it and critically checks herself before opening it. Expectantly, I follow her heels and form a supportive welcoming committee. Carolin flings open the door; I make a little bow - it's Nina. Ugh! What does she want here?

"What do you want?"

"I'd call that a warm welcome! Thanks, I'm doing fine, and I'd love to come in."

"Well, actually, this isn't the best time for me."

"Oh, so Daniel is really not your type after all?"

"Hmm... Let me put it this way: I don't think he's my cup of tea. What's going on here?"

"I guess you'll never know unless I tell you. Can I have a glass? That bottle over there with the bulging belly? I need something to drink after yesterday's fiasco."

"Of course. It's a fine wine, by the way. Is there anything special to celebrate? Did you manage to bid on that million-euro violin for Aurora?"

"No, I just wanted to cook and have a glass of wine with Daniel. Carolin, look who's here!" She winks at Nina, unaware that Carolin wants to say something but is held back in the moment.

"Well, you're lucky I decided to drop by spontaneously; otherwise, we'd only be talking about work all evening."

I notice Carolin wanting to say something, but Daniel rings the doorbell again.

"Wow, Carolin, you look gorgeous!" He kisses her on both cheeks. This is a first for me - I never saw this between them before. Then he sees Nina and looks disappointed, which I know from experience is only a facade. Nina waves cheerfully at him.

"Yes, I decided to join the party. While I'd rather have had a women's evening, you can stay if you want. " She laughs.

Daniel forces a smile. "Given that I'm following an invitation instead of giving it, I'll say that's very generous of you."

"Please do."

Nina beams at Daniel. Evidently, she won't be pulled on the leash. "What are we cooking?"

She heads towards the kitchen, Carolin looks at Daniel and shrugs. Then both follow Nina. In front of the fridge, they stop.

"I've prepared everything for a Coq au vin. We just need to chop some potatoes while the chicken roasts in the oven." So then, sit down, I'll give you knives.

It's happening as expected: After half an hour, Nina is still there. No wonder, the chicken now exudes a rather seductive scent. She clearly wants a bite, and I can't even blame her. Me too, I'm already speculating about a small snack. To improve my starting position, I slide next to Carolin, who's now sitting on the kitchen bench, rest my head on her lap, and look at her as heart-wrenching as possible. Unfortunately, with little success, because Carolin, Nina, and Daniel are so engrossed in their conversation that they don't even notice me.

"So, and Dr. Wagner isn't really the dream guy?!"

How can you constantly talk about a veterinarian? And then with this diagnosis? With all due respect, it was probably already decided beforehand. Well, Nina, if only you had asked me, I would have told you right away. Nina pours herself another glass.

"We've been on three dates now. It's always nice and funny - but nothing else happens. And today, it wasn't even particularly nice because our beach trip was unfortunately boycotted by several large families with their unruly children." Annoying, that.

"Well, you know, I'm a known friend of kids. Then it must have been really bad."

Am I imagining things or is Daniel making fun of Nina? It's probably the latter, as she reacts very irritably.

"Well then? Not everyone dreams of a bunch of kids. Just because I'm a woman, I don't want to be a mother."

Daniel raises his hands in a calming gesture. "Alright. No kids then."

"Well, at least nothing happens between Marc and me. And that's just not worth it. I'm looking for a lover, not a buddy."

Maybe Marc is gay?

Gay? What does that even mean? A shy guy?

Daniel grinses. "Not every man who doesn't want to go out with you has to be gay. See me, I'm living proof."

Nina looks at him angrily. Hm, it must have a different meaning than just being shy.

"Thanks for the flowers. And don't worry, I won't approach you inappropriately."

"Well then, that's settled," Carolin cheerfully exclaims. "Let's eat something."

An excellent idea. Quickly, I set my most loyal 'Dackelblick' (a term meaning a determined and intense gaze) again. And this time, Carolin reacts.

"I guess Hercules would also like a small appetizer. If I had known we were four, I would have bought more."

"Listen, you can't compare me to a dog, right? Plus, no one told me tonight was going to be a cooking event. Or... wait... did you want to be alone?"

I want to shout, but neither Carolin nor Daniel respond. Instead, Carolin takes the roasting pan out of the oven. A warm cloud of chicken dream floats towards me. Mmm, delicious! I lick my lips. Nina looks at me thoughtfully. "You're right!" she says. "Is this even suitable for a dog? After all, there's alcohol in it."

Well, that's just too much! Inviting yourself first and then disputing my portion? Rude! I growl at her.

"Hey, come on! I just don't want you to have a hangover tomorrow."

How does Mr. Beck even relate to the chicken now? Let me put it this way: In my opinion, too much is being talked about tonight. And that's all Nina's fault. It looked so good at first - without that stupid cow, Daniel would probably have grabbed Carolin's hand again, maybe they would have even kissed. I decide to intervene in the further course of the evening. But only after I finally get something to eat!

Daniel, the old dog lover, actually prepares a small plate for me. Finest chicken meat, without bones or tendons. It smells heavenly, but also a bit unfamiliar. That must be due to the red liquid Carolin not only poured into the other bottle but also generously into the roasting pan. Is that alcohol? And why would that be harmful? Or is that exactly what got Carolin into the hospital? Oh well, appetite wins over mistrust, and after the first bite, I'm captivated by how delicious it tastes. I have to fight hard not to gobble everything down. Unfortunately, the dream ends after five bites as I lick my plate clean to avoid wasting any of the delicious sauce.

The others also taste it just as well, so a second helping is out of the question. But that's okay because now the action starts. I dash from the kitchen to the wardrobe and grab my leash. With this in my mouth, I return and make a fool of myself in front of Nina. She looks at me in surprise. Freedom for Carolin and Daniel!

"Do you want to go for a walk with me?"

Of course! To confirm, I jump up and down.

"Oh no, I'm just sitting here nicely. Ask your mom - or better yet, ask Uncle Daniel." She grins at Daniel.

"I see, you want to have some hardcore woman's talk. Well then, Herkules, let's take a walk around the block."

No! Absolutely not! That's exactly the opposite of what I wanted! Quickly, I drop the leash and run out of the kitchen. Unfortunately, Daniel misinterprets this completely and follows me with the leash towards the apartment door. I growl briefly but it doesn't help: Daniel puts on his jacket, then he leashes me up, and two minutes later we're standing on the sidewalk in front of the house. Wordlessly, we start walking. When we reach the park, Daniel clears his throat.

"Probably it's weird to talk with a dog about this - but since there's no one else around, I need to vent my frustration. Because if I'm honest, I had a different evening in mind. More romantic. Intimater. And above all: solitary. What on earth was Nina thinking when she showed up at our date? Can you tell me that, Herkules?"

I shake my head and hope Daniel recognizes this impressive human-dog communication.

"Well, I don't know - I thought there might be something more between Carolin and me. But obviously, she was happy about Nina's spontaneous visit tonight."

I bark briefly.

"Or not? But why didn't she say anything?"

Dejected, I hang my ears down. That, my dear Daniel, I honestly don't know. I also think she should have sent Nina away. We walk side by side in silence.

"But it's probably my own fault. I need to show clearer that Carolin means more to me than just a colleague and friend. Otherwise, she'll only ever see me as nice Daniel. I need to act finally."

A good idea! I'd definitely be on board for that and jump up at Daniel's leg in support.

"You think so too?" He looks around, then laughs. "Would anyone watch a man having a conversation with a dog? And would they commit me because of it? It would look pretty crazy."

Whatever. We finish our walk, then I'll put my new plan into action.

Back in the apartment, Nina has finally left. Carolin cleans up the kitchen and greets us cheerfully.

"You're back! Did you enjoy your walk, Hercules? It must have been a boring evening for you - too much talking, right? But I'm glad my chicken tasted good to you."

"It did to me as well. Thanks again for the invitation. Would you like another glass of wine?"

"Sure, why not. I am quite tired though. I won't be staying up late tonight."

She takes two new glasses from the kitchen cabinet and places them next to the bottle still on the dining table. Daniel pours one and hands it to Carolin.

"Here you go. Cheers to our duo cooking evening!"

They laugh.

"Hmm, it seems Nina was determined to ignore all our clues. But she got so caught up in this veterinarian story that she needed emotional support today. I'm sorry, I had envisioned the evening differently."

"No worries, female solidarity is something I can deal with. I already thought you might enjoy Nina's visit."

Carolin shakes her head and yawns. "Absolutely not. But now I really must go to bed. I have an early client meeting tomorrow - unfortunately at 8 am."

"Let's set a new date for our cooking event - let's make it a secret location this time."

She stands up, Daniel does too. Ugh, what a waste. With that, Daniel goes home and nothing has changed. A real failure. I won't be going to Mr Beck with this story. He was right all along. Daniel is just too nice and... slow. I need to act.

The two stand in the hallway, and Carolin opens the door for Daniel. For a moment it seems he's going to walk past her, but then he hesitates - and closes the door again.

"Listen, Carolin, I have something to tell you. I... no, I need to do something."

Then he puts his hands on her shoulders, pulls her close, and kisses her. On the mouth. Just as quickly as it happens, he lets her go, mumbles an unclear phrase, and disappears. Goodbye.

FIFTEEN

Darn! I actually wanted to tell Mr Beck about yesterday's evening right away. I was looking forward to his reaction when he realized I was right. But that's not going to happen now. Instead of strolling into the workshop in the morning and then heading out to the garden, I'm chasing after Carolin, who is carrying a large suitcase and clearly in a hurry. She gives me a look over her shoulder.

"Come on, Hercules, pick up the pace! Don't make friends with every tree!"

She pulls hard on my leash, and that's not something I enjoy. Not like this. Not with me. As a protest, I sit down.

"Hercules, what's going on? Come on, we're late. I need to deliver this on time."

Another tug. I put the car in reverse. Carolin sighs and sets the large box down.

"You're an undisciplined mutt! Your mistress has to work so she can buy you sausage. We have an appointment, people are waiting for us."

Pah! I don't care. I could've left Daniel to watch over me if I was bothering anyone. Carolin thinks for a moment, then gets down on her knees and pets my neck.

"Hercules, sweetie, please be a good dog and come with me now. I promise it'll be quick. I just need to deliver something, then we're going back home to Daniel. Please!"

Am I imagining things or does her voice sound warmer when she says "Daniel"? Maybe it's wishful thinking, but the thought of returning soon calms me down. I give in and trot closer to Carolin. She scratches behind my ears for a moment.

"Thank you, sweetie. I'll hurry."

Shortly after, we're standing in front of a large building's revolving door.

"Do you want to wait outside?"

No, I don't. I press myself close to Carolin's leg.

"Alright then, come on in. But let me take your leash off first, or you might get stuck in the door."

She bends down and unhooks the leash from my collar, then we struggle our way through the door, which is no easy feat. I'm small but long, and navigating a revolving door without getting my tail caught is quite a challenge. Humans wouldn't have fallen for such a stupid design if they didn't need to. The glass also brushes against the tip of my tail, but then we're inside. In front of us lies a vast hall with many people walking back and forth. On each side are corridors lined with columns, giving the place a certain resemblance to the ballroom at Schloss Eschersbach.

I'm sure I've never been here before, yet something about this place feels familiar. Not just because of Schloss Eschersbach - I've been in a similar room with Carolin before. Along the walls stand large machines that look like a combination of a cabinet and one of those TVs Carolin has in our living room. When people now stand in front of these cabinets and type something under the TV, they start to rattle and spit out papers, just like the ones Carolin always carries around. That's where you get panse from the butcher and coffee at the restaurant, I've figured that out already.

Further ahead in the hall, people are sitting behind high tables talking to each other. It seems this is some kind of meeting place. There doesn't seem to be anything edible, which is strange because there's usually something to eat when people get together deliberately. But maybe they're just playing here? Well, we'll be back outside soon enough, so it's not worth investigating the hall's purpose further.

While Carolin chats with a man further ahead in the hall, I wander around, look at the people, and eventually sit down on the edge of the platform. I'm bored. When people talk, they seem to completely lose track of time. Should I trot over to Carolin and tug at her pants? But right now I can't even see her. Where did she go? Maybe I should look for her, or this will take forever.

Just then there's a loud bang. I jump in fear and press myself against the wall behind me. What was that? Further ahead in the hall, people start talking loudly, people are running around. Then another command: "Everyone down on the floor, now!"

And indeed, most of the people quickly lie down. It's like a dog training session my mother sometimes tells me about. A fun event! But what's going on? And - where is Carolin? Is she lying around here somewhere too? Slowly, I make my way further forward, trying not to disturb this performance. At the head of the hall stands the man who gave the command. He's the only one still standing. Not only that, he seems to be holding something in his hand and waving it around while giving more orders. What is that? I try to sneak closer to him without drawing attention, but I need to take a closer look.

As I inched closer to within two meters, the man suddenly turns towards me. Now I can see what he's holding: a rifle. How embarrassing, I should've realized that from the bang! So really - as a descendant of famous hunting dogs, this shouldn't happen to me. However, the purpose of all this exercise remains unclear to me, because the man doesn't look like a hunter: He's completely black-clad, plus his face is unrecognizable since he's also wearing a black cap that reaches from his crown to his neck and only leaves a slit for his eyes. Very strange.

I'm so distracted by this intriguing scene that I don't notice another man crawling forward next to me. Only when he jumps up and charges directly at the man with the black cap do I realize something. The two wrestle, eventually ending up on the ground where they continue fighting. Unbelievable! Here more happens in five minutes than in Carolyn's workshop in two weeks. The rifle-less man clearly tries to get hold of the gun while the cap-wearer defends himself at all costs. They roll back and forth, so tangled together that it's hard to say whose arms and legs belong to whom. Then suddenly there's another loud bang - obviously a shot was fired. The rifle-less man rolls loudly to the side, the other stands up and shakes his head. He then takes the gun that's now on the ground, approaches his attacker, and... aims!

I immediately know what this means: a warning shot. The cap wants to kill him! No!, I want to shout. This is a person, not a rabbit! My heart races. Then, without further thought, I burst out of cover and jump at the man with the cap. It feels almost like I'm observing myself as it all happens: I leap high and sink my teeth into the man's pant leg before he can fire again. The black fabric of his pants isn't very strong; I feel it tear instantly. Then, my teeth are already hanging onto his leg. The man jerks back sharply, screaming in pain, and raises his leg. I let go and fall in front of him. He removes the cap from his head and stares at me angrily.

"What the hell is going on? Can someone explain where this dog came from all of a sudden?"

Suddenly, people are running towards us from everywhere; the order to lie down seems forgotten. But the biggest surprise: The wounded man who was just writhing in agony on the floor has suddenly stood up and looks compassionately at his tormentor.

"Damn it, Jens, does it hurt?"

"And about time!" The bitten man pushes his leg higher; a beautiful bite mark from me is visible on his thigh. "Helen! I think I need a Coolpad or something."

A young woman with blonde hair emerges from behind one of the columns, and from a group of people, an older man with silver locks detaches himself - he has a certain resemblance to old Eschersbach. The young woman kneels down in front of the man named Jens and examines the bite; the older gentleman turns to the other people.

"Alright, come clean: Who brought the dog here?"

Silence.

"Who?," Silverlocks repeats.

I'd like to run away because my gut tells me that Silverlocks is really angry, and Carolyn will get into a lot of trouble. But why, I still don't understand; after all, I prevented a crime. However, it seems no one cares - everyone acts as if bringing a rifle to your fellow humans is the most normal thing in the world. Before I can even think about how to skillfully wriggle out of this situation, I hear Carolyn's voice: "I was the one. I brought the dog here."

Now I finally see her; she stands next to one of the columns on the left side.

"I'm sorry, I didn't realize Hercules had run away. I thought he was still standing by my side and..."

She tries to explain further, but the man has already started yelling: "Are you crazy? Do you know how expensive this whole shoot is? Every hour we spend here costs money! And then you bring your unruly dachshund here. I hope he didn't really hurt Jens - without him, we can forget about production; he's our lead actor!" He snorts once more, takes a deep breath, and continues in a slightly calmer tone. "Who are you, anyway?"

Carolyn has turned pale around her nose and whispers as she answers: "My name is Neumann. I delivered the cello case for the rifle. I'm really sorry about Hercules. He thought it was a real bank robbery and wanted to protect Mr. There."

Exactly! I'm not unruly; I'm just helpful - and quite brave!

By now, Jens has stood next to us and is curiously eyeing Carolyn. Without the cap, he looks better. He has those apparently important blue eyes for men, his hair is completely dark and wild-messy. That could also be due to the cap, though.

"Let's just drop it, Roland. I'm fine; the kid bit pretty hard, but I think I'll make it." He winks at Carolyn, who actually smiles back. Then he bends down to me. "Well, thought you were going to help Uwe rob a bank? Brave dog."

Robbing a bank? What in the world? And why are Jens and Uwe apparently friends? They were just about to go at each other; now they're making up? My head is spinning.

A dog shouldn't have to deal with this. Silverlocks seems to be tired of all the talking, too. He claps briefly and energetically in his hands.

"Alright, kids, let's get some peace here. Jens, take a break for a moment. The extras please back in twenty minutes. And I'm going to have a nice Yogi tea now." Then he looks at me again. "And get rid of the dog quickly."

Carolyn nods and bends down to leash me; "Let's go before you cause any more chaos," she says.

I'm offended. After all, I still don't know what exactly I did wrong. But since it's also very unpleasant for me to have caused so much trouble for Carolyn, I obediently trot along beside her.

She turns back to Jens one last time: "I'm truly sorry, and I hope you're not in too much pain. If there's anything I can do for you, please let me know."

"Not a big deal, Mrs. Neumann. But if you could do me one big favor and make my pain go away... that would be great."

Oh no, is he going to say "Take this naughty animal to the pound"?

Jens rummages in his pocket for a moment, then hands Carolyn a piece of paper. "Please have dinner with me? My number's on there. I'll wait for your call."

"Incredible! Jens Uhland! The most promising young actor in Germany wants to eat with you! I can't believe it! Incredible, incredible, incredible!"

Nina ist wirklich völlig aus dem Häuschen. Der Mann mit der Mütze scheint irgendwie wichtig zu sein. Seitdem Carolin ihr beim Mittagessen von dem Vorfall heute Morgen erzählt hat, hat Nina kein einziges Mal richtig Luft geholt. Stattdessen redet sie fast ununterbrochen. Bei Menschen, besonders bei Frauen, laut Herrn Beck ein sicheres Zeichen großer Aufregung. Ich verstehe aber nicht, warum Nina so aufgeregt ist. Tatsächlich ist doch nichts Außergewöhnliches passiert. Jens hat keine bleibenden Schäden davongetragen, Silberlocke hat auch aufgehört zu schimpfen und wir sind alle wohlbehalten, wenn auch ohne unseren Cellokasten, wieder zu Hause angekommen. Ich verstehe immer noch nicht, was der ganze Trara mit der Waffe und dem Schuss soll. Jens hat den anderen Mann doch eindeutig angeschossen – aber warum sprang er später trotzdem herum wie ein junges Reh? Die Erklärung von Carolin habe ich auch nicht verstanden: Film, Drehort, Dreharbeiten? Was bedeutet das bloß? Nina scheint sich hingegen nur für eine Sache zu interessieren: nämlich für Jens. Furchtbar, diese Frau.

„Hast du ihn schon angerufen?“

„Quatsch – wann denn? Das ist doch gerade mal drei Stunden her.“

„Ach stimmt – aber wirst du ihn anrufen?“ „Na ja, ich bin mir nicht so sicher.“

„Du bist dir nicht sicher? Ich verstehe das nicht – du bist jung, Single: Was gibt es da noch zu überlegen?“

„Nun, nur weil er ein angesehener C-Promi ist, muss er doch nicht gleich mein Typ sein. Ich fand ihn zwar ganz süß, aber mehr auch nicht.“

„Was nicht ist, kann ja noch werden. Und ‚ganz süß‘ ist wirklich eine Untertreibung des Jahrhunderts. Jens Uhland ist ein richtiger Hammer-Typ. Er sieht blendend aus, ist witzig und hat offensichtlich Charme.“

Carolin rollt mit den Augen. „Hast du jemals daran gedacht, dass ich im Moment gar nicht auf der Suche nach einem neuen Freund bin?“

„Nein, das finde ich völlig abwegig. Aber selbst wenn – du musst ja nicht suchen, trotzdem kannst du zugreifen, wenn der Richtige vorbeikommt.“

Das ist ja alles höchst interessant. Nina hält tatsächlich Jens für den „Richtigen“. Ich verstehe nicht, warum. Im Gegenteil – ich würde ihn eher in die Kategorie „bewaffneter Gewalttäter“ einordnen. Außer mir scheint das aber niemandem aufgefallen zu sein. Stattdessen werde ich jetzt als gewalttätiger Dackel gebrandmarkt. Und noch etwas stört mich sehr: Carolin soll sich keinen Freund suchen. Denn wir haben doch schon den idealen Kandidaten gefunden – Daniel. Diese Nina geht mir langsam wirklich auf die Nerven: Erst stört sie das intime Treffen mit Daniel und jetzt will sie Carolin noch diesen doofen Jens unterjubeln. Unmöglich! Sie sollte sich lieber um ihr eigenes Liebesleben kümmern, da hat sie genug zu tun.

Ich beschließe, mich dem Projekt „Jens“ in den Weg zu stellen. Jetzt habe ich ihn schon gebissen, da werden wir keine Freunde mehr. Sollte er noch einmal auftauchen, werde ich ihn einfach anpinkeln. Und das meine ich wörtlich.

SECHZENFTS

Wenn Frauen lange vor ihrem Kleiderschrank stehen, immer wieder ein Teil herausnehmen, es sich an den Körper halten und dann vor dem Spiegel betrachten, hat das aus Dackelsicht etwas Unfassbares Komisches. Gut, ich weiß mittlerweile, dass Menschen je nach Anlass ein anderes Fell anlegen – aber nach welchen Kriterien sie ihre Fellwahl treffen, ist mir immer noch ein Rätsel. Warum gestern ein blümiges Kleid und morgen eine schwarze Hose? Und zum Kleid: Auch die Länge scheint hier eine entscheidende Rolle zu spielen. Denn gerade jetzt legt Carolin drei schwarze Kleider nebeneinander auf ihr Bett, die eigentlich völlig gleich aussehen. Nur, dass sie eben unterschiedlich lang sind. Schweigend betrachtet sie die Kleider und dreht sich dann zu mir um.

„Hmm, was meinst du, Herkules? Mini, Midi oder Maxi?“ Ich bin ratlos. Natürlich möchte ich Carolin gerne beraten, denn wenn sie sich heute Abend wieder mit Daniel trifft, soll es endlich klappen zwischen den beiden. Und ich bin mir sicher, dass ein entscheidender Schritt zum Erfolg gemacht ist, wenn sich Carolin in ihrer Haut bzw. ihrem Kleid wohlfühlt. Die Rocklänge ist allerdings etwas, über das ich mir noch nie im Leben Gedanken gemacht habe. Das wäre bei der Länge meiner eigenen Beine auch ziemlich unsinnig – selbst wenn es Röcke für Dackel gäbe, müssten die zwangsläufig immer sehr kurz sein. Was will die Frau als solche mit der Rocklänge sagen? Ich laufe unsicher vor dem Bett hin und her. Soll man viel Bein zeigen oder lieber weniger? Worauf achten Männer?

Bei Hunden bzw. Dackeln ist das natürlich viel einfacher. Da gibt es den Welthundeverband FCI, der in seiner Rasse-Richtlinie Nr. 148/13.3.2001 D einen schönen Dackel wie folgt beschreibt: Niedrige, kurzläufige, langgestreckte, aber kompakte Gestalt, sehr muskulös mit keck herausfordernder Haltung des Kopfes und aufmerksamem Gesichtsausdruck. Bei einem Bodenabstand von etwa einem Drittel der Widerristhöhe soll die Körperlänge in harmonischem Verhältnis zur Widerristhöhe stehen, ungefähr 1 zu 1,8.

Was das Fellkleid des Rauhaardackels betrifft, hat der Deutsche Teckelclub von 1888 klare Vorstellungen: Der Rauhhaarteckel zeigt ein kurzes, dichtes, eng anliegendes, drahtiges Deckhaar mit ausreichender Unterwolle. Am Fang ist ein Bart deutlich erkennbar, die Augenbrauen sind buschig. An den Beinen ist das Haar kürzer als am Körper, fast glatt. An der Rute entspricht die Behaarung der Körperbehaarung, sie ist eng anliegend und verjüngt sich.

So einfach ist es also bei Dackeln. Ich weiß das so genau, weil ich selbst ein Mischling war. Auf Schloss Eschersbach musste ich mir oft anhören, dass etwas nicht stimmte mit mir. Meine Beine sind eindeutig zu lang und mein Fell zu weich und wuschelig für einen echten Rauhaar. Und Opili liebte mich zwar innig, aber seinem Sohn hat er den Seitensprung nie verziehen. Schliesslich waren alle seine bisherigen Enkel immer Champions gewesen.

Aber zurück zur eigentlichen Frage: Wie sieht denn nun der gültige Standard für Menschen aus? Denn dass es einen gibt, da bin ich mir ganz sicher. Andernfalls wäre es kaum zu erklären, warum Carolin vor jeder Verabredung so viel Zeit mit ihrem Outfit verbringt. Das wäre dann ja egal und sie könnte einfach so losgehen, wie sie ist. Leider kenne ich diesen Standard nicht. Denn sonst wüsste ich, ob man zum Beispiel lange Beine wie Carolins eher versteckt oder betont. Ich setze mich auf meinen Hintern und mustere Carolin genau an. In meinen Augen ist sie ein äußerst hübscher Mensch. Aber ist sie das auch für andere?

„Hey Herkules, du guckst ja so nachdenklich? Willst du dich wirklich bemühen, mir gute Ratschläge zu geben? Also, es geht darum: Ich schwanke zwischen diesem kurzen schwarzen Kleid hier oder dem langen anthrazitfarbenen, das ich gerade trage. Die schwarze Hose gefällt mir nicht so gut. Schwierig, oder? Meine Oma sagt immer, die Schönheit liegt im Auge des Betrachters – das heisst, jeder sieht etwas anderes schön.“

Also, what is this saying? My grandmother knows better now, and Caroline's grandma has no clue, otherwise she wouldn't be telling such nonsense. Maybe there isn't a clearly defined standard for humans, but something similar definitely exists. I can't imagine that every person decides for themselves what they find beautiful. By the way, last night I watched a TV show with Caroline that was essentially like a dog show. Only instead of dogs, it featured women. Just as in a dog show, the women walked individually before the judges in a circle, and then the judges told them if they were beautiful or not. Okay, the evaluation scale didn't go from "Excellent" to "Inadequate," but otherwise, it was exactly the same. The women who received good ratings apparently won something - then the chief judge would say things like "You're through to the casting," and the respective woman would be overjoyed. And for the less fortunate ones, she'd say, "Unfortunately, it's not enough." They'd start crying. For what? I have no idea. Maybe for breeding? But that's just a guess.

So, jeans or skirt? What's better? I tilt my head and try to imagine Caroline in both side by side. Caroline nods encouragingly at me and holds up the short dress again.

"What do you think makes Jens find me most beautiful?"

JENS?! Caroline isn't even seeing Daniel? This bad news literally knocks me off my feet, and with a whining howl, I roll over onto my side.

"Hercules!" Caroline calls out. "Are you having another fit now?"

She lets the dress fall, kneels down next to me, and runs her hand through my fur. That's when I have an idea: If I'm sick, she definitely cancels the date with this Jens. So, I give my famous park performance - complete with all the trimmings: I howl and shiver, twist in spasms. Caroline looks at me in horror, then jumps up and runs out of the bedroom. Uff, a short break. This acting business is quite exhausting. I hear Caroline apparently talking on the phone with Nina.

"Nina? Do you have Marc Wagner's private number? Hercules has another fit, and the appointment time is long past... " A pause. "Thanks, I'll pass it along."

She appears in the bedroom with the phone in hand. Now, I'm lying on my back, just twitching occasionally. I think I make a pretty convincing show.

"Dr. Wagner? Neumann here, you know, Nina's friend with the dachshund. I'm sorry to bother you at this hour, but Hercules had another fit just now and is lying here completely apathetic. I'm so worried... " She kneels down next to me again. "Oh yes, would you do that? That's very, very kind of you. Helvetiastraße 12, a large Art Nouveau house. Right, see you soon."

As soon as she ends the call, she dials another number. "Jens? It's Caroline. I'm sorry, and it sounds ridiculous, but my dachshund just had another epileptic fit, and now the vet is on his way. Can we reschedule for another night? I don't feel so good about leaving Hercules alone today. Yes? Thank you, I'll call tomorrow. Bye!"

If I weren't miming a sick dog, it would be time for triumphal howls. Unfortunately, my cover would then be blown, so instead, I just lie on the bedspread and whine occasionally. Caroline pets me and hums to herself. She's probably trying to calm me down. Then the doorbell rings: Dr. Wagner. Not the man I'd like to see here, but before Jens ruins my plans for Caroline and Daniel, I'd rather get checked out by this vet a few more times. After all, it's for a good cause.

Caroline lets him in and leads him straight into the bedroom. "Thank you so much for coming so quickly. Look at how bad he still is!"

Wagner has a bag with him that he sets down next to me. "Hmm, well, let's see what we can do."

He sits down on the floor beside me and pulls out some sort of thick pen from his bag. With it, he aims directly at my eyes - a bright beam blinds me.

"Pupil reflexes are normal," he says and stands up again. "So, it doesn't look like Hercules had an epileptic fit. If he had, his pupils would be dilated and wouldn't constrict when exposed to light." He sits back down. "But I don't think that's what happened here. I have another idea."

He rummages in his bag again, then pulls out a metal device with two prongs and some sort of pliers. He sits back down beside me and attaches the pronged device to my chest. It seems he's listening to something.

"Well, the heart sounds normal," he says after a moment. "His heartbeat is completely unaffected." He unplugs the device from his ears. "Heart rhythm disturbances can also trigger seizures. You could think of it like this: The disturbance in rhythm leads to a drop in blood pressure in the brain, which then causes a loss of consciousness with convulsions." He pets me. "Of course, the fit is over now, but usually it takes some time for the rhythm to return to normal. Hercules, what on earth did you do?"

Caroline looks at me worriedly. "Maybe he has some other serious illness?"

Caroline's voice sounds so nervous that I decide to act healthy again. I don't want to overdo it, so I stand up and give myself a quick shake.

"Ms. Neumann, the way Hercules looks now, he seems completely healthy to me. Sure, we can have him checked out from head to toe next week in my practice, but I think there's no health issue here. Call it my animal instinct, but I believe Hercules is not as bad off as we think. You're probably just worrying unnecessarily."

Grrr, you traitor - stop digging! Caroline will be furious if she realizes this is all just a show. I decide to drop the act. I'm done with this fit-pretending business.

"But didn't you say you know the breeding line Hercules comes from? It would reassure me if you looked into it again."

"Yes, good point," he says. "I'll be there next week anyway, so I'll ask around. But for now, just assume that Hercules is fine." He stands up and grabs his bag. "Well, I'm off. You seem to have had a little costume rehearsal here. After all, you did sacrifice your evening for us."

Вагнер колеблется. Пойди, парень, убирайся - Каролин хочет быть вежливой. На самом деле здесь никто не придает значения твоей присутствию!

- Но ты ведь все еще встречен с кем-то, я не хочу мешать.

Вагнер остроумно замечает: "Ты правильно понял, Вагнер". До свидания!

- Напротив, я рад, что ты останешься. К тому же я уже отменил свою встречу с Геркулесом.

- Тогда я буду рад помочь.

- О нет! Как только мы избавляемся от одного, появляется другой! Ну хорошо, по крайней мере Вагнер не представляет опасности для Даниэля, но это и его единственный преимущество. Каролин улыбается ему: "Кстати, я Каролин".

- Да, спасибо. Я Марк.

Каролин кивает: "Я знаю".

Они оба смеются - немного неловко, как мне кажется.

- Ты можешь пойти в гостиную, э-э, ты уже идёшь туда? Она прямо напротив входной двери. Я быстро оденусь в что-то другое.

- Не нужно, я считаю, ты выглядишь очаровательно в джинсах и потертой футболке.

Каролин смущенно смеется: "Ну хорошо, если ты терпишь меня в таком виде, то останусь". - "С удовольствием, без проблем".

Вместо того чтобы схватить свой глупый чемодан и убегать, через две минуты Вагнер сидит на нашей гостевой диване. Я злюсь и кладусь прямо перед ним, наблюдая, как Каролин достает из шкафа два стакана. Она просто слишком доброжелательна. Почему она не пустила Вагнера уходить? Вместо этого снова начинается эта "питье вина" история. Я неохотно признаю это, но иногда люди просто бесконечно скучны. Например, когда они наливают вино: скоро Каролин снова будет переливать вино из одной бутылки в другую. Затем они наполнят два стакана вторым напитком. Если мне повезет, они быстро выпьют его и мы избавимся от доктора Вагнера. К сожалению, такое развитие событий крайне маловероятно. Скорее всего, они будут сидеть на диване вечно и болтать о том, что в жизни якобы важно. Слово "разговор" - синоним для особенно неэффективного способа провести вечер, если ты, как большинство людей, просто слишком ленив, чтобы пробежаться по ближайшему парку.

Я внутренне вздыхаю и кладу голову на передние лапы. Когда голоса Каролин и Вагнера тускнеют в качестве фона, я думаю, как я могу заставить дело с Даниэлом двигаться вперед. Может быть, я смогу как-то заманить их в засаду, чтобы они наконец остались одни? Тогда все остальное может решиться само собой. Но как?

Мне срочно нужно снова проконсультироваться с господином Беком. Кошки, как известно, мастера стратегии, и он, без сомнения, имеет идею, как это сделать. Страшно даже подумать, что было так близко! Если бы не Нина, они уже могли бы быть парой. Но с тех пор, как этот вечер, Даниэль и Каролин редко виделись, только коротко в мастерской, и почти всегда были какие-то клиенты рядом. Конечно, это ничего не получится. Потому что я уже понял, даже без уроков господина Бека: когда дело доходит до парных отношений, люди, кажется, очень стеснительны. По крайней мере, так было в тот вечер, который мы наблюдали с Беком за Томасом и другой женщиной. Мы собаки на это смотрим иначе. Например, если дакель-самец увидит свою любимую на другой стороне улицы, он не позволит себе отвлечься от прогулки даже на несколько минут. Странно, что люди так часто проявляют стеснительность.

Теперь Каролин и Вагнер действительно обсуждают работу. Я говорю "конечно", потому что я уже начинаю понимать этих двоих. Вагнер, в частности, увлеченно рассказывает о своем профессиональном пути:

- И тогда я понял, что хочу стать ветеринаром. Так что я перешел от медицины человека к ветеринарии. Хотя я, конечно, поклялся никогда не делать то же самое, что и мой отец. Ну и теперь я даже взял на себя его практику и живу в том же доме, что и он тогда. Вначале это было странное чувство, но теперь я могу сказать, что это лучшее решение в моей жизни. Моя профессия делает меня счастливым.

И кто ему каков? Мне точно не интересно. Надеюсь, Каролин скоро выгонит этого надутого нудиста. Хорошо, признаюсь, я сам не без вины в его присутствии, но основная цель визита Вагнера теперь выполнена: Дженс на этот вечер занят, а Вагнер - лишний. Ему нужно быстро допить вино и спуститься в свою клетку. В переносном смысле, конечно. Скорее всего, Каролин уже думает, как вывести его из квартиры как можно более изящно.

- Мне нравится, что ты последовал своему внутреннему голосу. Это вообще нужно делать чаще. А еще - еще бокал вина?

Я, наверное, ошибся - больше вина? Но, похоже, Вагнер не так уже и раздражает Каролин. Вежливость здесь уже не оправдание. Интересует ли ее его история?

Каролин снова наполняет оба стакана, затем садится рядом с Вагнером. 
- Я тоже живу над своей мастерской. Нина говорит, что ей это нервирует, но я считаю это идеальным.

Вагнер кивает: 
- Мне тоже так кажется. Как ты пришла на идею открыть мастерскую? Это ведь не как другие профессии. Или это у тебя в семье?

Каролин встряхивает головой: 
- Нет, совсем нет. Мой отец - адвокат, моя мать - домохозяйка. Но я знала уже в школе, что хочу стать скрипачкой. Я люблю музыку, сама много играла на скрипке, и хотела заниматься чем-то рукодельным. Тогда я прошла стажировку в мастерской по изготовлению скрипок и поняла: это то, что мне нужно!

He clears his throat. "Well, I have an idea. What if we went to a concert together next week? I have two tickets for the music hall and no company yet."

Carolin hesitates. Of course, it's not easy to give her saviour of her dog a clear refusal right away. "So...um...I mean..."

Now out with it, Carolin! It's time we get rid of this guy. "Absolutely! I'd love to!"

**SEVENTEEN**

"Look, kid, you can't rush into things. Forget about it."

Okay, that doesn't sound like agreement. But I'm not easily discouraged. After all, I'm not Carl-Leopold von Eschersbach if a bland objection from an aging cat is going to throw me off track. We're back in our favourite spot under the tree, and I've just given Beck a brief update on the ups and downs of the past three days regarding Carolin and the men.

"Believe me, Mr. Beck, something's happening! You didn't see them - Daniel kissed her, after all. That has to mean something."

"Yes. It means that Daniel is in love with Carolin. Not necessarily that she feels the same way about him. Otherwise, she would have kissed him back, not the other way around."

I start getting properly annoyed with Beck. "This is really nitpicking - what difference does it make whether he kisses her or she kisses him? It's the same thing - they kissed each other!"

"There you go again, Hercules: A guy who relies on a fat cat and a short-legged mutt to impress a woman is a lost cause. A loser. A zero."

I feel anger rising within me. My voice sounds hoarse as I scold Beck: "Take that back right now! Right this instant! Otherwise, you won't be my friend for much longer!"

"God, yes, I'm sorry. I apologise for the short-legged mix-up."

"Not what I meant."

"Well, I apologise for being short-legged too. Are you satisfied now?"

"I mean the loser. Daniel isn't a lost cause. He's a very nice person who means a lot to me by chance. He's my friend."

Mr. Beck rolls his eyes. "So, dogs, always talking about 'now let's be clear': The dog is not man's best friend, and vice versa. Daniel is a human being. And man's best friend is...man. And just because one person doesn't have another person as a friend, that doesn't mean they can suddenly become friends with an animal. Whenever a person talks to you and tells you all this personal stuff, they're not talking to you; they're talking to themselves. They're having self-conversations, get it? But since they don't want to feel so stupid or lonely, they have them with you. You are, in essence, something like that little plastic colleague of the dumb parakeet. A replacement. Nothing more."

Don't talk badly about my friend Daniel -

If I could cry, now would be a good time. And if I were a violent person, this would also be a good moment. That Mr. Beck can be so mean surprises me. But one thing is clear: While Beck may have gathered quite a bit of knowledge about people, he doesn't really know them. Of course, humans can be nasty, think of Thomas. But they also have something that makes them unique and valuable to me. They have feelings, compassion. They can share joy and sadness with others, get angry when their friend is upset. And they can love. Yes, even a little dog like me. I will never forget Emilia's tears as she put me in the box and knew she wouldn't see me again. Carolin is my friend. And Daniel is my friend. I know it for sure. Now all that's left is for them to become a couple. And I'll make sure of that, with or without Beck. In this case, probably without. So I turn around and leave Beck sitting there.

"Hey, little guy, don't just walk away! That wasn't meant badly!" Beck calls after me, but I pretend not to hear him and keep walking. Beck follows. "I do like Daniel too, but you have to stay realistic. Hey, now hold on a second, Hercules!" I'm almost at the terrace door. "Carl-Leopold! I'm sorry!"

Okay, that does seem more serious. I stop. Let no one say I'm stubborn and vengeful. Mr. Beck walks around me and sits in front of me.

"I didn't mean to hurt your feelings. If you've let so many people into your heart, it's obviously up to you. They probably like you a lot too. Maybe I'm just a bit jealous."

I tilt my head. That does sound better.

"But there is one thing I stand by: If Carolin isn't in love with Daniel, then neither can we change that. The human heart is not easily influenced and only very rarely open to rational arguments. In other words, even if we both know that Daniel would be perfect for Carolin, we can do nothing in this case. And if we create as many opportunities as possible for the two of them to be alone."

Hmm, that does make sense. Still, I don't want to give up yet.

"But we don't even know if Carolin is in love with Daniel. So that's why I thought we should at least try again. If it doesn't work out, I'll lay down my weapons, promised!"

Mr. Beck sighs. "Man, you're stubborn. I think all these near-arrangements with actors and veterinarians speak extremely against it - but for me, let's go ahead. What's the plan?"

"I don't have one," I admit a bit sheepishly. "That's why I asked you. Because you are, after all."

Mr. Beck grins and stretches out in front of me. "Yes, that's right. I'll think about it."

There's not much going on in the workshop today. Daniel and Carolin stand silently at their desks, sawing and sanding away at some random pieces of wood. Boring. Plus, I have a feeling the atmosphere between them is no longer charged but rather tense. Carolin hasn't even looked at Daniel once today - while he keeps stealing glances at her when he thinks she's not looking. Very strange. Maybe Beck is right, and we should scrap our plan before we've even developed it properly.

Daniel clears his throat. "Hey, Carolin," Daniel emerges from behind his desk and walks towards Carolin. Finally, some progress!

"Yes?"

"Uh...do you have any collophonium over there? I can't seem to find any here."

Argh! What's this supposed to mean? Collophonium? I don't know what that is, but I'm pretty sure it's not a code word for... "I love you, can I kiss you?"

"Yes, I have some left. Here."

*Thud!* A small, transparent, brown block falls from the jar Carolin has just handed to Daniel without really looking. Now both items lie on the floor. Carolin kneels down to pick up the block and jar. Daniel bends over as well. For a moment, they're very close to each other—almost face-to-face. I'd love to shout it out loud. Unfortunately, of course, nothing short of thinking it very loudly is left for me to do. And indeed, telepathy between dachshund and man works: Daniel grabs Carolin's hand and holds on tight. Come on, Daniel! Do something!, 

"Carolin, is something wrong?"

Carolin gives Daniel a brief look, then turns back to the ground.

"No, why?" she mutters.

"You're avoiding me," Daniel says. 
"Not at all, you're imagining it."

"Is it because of what happened earlier?"

"I don't understand what you mean."

"Come on, let's at least talk about this: Is it because I kissed you?" 

"No, well... I..." Daniel sighs. "I knew it was a mistake."

Daniel lets go of Carolin's hand and sits down next to her on the floor. A moment passes in silence, then Daniel pats Carolin on the side.

"Hey, Carolin. Come on, don't take this so seriously. You don't have to explain anything to me. It's all good. It was a moment, you looked great, and I was already kind of out of it. There was nothing I could do."

Carolin nods. "Yes, it was a very nice moment. But now..."

"Now in broad daylight, things look different, I know," Daniel finishes her sentence. "And you're wondering if this is such a good idea with me, your friend and partner."

Carolin nods.

"No, probably not," Daniel continues.  "Although it was a very beautiful thought of us being a couple. At least for a moment."

"Are you not mad at me?"

"No, I had the same thoughts. Well, and now stop moping around like that. That's an order!"

Daniel laughs, and finally, albeit a bit reluctantly, Carolin laughs too.

As for me, I could cry. My beautiful plan! Well, not exactly a plan—more of a half-baked idea! It would have been so great to have Daniel and Carolin as master and mistress. A real little family. And the worst part is: The search starts all over again, and the danger that we'll catch someone like Thomas is far from gone. I was really sure Daniel was the perfect man for Carolin. Well, not "was," I'm certain he is. But Mr. Beck was right: If Carolin's heart doesn't realize it on its own, there's no point. With a drooped head, I slink back towards the terrace door to report my defeat to Beck. If he's truly my friend and his apology was sincere, maybe he'll comfort me.

But before I get outside, the doorbell rings. Usually, I love running up front and greeting visitors, but my mood is so low, I can barely motivate myself. It probably rings again because it's that stupid Aurora trying to flirt with Daniel. But no, it rings once more, and naturally, I'm quite curious. On the other hand, the sun is shining beautifully outside, and the thought of lying under a tree in my backyard while Mr. Beck listens to my woes as the grass tickles my belly is almost tempting. Finally, though, curiosity wins out, and I run towards the door.

Carolin has already answered it. In our hallway stands a man in a brown uniform handing Carolin a package.

"Are you Mrs. Neumann? I need your signature here."

Carolin sets the package down on the floor to sign. I sniff curiously. Hmm, smells kind of delicious. What could be inside? As Carolin takes the package to her worktable in the workshop, I follow.

"Was that for you?" Daniel asks.

"Yes."

"What is it?"

"I have no idea. I didn't order anything."

"Maybe you left something behind," Daniel says. 
"Could be," Carolin agrees.

I highly doubt it. After all, I was there, and I'm sure we took everything with us when we left—except the box, of course; it's definitely not in the package. It smells way different anyway. Not nearly as good. What is inside? I make a face and get down on my knees to be at Carolin's level. She looks surprised.

"Hey, sweetheart, what's wrong with you? You're acting crazy."

"Well, maybe he sent you a kilo of coke, and Hercules has all the facilities for a top drug-sniffing dog."

Daniel grins. "You know, those film types are always up to no good."

Carolin shakes her head. "A kilo of coke? That'd be expensive to send to a passing acquaintance. But what could it be?" She opens the package with a knife and pulls out a card. "Let me see..."

She reads it and starts grinning. Daniel comes over to her table, trying to peek over her shoulder while she reads. Carolin wants to put some distance between them, but he moves lightning fast and snatches the card from her.

"Hey! That's against the rules!" She sounds annoyed, but Daniel just laughs.

"Well, my love, this one isn't for you. Here it says: 'Dear Hercules!'"

What? The card is for me? I'm instantly thrilled! Exciting! But also a bit strange, since who would write to someone who can't read? I sit down in front of Daniel and give him an expectant look. He gets the hint and starts reading aloud.

"Dear Hercules, I hope this finds you feeling better soon. To help you get back on your feet faster, I've arranged for a top-notch dog treat—a real delicacy. Drop me a line when you're back in action. Best regards, your Jens."

Wow, what a nice guy! No wonder he has a gun. I'm blown away! No wonder the package smells so good. I have to try that sausage right away.

"Why would Jens think Hercules is sick?"

"He had another fit," Carolin says in a strange voice.

"While we were at the bank?"

"Yes, exactly. That's why they even had to stop filming."

Hey! That can't be right! What's Carolin telling me?

Daniel looks concerned. "Hmm... I should call Wagner. He wanted to ask around at the breeder."

"I know," Carolin says, "but I want to talk to Dr. Wagner in person later on."

Aha, so she obviously doesn't want Daniel to know about Dr. Wagner's rescue mission—that's even more mysterious. Two big lies in two sentences! This isn't the Carolin I know! And then not even tell her friend! Why is she doing this?

On this occasion, numerous lectures by the old Eschersbach on the topic of "Disbelief" come to mind. Disbelief was, for him, one of the greatest character flaws in humans, if not the greatest. Lying came well before gluttony and shortness of breath - he often preached this. In brief, simply put: Bad people lie, good ones tell the truth. Deception and lies are a sign of weakness!

The brave are honest, disbelief is the sister of cowardice.

Of course, one must allow for the fact that bad people even come up with the idea to deliberately tell an untruth. That's already quite clever, and I'm not sure if something like that would occur to me on my own. But it doesn't help: Dishonest people may be smart, but they're always bad. This means for me that Carolin didn't lie, because it goes without saying that she's a wonderful person. But if she didn't lie, what then? Perhaps she suffers from an illness and can't quite remember how things really went with Jens and Dr. Wagner?

However, before I can further ponder this difficult topic, Carolin sets a bowl of chopped dog sausage in front of me. Immediately, I start devouring it - divine! We should take a closer look at Jens. Perhaps sabotaging the appointment between him and Carolin was a mistake. He does seem to be quite knowledgeable about dogs, or at least a dog lover. I take two more pieces into my mouth. Delicious! On the other hand, if the two had met last night, I wouldn't have gotten this delicious sausage. So it wasn't a mistake. Maybe just a small detour.

"So, do I understand you correctly: The Daniel affair is finally over, and the new hopeful candidate is Jens, who knows about dogs?" "That's right."

"This proves to me at most one thing." "What?"

"That you're for sale. You were still whining about Daniel being your friend, and we absolutely had to help him, and so on and so forth. And now? Almost as soon as some random actor sends you a piece of dog sausage, Daniel is history. Who exactly spread the rumor that dogs are loyal like gold?" Mr. Beck shakes his head in disgust. "I thought you wanted to tell me about a great plan, instead I have to listen to this dog sausage nonsense." He considers my time too precious for that.

"But...", I stammer weakly, "you yourself said you didn't think Daniel and Carolin would work out, and I just wanted to tell you that you were completely right. Why are you so angry with me now?"

"Maybe I'm disappointed that I was right? Maybe I had somehow hoped that you'd be right and a cat and a dachshund could do more than I ever thought possible. In some way, you dragged me into your optimism. I would have been happy if the two of them had succeeded. And now!"

I lower my head. "Sorry", I whisper.

"Don't get me wrong - it's not your fault that Daniel and Carolin don't work out. But to immediately switch over to Jens! "

"Well, what can I say? I was happy he cared about me. And it's the first time in my life that someone has written me a letter!"

"Goodness, you're naive! He wasn't actually worried about you! He just wanted to impress Carolin! That's all. And with that, he immediately shot down the next appointment."

"Do you think so?"

"It's obvious. What did Daniel have to say? It must have hit him hard when he found out Carolin was actually supposed to go out with Jens and my intervention ruined everything. She claimed I had already passed out in the bank, and that's what Jens heard." Disease

"Aha, a little white lie."

"White lie?"

"I guess Carolin didn't want to hurt Daniel with this dog sausage story. After all, he's her best friend."

"And then you can lie? Even though disbelief is something bad, according to my old master."

"Generally speaking, he's right. A white lie is still a lie. But sometimes people do lie because they don't want to hurt someone. And that's not so bad. It falls more into the category of exaggeration."

My head starts to swim. Lies, white lies, exaggerations - who can make sense of it all?

"But why should Daniel be hurt? He himself said his kiss was probably a bad idea. He even said it was now completely fine."

Mr. Beck shakes his head. "Hercules, I thought you would have learned to know people better by now. This is about a heart matter. No one likes to admit when they've been hit hard right in the heart. They'd rather pretend like everything isn't a problem. As I've already explained - the other person can never really know how much you love them. That's an iron rule. Otherwise, you're as good as lost."

I'm afraid Mr. Beck is right about that. The chaos humans create in love doesn't surprise me at all. Their rules are simply completely absurd. A dog would never come up with such nonsense.

EIGHTEEN

Today is a completely boring, uneventful day. Wonderful! I lie around in my workshop box, occasionally peek into the garden, and then go back to take another nap in said box. The only thing missing from complete happiness is a nice bowl full of tripe or heart. For a while, I ponder whether to ignore the growing hunger pangs - I'm actually too lazy to run over to Carolin's and demand my next meal. But eventually, my growling stomach becomes so loud that I can no longer sleep soundly. So I get up, walk over to Carolin who is sitting at her desk, and nudge her with my nose.

"Time for your food?" Carolin looks at her watch. "But you'll have to wait a little while. We still have something planned."

Ugh! I'm hungry! And not just a little bit! I nudge Carolin again. She laughs and scratches me behind the neck.

"Just wait, Hercules. We're going to do something you'll also enjoy. And then there will be food."

Hey! I don't want to do anything! I want my tripe right now, and then I want to keep lying around. Yesterday - that would have been a good time to go for a walk in the park. The whole time, I had this urge for fresh air, rabbit sniffing, and fly catching. But instead, I couldn't even get Carolin to take a small stroll with me because she was so busy. As if a dog could console her. The moment is there when the moment is there. But humans just don't understand that. Tomorrow is Saturday, so I'll have more time, I promise.

I trot back to my box. Just a moment ago I was in such a good mood, but suddenly it's vanished. With my head resting on my cuddly pillow, I grumble resentfully. We always do what Carolin wants. That's so unfair. By now the feeling in my stomach has escalated from a healthy appetite to an adult lion's hunger. I start whining a little. Carolin should definitely know that she's pushing it close to animal cruelty.

"Stop making such a fuss!" comes a heartless voice from her room. "We're about to leave. We just need to grab a few things from the apartment and then we're off. Our chauffeur should be at our doorstep in roughly thirty seconds."

Our chauffeur? That sounds exciting again. I haven't heard that word for months, and it immediately brings back memories of old times. After all, there was also a chauffeur at Schloss Eschersbach. Old Eschersbach only reluctantly got behind the wheel himself. He would often explain, while waiting for his car to arrive to take him hunting, "I don't particularly enjoy driving anymore." Most people would have likely wondered at this point whether it was really a good idea to go hunting with von Eschersbach. However, as far as I know, nothing bad ever happened. It seems the chauffeur thing was just an aristocratic form of showing off.

"My eyes are just too bad, I'd be a danger to society," I say.

The doorbell rings, and Carolin walks past my box to open it. There it says: Jens!

"Good morning, Carolin. Good morning, Hercules!" he greets us.

I have to admit that without his black mask and rifle, Jens actually looks quite nice.

He gives Carolin a kiss on both cheeks. "So, are you two ready for our picnic?"

"Of course, I just need to grab the basket from upstairs. I've chilled some things, so I'll pack them up."

She runs up the stairs, and I stay seated next to Jens.

"Are you feeling better now?" he asks me. I look at him curiously. "And did you like my sausage?"

Okay, according to Mr Beck, the sausage was pure bribery, but since I'm a polite little dog and the sausage was actually delicious, I wag my tail a bit. Then I think that maybe there could be two sausages instead of one, and I wag even more enthusiastically.

"I knew it, good boy!" He leans down to pet me behind the ears. Just at that moment, Carolin comes downstairs with a giant basket.

"It's nice to see you guys getting along better. Your last meeting wasn't exactly harmonious."

Jens laughs. "I still have a blue mark where Hercules snapped at me. But let bygones be bygones, he did try to save us after all. Plus, I finally have an effective tetanus shot now. It turned out my last vaccination was way too long ago. So the sausage had its good side too. And it seems my little present went down well."

Carolin nods. "Yes, Hercules gobbled up the sausage in about twenty seconds. He loved it."

Ugh, if they keep talking like this about dog sausages, I'm going to pass out from hunger. Hopefully it won't take much longer until I get something to eat. The basket Carolin brought from the apartment also smells like there's something really delicious inside. Unwittingly, I start drooling.

"So shall we go?"

"Let's hit the road!" Carolin cheerfully exclaims and opens the front door. Jens walks past her and towards the car parked right in front of the house.

"Please buckle up!" He swings open the passenger-side door.

"Come on, Hercules!" Carolin calls, but I hesitate. Something about the car seems off. It's... dangerous somehow. I can't quite put my finger on it, but when Carolin lifts me up to put me in the car, it becomes clear. The car: It has no roof!

Twenty minutes later, my concerns about getting into something too fast and potentially falling out are gone. I'm sitting on Carolin's lap, blowing in the wonderful summer air, and my ears flutter in the wind. A dream! Jens and Carolin chat happily. I can't quite make out what they're talking about because it's so loud in my ears, but that doesn't matter. At this moment, I feel like flying, and it feels amazing. The trees along the roadside rush by, blurring into a bright green hedge, while the sky above us is blue and vast; I could keep driving like this forever. Actually. Except unfortunately, my stomach still twitches a bit, so I hope the moment when Carolin pulls out the basket with treats from the trunk will come soon.

Indeed, Jens now slows down, and the green wall turns back into individual trees. Finally, he stops.

"Here we are. Hold on, I'll help you!"

Jens jumps out of the car, walks around, and opens Carolin's door for her. Very considerate, that has to be said. I jump off Carolin's lap, then Jens reaches out his hand to help her out. Smelling the forest and water, a bit wild, we make our way to a small clearing at the edge of a hill with steps leading down. We stand there for a moment.

"Look," Jens points ahead, "isn't that view amazing?"

"Yes, it's beautiful when the Elbe glistens in the sun."

Sounds nice, but if anyone cares: I can't see anything. My short legs put me at eye level with the stinging nettles growing on either side of the steps. Would someone please lift me up? I want to take a look too! I make myself into a ball.

"I think Hercules is really hungry now," Carolin completely misreads my situation. "He usually gets something to eat at 11 o'clock."

"We're almost there. Right in front of us starts the beach."

Good news, finally food! But what's at the end of the second set of steps? The forest stops abruptly, and we cross a small path. Now I can see where the water smell is coming from: In front of us lies a very large river. A truly, incredibly large river if you consider the size of the ship passing by. Gigantic, something like that I've never seen before! It looks like a huge floating house. I bark excitedly, and Carolin laughs. Beach?

"You'll be amazed, Hercules! We've only ever been to Alster before, and Hercules knows nothing but sailboats."

"Then it was high time he saw a real ship. After all, you can't live in Hamburg and not know the Elbe!" Jens almost accuses. We walk towards the shore—and land in a gigantic sandbox. I brake hard because sand on my paws is now an undeniable sign that a human mother will soon round the corner to scold me severely. But strangely, I didn't see any wooden fence. Unsure, I stay seated.

"Now tell me, your dog has never been to a beach? He seems almost afraid."

No, he hadn't yet. I haven't had him that long, and up until now I'd only been with him at our park or by the Alster. We haven't taken a holiday together yet. This is his first time.

Jens takes a step towards me and picks me up. "Take a good look around, little guy. Hamburg is undoubtedly the most beautiful city in the world on this kind of day, and this here is its most beautiful part. Get used to the sand at your feet, then you'll quickly realize how great it is here. From here, you can pretty much walk in any direction as far as you like. But if you see your first sheep on the dike, better turn back, or we'll lose you."

He puts me down again. To walk as far as I want - what a fantastic thought. But first of all, I need: properly! Something to eat.

Carolin has taken a blanket along beside the basket and now spreads it out on the sand. The river is just far enough away that the waves can't reach us, even if another big ship passes by. Then Carolin opens the basket and takes out the contents. Mmm, delicious. I see a little bowl with heart-shaped pieces for me. She puts it a bit further away, and I dive straight into it. As I gobble down my meal, I catch a glimpse from the corner of my eye that Carolin is serving up a feast: sausage, cheese, even a cake. Jens sets his backpack down, which he'd been carrying on his back just moments before.

"So, to celebrate, I've also brought something nice," he says. He pulls a piece of fabric out of the bag and unrolls it to reveal two long-stemmed glasses. Then he reaches into the backpack again and brings out a green bottle, which he immediately starts unscrewing. With a loud pop, the cork comes out, and Jens pours the liquid into the glasses. It's pale yellow and looks very bubbly. Plop!

"Here you go: champagne! A lovely drink for a beautiful woman!"

Carolin giggles a little shyly, then takes the glass Jens gives her.

"Thank you so much. And anyway - thank you for this great idea."

"I'm glad I could help! It's good that our date worked out. And now: let's enjoy a wonderful day!" She clinks glasses with Jens. "Yes, let's have a wonderful day."

As we return home late at night, I'm tired but in high spirits. This is the first time in my life I've swum in a river, which is actually much more tiring than swimming in a lake. I almost caught a fish. I walked until I saw the sheep. Jens threw me at least a hundred thousand sticks. I devoured dog food as well as sausage and strawberry cake. I settled down on the cozy picnic blanket, dozed off, and watched Jens and Carolin talking. And eventually, we all lay on the blanket, gazing up at the sky, watching the moon rise, then later the sun slowly sink into the great river. It was the perfect day.

Now I'm sprawled in our apartment on the couch, sanded off as usual, just happy. Carolin puts the empty picnic basket back in the kitchen and heads to the small box next to the phone that tells you who called while you weren't there. There are two new messages. First new message:

"Hello, sweetie, it's Nina! How was it? I'm so curious! Call me right away."

Second new message:

"Hello, Carolin, Marc Wagner here. You know, your trusted vet. Just wanted to see if our date on Wednesday works out. Should we grab something to eat before the concert? I usually finish a bit early on Wednesdays and could pick you up. Give me a call."

There are no other messages.

Marc Wagner - I had completely forgotten about him. And in the spirit of efficient dating, Carolin could actually cancel this appointment. Jens makes a great impression; I couldn't find any flaws today. He smells good, his hair is neat, and he was wearing something clean - so why waste time with the unsympathetic Wagner? And Carolin has clearly enjoyed it too; I haven't seen her so happy and carefree in ages. So let's call Wagner and say no! But Carolin looks undecided. After thoughtfully considering the black box, she picks up the phone.

"Hello, Nina. I know it's late - but could I maybe come over? Really? Thank you, that's kind of you. I need someone to talk to."

Great. She obviously doesn't think I'd want to hear about this either. Now isn't really a good time for me; I'm just so comfortable here. But when Carolin stands up, I also get up, not wanting to be in the way.

"Hercules, lie down again. I'm going to see Nina now, but you stay here."

Why? Do they have secrets from me? I jump off the couch. I'm not that tired!

"No, really, Hercules. You can lie down for a while. Look at how sandy you are; I don't want to bathe you right now. And Nina won't be happy if I show up with a dirty dog." She sighs. "So go ahead and curl up in your basket. You've had enough adventure for today."

Hmpf. She really doesn't want me coming along. I'm not that bad, am I? Stupid Nina!

As Carolin leaves the apartment, I fall dejectedly into my basket. It's kind of mean to spend the whole day together and then suddenly be left out. I feel so... demoted. I used to belong in there, and now I'm just a pet again. Panting. And to top it off, I'm not even tired anymore.

I lie in my basket for a while, then stand up and traipse into the kitchen. Maybe there's something left in my bowl. Nope, it's clean. I traipse back out. As I pass by the front door, I catch a familiar scent. Mr Beck! He must be standing right outside, probably on his way to an evening walk. A little chat with him would be just the thing to take my mind off things! I start barking loudly.

"Hey buddy," I hear his voice through the door, "how are you?"

"Not too bad. Just feeling a bit bored, and Carolin left me here all alone."

"That's unlucky. I'm on my way to the park. I'd take you with me, but without Carolin we can't get you out of the apartment."

"Yeah, that sucks. I really want to come along. But through the letter slot? I don't think so. I'll just stay here and keep myself entertained. Say hi to the park and the rabbits for me."

"Hmm. Will do."

Silence falls again. But just as I'm about to turn around and head back to my basket, I catch a stronger whiff of Mr Beck.

"Hey Hercules! I had one more idea, but it's something a bit daring for a brave little dog like you."

"Do you remember how I got in? Through the open window. Go quickly into Carolyn's bedroom. Maybe we're lucky and the balcony door is also propped open. That way you can get out."

Only a cat could come up with such an idea.

"Beck, your trust in my artistic abilities is commendable, but I can't do that. Even if the window is propped open: I would never fit through it. And even though you—forgive me—are overweight, you can squeeze through gaps in a way I simply don't possess. I'd definitely get stuck."

"You have no ambition at all. Let's at least take a look. I'll come through the garden onto your balcony, and then we'll assess the situation. It would be great to go for an evening stroll without people."

Mr. Beck, or rather his scent, disappears.

This cat... there's just no way. And after all this trouble I'm not even that eager to risk my head and neck here. On the other hand, wandering alone at night in the park is a pretty enticing idea. I sigh internally, then trot into the bedroom.

Indeed, the balcony door is propped open. However, the gap only starts to widen above my head, well above my reach. At that moment, Mr. Beck leaps from the ivy-covered wall of the house onto our balcony.

"Well done!" he cheerfully calls out to me.

"What exactly do you find so good about this?"

"The door is propped open. Perfect."

"But I can't fit through down here, and I can't reach up high enough to get there from above. We're out of luck."

"Can't you jump? "

"No, how would I do that?"

"Well, why don't you try climbing the curtain?"

"Mr. Beck, you clearly have the wrong idea about my claws. They won't grip anything like that. They're too straight and smooth for such a thing."

"That does make things difficult."

"I told you so."

We sit there for a while, looking at each other through the balcony door. Then, to change things up, I have a good idea. I quickly scan the room and sure enough: In the corner stands Carolyn's chair, where she always leaves her clothes when she goes to bed. It's quite sturdy and has a high back—if I can use it as a ladder, maybe we'll be able to make it work. I trot over to the chair and try pushing it through the room towards the balcony door. Phew, it's heavy!

"Shall I help or should I stay?"

"Take your time! I need to focus."

Without a little jump, it won't happen—after all, I don't want to get stuck. But to jump, I need some momentum, and that's impossible on the chair. Darn it, I wish I were just a bit taller, then it would be much easier.

"Hercules, see if you can reach the handle with your snout. Maybe you can open the door by biting it. Then all you'd have to do is pull it down."

Are we at the circus or something? Opening locked doors with little dogs is definitely beyond me! "Just try it, it can't be that hard!"

He has a good laugh, sitting there on his fat bottom. But on second thought, maybe this isn't such a bad idea. After all, it's better than getting stuck in the gap. So I make myself into a ball, get hold of the door handle, and snap back down onto the chair seat. With a jerk, the handle moves downward—and the door swings open! Amazing! I, Carl-Leopold von Eschersbach, have just opened a balcony door!

However, my euphoria only lasts for a moment. Because two seconds later, although I'm standing next to Mr. Beck on the balcony, it quickly becomes clear that our master plan had some serious flaws. How am I supposed to get down from this balcony?

NINETEEN

"I'm a hero, a Superman, a super-cat!" "Did you see that? Wasn't that phenomenal? A sensation!" "Yeah, it was pretty good."

"Pretty good? It was PERFECT! I just have it, my friend." Mr. Beck doesn't seem to realize that this is quite an athletic feat. "I mean, have you ever seen another cat jump from the first floor?" I would say he's passed as a cat now.

Beck shakes his head. "So, as I said: It was okay. But first of all, it was more like ground level, and second, you jumped into Mrs. Meyer's laundry basket. If I hadn't found that, you'd still be sitting on the balcony rail, crying."

Is this even possible? This fat cat! I don't know any other cat who has ever pulled off such a daring stunt. Just the feat of contorting himself through the bars of the balcony railing, facing certain death. And then the jump itself: spot-on into Mrs. Meyer's basket of towels. And in the dark! Well, if Mrs. Meyer hadn't left her basket outside, it would have been a bit more complicated. But it was still a brave act. I could easily have ended up with all four paws broken after landing awkwardly next to the basket.

"So, once you've recovered from your heroic deed, shall we go?"

I give Mr. Beck a mean look, which he can't see because it's dark. On the other hand, sitting here between Mrs. Meyer's towels is getting boring. So in an act of true grandeur, I forgive Mr. Beck, even though he hasn't apologized.

I leap out of the basket and follow Mr. Beck as he makes his way towards the garden gate. A light breeze tickles my nose, and adventure smells already on the wind. Even if Mr. Beck is a silly cat, in this one respect he's absolutely right: This walk without Carolyn is the perfect opportunity to finally go hunting. I feel that familiar tingling in my nose, and my tail automatically perks up. Take heed, rabbits! Carl-Leopold von Eschersbach is on the prowl—and he will catch you.

In the park, there's no sign of rabbits or anything else to hunt. Are they all already tucked away in their burrows and asleep? Who cares; I'll find them. Mr. Beck is more into birds, so we won't get in each other's way. Sniffing the ground close to the path, after a few meters I pick up a very promising scent: A rabbit has just hopped along this path, probably on its way to its burrow. I'm excited! There are sure to be several more rabbits in there; I'll catch one for sure. The scent gets stronger and stronger. I leave the path and trot towards some tall bushes by the side of a meadow.

Just here it has to be! I poke my nose through the grass, always searching for the entrance. Finally a tuft of grass in front of me gives way, revealing a deep hole. The smell is now intense; I can't help but let out a loud joyful cry. Hurrah!

"Hey, everything okay with you, Hercules?"

Mr Beck suddenly stands next to me.

"Everything's fine! I've just discovered my first rabbit burrow during my hunting dog career!"

"You know, rabbit burrows are quite cramped, right?"

What a stupid question.

"Of course I do. And now excuse me, I have work to do."

Instead of letting me enter the burrow in peace, Mr Beck sits directly in front of me. Annoying!

"You should be aware that you're not a dachshund, right?"

I take a deep breath. "Well, and? You see, how wrong Escherbach was: I may not be purebred, but I found the burrow immediately. So all my doubts about myself are unfounded. I am indeed a hunting dog through and through."

"Excuse me, you're misunderstanding me. I didn't mean to get into your pedigree discussion. What I wanted to say is that you are indeed larger than a dachshund. Not that you'd get stuck in the burrow. I wouldn't know how to get you out anyway. And at this time of day, there aren't many helpers around."

"Nonsense. Getting stuck - what do my claws have to do with it? I can't swing myself through ivy, but digging is a piece of cake with them. So don't worry about me. And now I need to get going, or the rabbits will be gone and we'll still be talking."

I stick my nose deep into the hole and start widening the entrance with my forelegs. It's a fantastic feeling! Finally, I've found my true calling. Too bad I'm not out hunting with a passionate hunter like Mr Beck; I have to make do with this cautious fellow.

After a few minutes, I'm almost completely underground. The rabbit smell is now so strong that my nose feels strained and twice its normal size. Itching has spread throughout my body, and I'm so excited that my heart races. Just a little bit further, then I've got them! I think I can even hear the rabbits in their den, probably frozen with fear and not thinking of escape. Almost too easy, this whole thing. I press my paws and nose into the suspected den - suddenly, the earth gives way beneath me, and I hang halfway out of the hole. Horrible! I'm in the den!

Then comes the big disappointment: although it's pitch black, my nose immediately reveals that the rabbits have already left. The smell isn't as distinct as it was a few minutes ago. Damn, I must have missed them by a hair! It's all Beck's fault! If I had started digging right away, I would've surely caught those companions down here. So they had plenty of time to dig themselves a new hole and escape. Annoying. All I can do now is look for another burrow.

I put the reverse gear on. Better said: I try to put the reverse gear on. Because actually, it's not that easy when you're hanging by your front paws like I am right now. I try to pull myself back with my hind legs so as to regain some forward momentum. But no matter how hard I push myself backward, nothing happens. I'm stuck in a bottleneck and can neither move forward nor backward. I give it another try, but after a while, I have to take a break because the heat is becoming unbearable. Plus, it's stuffy down here. My nose starts itching again, not because some rabbit has come back, but out of fear. How on earth am I going to get out of here?

Hopefully Mr Beck is still up there. I bark as loudly and clearly as possible under the circumstances. Can he even hear me? He himself says his vision isn't great. Maybe he can't hear well either. That would be a disaster. I'm getting hotter and hotter, it's almost unbearable. Stay calm, Carl-Leopold! Don't panic! At least someone knows where you are. Mr Beck will surely wonder why I haven't come back up. He won't leave without me. And if I've so disconcerted him that he's already gone? My stupid hunting dog chatter has probably really annoyed him. I keep barking. Dear, dear Mr Beck, all the foolish things I've ever said to you were definitely not meant in a bad way. You're a very dear friend of mine. In fact, my only one. Help!

"Hercules? Everything okay down there?"

Praise be! He heard me!

"No! I'm stuck!"

"What? I can barely understand you."

"I'M STUCK!"

Coughing fits, I feel like vomiting. Come on, Mr Beck, do something!

"Darn it. I knew this rabbit stunt was a bad idea. How are we going to get you out now?" He pauses. "Are you not too deep down?"

"No, about right. The tunnel is relatively high up."

"I'll see if I can find someone to help."

"Please don't leave me alone! I'm scared!"

"I need to find someone who can dig you out. Ideally a human. There's no other way; I definitely can't do it myself. Stay as calm as possible, otherwise you'll use up all your air. And try to relax."

Relax? Very funny. I'd like to see Mr Beck trying that. But he's right; we need help.

"Okay, but hurry!"

"I'm doing my best! Hold on!"

It probably feels like only a few minutes since Mr Beck started looking for help, but it feels like an eternity down here. It's completely silent, deathly quiet. I try to stay calm as Mr Beck advised, but I'm terrified. I manage to suppress my fear, though. Why did I act so stupid and get myself into this situation? Mr Beck was right. And I am a complete idiot. For hunting, at least. As Opili always said: Passion is a poor counselor. And pride too. Maybe I should just settle for being a pet, eat some sausage now and then, chase a pigeon here and there. Nothing dangerous. Dear Dachshund God, if you exist, please make sure Mr Beck finds someone to help me. I promise I'll be the bravest dog in the world from now on, always on a leash, never escape at night, and generally behave myself. And no more chasing rabbits.

Suddenly, I hear heavy footsteps right above me. Human feet! This must be my salvation! My prayer to the Dachshund God was apparently answered, and Mr Beck has found someone.

"Hey, Hercules! You won't believe who I've brought!"

This isn't the time for riddles, but I suppress this thought and am too weak to shout anyway.

"Willi. I've found Willi. He did swear at us both quite a bit, but he came back with us nonetheless. Bark once more so he understands what we want."

I gather all my strength and bark as loudly as I can.

"Ah, I see!" I hear Willis deep voice rumble from above. "Your little friend is stuck down there, right?"

I can't see what Mr. Beck is doing now, but I sure hope he's encouraging Willi in some way.

"Then I'll try to dig him out. Hopefully he's not too deep, without a shovel, digging with my bare hands will be difficult."

There's another loud rumble above me, Willis seems to have knelt down. After a while, I hear nothing, then the earth starts shaking again. Willis is digging. Thank God!

I hear Willis grunting and gasping, digging seems quite exhausting for humans. No wonder, without claws it must be hard to move the earth aside. But the tremors are getting closer and every now and then a little bit of earth falls from the cave ceiling onto my nose.

"Damn, you picked a prime spot for your underground excursion! The soil here is so clay-like - really exhausting!" Willis curses. Then he says nothing more but continues digging silently.

"Still going, Hercules?" Mr. Beck asks.

"Yes!" I reply briefly because by now I'm having trouble breathing.

"You're lucky Willis was sitting on his usual spot and hadn't had too much beer. It didn't take long to make him understand what I want."

Just as I'm about to tell Mr. Beck that despite all the speed, things are getting tight for me, I feel a gust of wind at my rear end.

"Finally!" Willis exclaims. "I've reached the tunnel. Now it's done!"

Although I can't see anything yet, Willis has already reached my back legs. I hear his heavy breathing almost directly behind my neck. He completely exposes my hindquarters and strokes me over the back.

"Damn, my friend, you're doing things. Now I'll carefully free your head, then it's done."

More earth falls on my nose, but since I know that's Willis digging right next to my snout, I stay calm. There! Willis lifts the cave cover and I'm finally freed. I shiver and look up. Oh no! I sit in a pretty deep pit. Carefully, Willis lifts me up and sets me at the edge of the big hole he dug for me. Then he climbs out himself and sits down next to me.

"Willi needs a break," Mr. Beck huffs as he approaches us. We're sitting side by side under the dim light of a street lamp some distance away.

"You're lucky your buddy found you, my friend. You wouldn't have gotten out otherwise." Mr. Beck looks exhausted. "I'm beat. I feel a bit nauseous. Well, no wonder, I'm not used to this kind of thing at my age, hahaha!" Willis runs his hand through his messy hair. Then he takes a deep breath and stares into the distance. "But now it's getting weird. And suddenly I feel... sick." He trails off - and collapses onto the grass. There he lies motionless. Oh no! Not again!

"What's wrong with him?"

"Whatever it is, it doesn't look good," Mr. Beck mumbles as he nudges Willis with his paw. No response. "Damn it, Willi, don't do that!"

I also get up, consider for a moment, and then jump onto Willis' torso. If he doesn't react, things are serious.

It's serious: Even when I run forward and lick Willis across the face, he doesn't move. Instead, he breathes quickly and irregularly. I realize I'm panicking.

"Beck, I think Willi is in a bad way. What do we do now?"

"Damn it!" Beck swears. "It's all your fault! If you hadn't gone into that stupid construction site, Willis wouldn't have had to dig him out. That was obviously too much for him." We desperately need help.

I hang my head. Beck is right. It's all my fault. And not a single person is in sight.

"Were there any other people around earlier?" I ask Mr. Beck, but he just shakes his head.

"No one. Not even couples. Nobody."

Willis lets out a weak groan. Then the lightbulb goes off. Think, Carl-Leopold, think. Who can help us now?

"I've got it!" I exclaim excitedly. "I put Willis in this situation, so I'll get him out of it too. You stay with him, so he's not alone. See you soon!"

Before Mr. Beck can say anything, I'm already on my way.

TWENTY

Out of the park, down the small street all the way to its end. Then left, towards the big tree at the corner that smells so impressively of the black Doberman I've admired from afar many times. Cross a larger street - at this time of day, there are no cars around. I run as fast as I can without losing my bearings. At the next intersection, I'm unsure for a moment, but then I smell the bakery I passed with Daniel and Carolin. Right, I'm still in the right place. This street is quite long, I need to follow it until I reach a sharp bend. When I finally do, I'm out of breath.

And suddenly, I smell the familiar stench of a laundry service. I've made it - straight across from the Wagner practice is an apartment on the top floor. Fortunately, their windows are lit up. Dr. Wagner must be home. Without hesitating, I crouch down on the sidewalk in front of the house and start barking loudly. Non-stop. Eventually someone will hear me. Now, don't give up. That's for Willis!

It doesn't take long: Soon a window opens, unfortunately on the second floor, and a woman looks out.

"What the hell kind of noise is that? You stupid dog, get lost!"

Unfazed, I keep barking.

"Get lost or I'll call the police!"

I don't care. For a change, I start howling. The woman closes the window. A while passes. Nope. Keep going, Carl-Leopold, keep going. It's about Willis!

Then the front door opens and the woman comes out onto the street - accompanied by Dr. Wagner! They talk to each other as they walk towards me.

"I don't know how you came to think this is one of my patients, but I'll take a look."

"Well, thanks for looking. After all, he does belong to a friend of mine."

The two stand in front of me. Wagner stares at me. I stare back. Hopefully he recognizes me. Otherwise, they might really call the police.

"Indeed. I know this dog. He belongs to one of my friends."

That's not even close to true! Carolin isn't your friend! But in this situation, I don't want to be too picky. Instead, I jump up and greet Wagner with a wagging tail. Wagner briefly pets me and then looks at me thoughtfully.

"What's going on with you, Hercules?"

If only I could just tell him... But instead, I jump up at him again and pull on his sleeve. The woman who was previously so angry now looks amused.

"He seems to want you to come with us."

I jump up and down. Okay, that's a lie, but the main thing is he agrees to come with me. And I probably lure him with Carolin. That it's not about a pretty young woman but a rather ugly old man will become clear to him soon enough.

"Where is Carolin? At home?"

I take three steps forward, then turn back to Wagner and wag my tail. Wagner shakes his head in bewilderment, then pulls out his phone and dials a number. He holds the phone to his ear and listens. After a while, he puts it away and slips it back into his pocket. "Hmm, I can't get through to her, only her voicemail. Okay, Hercules, I guess I have to follow you. Wait a moment, I'll quickly grab my jacket."

The woman looks at Wagner in complete perplexity. "Hey, that was just a joke! Do you really think the dog is asking you to accompany him?"

"Yes, I do believe so, Mrs. Loretti. This dog doesn't jest. He means it seriously."

He turns around and goes back into the house. I'm thrilled. Wagner might be an idiot, but he knows his way around dogs.

A moment later, he's standing next to me again.

"Well, Hercules. I'm really curious what the reason for your dramatic entrance is."

I wag my tail again and gallop off, a quick glance over my shoulder: Wagner follows dutifully. On the way back, I don't have to think about where I want to go anymore; that simplifies things immensely. Soon we pass Carolin's house, and Dr. Wagner wants to turn onto the driveway leading to the entrance. I bark briefly - nope!

"Aha, so not here. Then?" Wagner looks at me questioningly, and I continue down the path in the park. Now, no slacking off, we've almost made it. Finally, I steer myself straight towards Mr. Beck, who is sitting by Willi as instructed. Willi is still lying in the grass just as I left him. Mr. Beck stares at me.

"Goodness, what a brilliant idea: you called the vet."

Now Wagner approaches us too. "What happened here? Did you find the man? And did you call for help? That's incredible." He kneels down next to Willi. "Hello? Can you hear me? Hello?"

Willi doesn't respond. Wagner pulls up his sleeve and places his ear on Willi's chest. Then he starts massaging him, or rather, hitting him. It looks quite brutal. I hope Wagner knows what he's doing! After a good while of massaging and pounding, Wagner puts his ear back on the chest. Apparently something seems to be working better now, because Wagner smiles contentedly. He sits up and pulls out his phone. Then he dials a number.

"Hello, this is Marc Wagner. I've just found an unconscious male person in the Helvetia Park, aged between 50 and 60. It's difficult to conduct a thorough examination, but I suspect it could be a heart attack. The heartbeat was very irregular anyway, and it seems to have improved after the heart massage."

He listens again. Someone on the other end of the line seems to ask him something.

"Not directly. I'm a vet. I've put him in the stable side position now, the heart massage isn't needed anymore. You can find us to the right of the playground. See you soon!"

Wagner hangs up, then looks at Mr. Beck and me seriously. "I think this man had a heart attack. Definitely something serious. It's good that you called for help." He pets me first, then Mr. Beck on the head. "Well done! But... I must say, no one is going to believe this crazy story, least of all your wife, Hercules." He raises an eyebrow. "Where was she anyway? And why are you running around in the park at night?" He studies me thoughtfully. "It's a pity you can't speak."

He pulls out his phone again and dials a number. "Hello, Nina, this is Marc. Say, do you happen to have your girlfriend Carolin's phone number?" "Hm, yes."

How smart of Wagner - he obviously called Nina.

"No, I just wanted to tell her something that might be of interest to her. So... if you'd be so kind..." Another pause. "Hey, Carolin! What a coincidence - you're with Nina. Well, this sounds strange now, but I'm here in the Helvetia Park with Hercules." He pauses again. "Would you like to come by?"

The paramedics load Willi onto a stretcher. Wagner chats briefly with the human doctor who just examined him.

"I think it was indeed a heart attack. You were very lucky he came along, Mr. Colleague."

"Well, usually my patients are much smaller or much larger and often quite hairy, but in an emergency, things do get done without fur too." They both laugh.

"Alright then, we're taking the gentleman to the university clinic. See you!"

Marc Wagner nods briefly, then turns to Carolin and Nina who have joined us by now. "Good evening, ladies!

Carolin says nothing at first but picks me up. "Herkules! What are you doing here? What on earth happened?"

Wagner grins a bit awkwardly. "So, let me tell it my way: Hercules and the cat were out for a walk in the park when they found this homeless man. Smart animals, they realized something was wrong. And then Hercules, knowing only one doctor, called... me."

Nina bursts into laughter. "Excuse me, Marc, but do you really think that's what happened? That you and Hercules came across a homeless guy and decided to save him? Or that you attacked him? Have you been drinking?"

What an insult! And why does it sound so crazy? This is pretty much the truth! I wonder why Nina seems so angry at Wagner - she sounds quite put out. I'm just curious why.

Wagner defends himself. "Why so hostile, Nina? What do you think happened? That I kidnapped Carolin's dog, took the cat with me, and then attacked this poor old man? Or that our two friends here attacked him? I can only tell you what I saw myself. And all I saw was Hercules barking at my practice for a long time until I came out. Then he led me here, and there lay this man, apparently guarded by the cat." He pauses. "I have no idea how they got from Carolin's place to the park or what happened to the man. But I suspect it was a heart attack. There's no sign of alcohol on him; I couldn't smell any." Oh, and by the way, I'm not drunk either, he adds.

Nina takes a deep breath, Carolin squeezes me tight.

"I can't explain how Hercules got out of the apartment, but thankfully he found you. I know this sick man - he stood one day in front of our house looking a bit confused. Thank you for helping him." She looks at both of them and chuckles - a little cruelly, as far as I can tell. "Why? What could have happened to him? Anyone else could've called an ambulance. I doubt Marc's veterinary studies helped much there."

Marc Wagner grins even wider. "Dear Nina, if you ever find yourself lying unconscious in the park, you'll be glad anyone at all found you - even a veterinarian."

Exactly! Better than a psychologist, I'd say. Even if I don't really know what that is.

"So ladies, the main thing has been said, I'm leaving now. Have a nice evening." 
He nods at Nina and Carolin amicably and heads towards the park exit. Five steps away, he turns back around.

"Oh, Carolin - Wednesday evening, half seven? I'll pick you up, okay? See you later!"

Carolin nods and waves him off briefly in goodbye. Nina says nothing. At least not as long as we're still in the park. On the way home, she talks all the more.

"You have a date? With Marc? Why didn't you tell me? "

"I wanted to, but..."

"I mean, you come by extra just to tell me about your great day at the Elbe with Jens and how romantic it was and how well you got along, and not a single word about your date with Marc?"

"Yes, but..." Carolin tries to say again, but Nina doesn't let her finish.

"And I thought my stupid dog would be sick. All you wanted was Marc's number! Have you even asked yourself how I feel when I find out like this? Honestly, that's just awful - honestly!"

"I wanted to tell you. That's why I came by extra tonight. But then Marc called, and I didn't get a chance."

"Who cares? We've only talked for an hour. Of course that's not enough time to get rid of something unimportant."

"Why, you asked Jens right away - nothing else interested you. "

"Of course not. How would I know there was anything else to report?"

"He invited me to a concert, that's all."

"Oh, that's nice. And so artistic. "

"Plus, you said he wasn't your type. I couldn't guess that wouldn't be true."

"Right. You couldn't have guessed it. But maybe if you'd asked me once in a while how I was doing, instead of always talking about yourself and your love problems... And when I came by the other day and really needed a friend, all of a sudden cooking with buddy Daniel became incredibly important to you, and I even felt like I was interrupting." Sweet friend, thank you. "I'll remember that next time you need someone to talk to." She turns around and walks away.

Carolin stays behind. "Darn it. I knew this would cause trouble. It seems the Dr. still has some strings to pull." She sighs.  "Let's go home, sweetie. Then maybe you can explain exactly how you got in here."

I'm happy to do that. But could someone please explain what exactly is going on between Nina and Carolin? Nina found the vet just as annoying as I did originally. And why is she angry? And apart from all that: What's happened to Jens? Does Carolin even know what she wants? I suspect not.

Upon arriving in the apartment, I walk guiltily into the bedroom and sit down on a chair next to the open balcony door. Carolin looks back and forth between me and evidence A and B.

"You're not going to tell me you managed to get this door open by yourself, right? That's incredible! You could perform in a circus." But how did you even get onto the balcony? Did you jump? "Sweetie, are you crazy? You're no cat! You don't have nine lives!"

Huh? Nine lives? Does a cat really have nine lives? That would certainly explain Dr. Beck's behavior. Or is that just another human saying?

Carolin closes the balcony door and pulls the curtain shut. "I'm so tired, my sweetie. And a little sad. Because of Nina and Marc. And a little happy. Because of Marc and Jens. What a mess! Hopefully I'll bring some order to it. What do you think? But now I'm going to bed. I won't be able to solve this problem today anyway." Goodnight, sweetie!"

I crawl into my basket and wrap myself up in my cozy blanket. I'm tired too. But my head is racing. First Daniel, then Jens. Or maybe Dr. Wagner? How can it be that just a few weeks ago there wasn't even a single candidate for my future master, and now suddenly there are three? And none of them did I choose myself, although I had such a great plan.

In my tired dog mind, a sobering realization dawns: the taste men have for young women and little dachshunds seems to be quite different.

ONE AND TWENTY-ONE

As we enter, Willi sits up in bed surprised.

"Wow, you're visiting me! That's great!"

"Well, I hope it doesn't strain you too much, but the sister said that was okay."

"No, I'm happy about it. It wasn't something I expected." She's referring to Carolin, of course. "You are the young woman with the violin workshop, right?"

"That's right. My name is Carolin Neumann." She extends her hand, which he squeezes briefly.

"Very nice to meet you. Wilhelm Schamoni, but everyone calls me Willi. And what about your little friend down there?" He points at me.

"This is Herkules."

Carolin sits down on a chair next to Willi's bed and takes me onto her lap. Willi stretches his arm out and pets me. Phew! Obviously he's not mad at me. I'm relieved.

"You're such a cutie! And now you've saved Willi, right?"

Carolin raises an eyebrow. "Well, how did you know that? Did you see Herkules arrive with Dr. Wagner?"

"No," comes a voice from behind us, "I just told Willi about it."

Carolin turns around. Marc Wagner is leaning against the doorway, grinning at us.  "It seems we both had the same idea. I thought some visitors would be good for Willi now."

"Oh, hi, Marc," Carolin says, a bit less enthusiastically than I'd expected. "Are you happy to see me?" Marc pets my head. "You're our little hero dog, right?"

Well, that's a bit much. I'm just glad Willi doesn't think I'm responsible for everything. But Willi is actually looking quite cheerful. Maybe heroes don't always have to be dogs?

"What a crazy day - yesterday Herkules and I saved each other: Before I had my heart attack, I freed Herkules from a rabbit hole. He was stuck and couldn't get out. I noticed it only because the fat cat suddenly appeared on my bench.  I started to wonder if maybe something wasn't right with my head. After all, this is the second time I've felt like the cat wanted to take me somewhere. But it turned out to be true. Amazing, isn't it? How animals help each other. They seem to be real friends."

Marc Wagner nods. "Yes, many people underestimate animals. And I have to admit, I was surprised yesterday too. It had something of Lassie about it."

Lassie? Who or what is that? Carolin and Marc laugh, and even Willi lets out a weak but happy whinny. That must be strange.

So, Herr Schamoni, I must unfortunately ask you to leave now. I will examine you again shortly, and besides, you also need a lot of rest. She turns to us - and her stern gaze remains on me. "Animals are not allowed here! Please don't bring the dog back."

Carolin looks guilty. "Oh, I'm sorry, we're leaving right away too. But Hercules discovered Herr Schamoni yesterday, so I thought... out of the ordinary..."

The woman shakes her head. "No exceptions. You can come back tomorrow, but without the dog."

Carolin sets me down on the ground and stands up. "So, Willi, I wish you a speedy recovery! Perhaps I'll drop by again."

"That would make me very happy!" He squeezes Carolin's hand in farewell.

Marc Wagner also shakes his hand. "Goodbye, Willi, get well soon!"

"Thank you so much for the visit, see you around."

Willi waves briefly, and we find ourselves back on the hospital corridor.

"Shall we go get a coffee?"

Carolin hesitates. "Well, I don't think it's a good idea to have a dog on hospital grounds."

"Let's go to the main exit then. There's a cafe just two corners further."

And so it comes to pass that the two sit down at a cozy wooden table while I curl up under the corresponding bench. They chat about trivial things first, then Carolin's voice takes on a strange, dark tone.

"Marc, I'd like to cancel our meeting next Wednesday."

Oh no! That is definitely not a good sign - and especially now that I'm just starting to get used to the veterinarian! Ugh. Marc is also not exactly thrilled about this turn of events.

"But why? I thought you were looking forward to the concert."

"Yes, I was. But..." "Is it because of Nina, or...?"

"No, not really. It's just... kind of a mix, I guess. So, yes, maybe."

Marc shakes his head. "I had a feeling after her performance in the park yesterday. I didn't quite understand it then, but now... well, let's just say there wasn't anything between Nina and me. We went out a few times, but that was it."

"Apparently she has different ideas."

"That's what I've noticed. But I don't really know why. Honestly, our last date was more of a disaster, after which she never got in touch again." Marc reaches for Carolin's hand. "Please, don't cancel. I just want to get to know you privately. Not always only when some emergency brings us together. I find you very nice, and I promise to behave myself like a gentleman. There won't be any cause for complaint." He raises his hand in the air. "Big Indian honor!"

Carolin smiles but pulls her hand away. "Really, Marc. I'm glad you invited me. But my life has been so stressful and complicated lately, and I don't want to slip right into another problem. Nina is my best friend; she helped me when it was really bad - not that long ago. I find you very nice too, but maybe it's not the right time right now to get to know each other better."

Jail! The stupid Nina! There are plenty of other men in town. And Mr. Beck has already noted that Nina never had any trouble getting to know a lot of them. Why is she interfering with us now? Marc seems to agree. He looks as if Mr. von Eschersbach just gave him a dog whip.

"And I can't do anything about it? I could talk to Nina."

Carolin firmly shakes her head. "No, please don't do that. It's not just Nina. I've had a lot of thoughts running through my head all night long. And then around four in the morning, it became clear: I need more peace in my life. And first, I need to find out what's important to me. To figure out who I am." Please don't be angry with me, I can't help it.

Marc looks sad but says no more. The two sit silently side by side for a while, then Carolin bids farewell, and we leave poor Wagner sitting alone in the café.

"She says she needs to figure out who she is. Can you believe that?" I complain to Mr. Beck upon my return home.

"Have you ever heard such nonsense? She's Carolin Neumann, for heaven's sake! She must have a family tree somewhere; there will be names and all that."

I am truly astounded. And this from Carolin - something I would never have expected.

"Now, calm down, little guy. You misunderstood her completely. Of course, Carolin still knows her name."

He thinks I'm stupid. I know what I heard!

"No, no, no! She said it literally, Mr. Beck, literally!" I must find out who I am too.

Suddenly a new thought strikes me: Perhaps the poor Carolin is like me? And she's not really pure-blood either? Or at least not as much as one would expect from humans if they don't know their father? In light of this, it makes perfect sense that she is being so cautious in her choice of partner. She can't be sure who would be the best match for her. But when I present Beck with this new theory, he bursts out laughing.

"Herkules, you're one-of-a-kind! It's obvious now that there are fundamental differences between humans and dogs. Humans are thinking beings!"

Well, thanks a lot! As if I don't think! I growl a little.

"That was to be expected; you always get upset when people try to explain things to you in a reasonable way. But of course, humans - or rather, some humans - are self-reflective. That means they constantly think about themselves. Who am I? Where do I come from? Where am I going?"

"So, that doesn't sound so special, if I may say so."

"I mean metaphorically! Carolin wants to know what makes her unique as a human. What sets her apart from others. What's important to her. Stuff like that."

Mr. Beck sighs. "No, believe me, Herkules. As long as Carolin feels she needs to find herself, we can bring over all sorts of guys - it won't help. Apparently some people only feel good with another person when they're also comfortable being alone. And that's what Carolin seems to need right now. So let's be patient. If she keeps on like this, we'll soon have no more potential candidates left, and we'll have to start all over again. But next time I'll come up with a different trick."

Mr. Beck shakes his head. "No, Herkules. As long as Carolin feels the need to discover herself, there's no use bringing in any particular type of guy. She needs time to figure things out on her own. So let's just be patient."

"I hope you're wrong for once," I say. "But there's still a chance: Jens. At least our trip to the river was simply beautiful, maybe something will come of it with them."

"Maybe," Mr Beck nods thoughtfully - but his gaze betrays that he doesn't believe it.

TWENTY-TWO

"Can you give me a reasonable reason why you didn't tell me?" Daniel sounds angry. "I don't understand you, Carolin. We were sitting here the other day and I was completely honest with you. I would have expected the same from you, right?" He's clearly upset.

Meanwhile, this day had started off quite harmlessly. When we went downstairs to the workshop, there was a large bouquet of flowers on Carolin's workbench. Carolin is delighted - until she sees Daniel standing with a frowning face at his own table. Then things happen quickly: It turns out that the bouquet came from Jens. And Daniel is jealous. Very jealous.

"I didn't mean to hurt you," he says. "Well, congratulations. That was a super idea. If you're interested, now I've really hurt you. If you had just told me straight away that you were interested in someone else, it would have been much easier for me. But now I feel like a complete idiot."

Carolin swallows. "But why? All I did was..."

"Because I broke off my engagement and gave up the understanding one," he replies bitterly. "Man, I can't even think about it without feeling sick."

I guess that's not such a good idea with us.

Maybe it's better this way.

"So now calm down again - that wasn't quite like that at all," I say. "I thought our cooking was nice too. And the fact that I couldn't imagine anything else has nothing to do with Jens. I only saw him once." What would I have told you then? There was nothing there.

"Well, don't pretend you don't know what I mean," Daniel retorts. "I had no chance. And I wish I knew. I thought we were friends."

Carolin's cheeks flush red. "Of course we're friends! And I can imagine that the situation is difficult for you too. But it's difficult for me as well, and I don't think I'm the bad guy here!"

"I haven't claimed to be," I reply.

"You haven't?" Carolin raises an eyebrow. "I do."

"Man, what a mess!" Daniel slams his fist so hard on the table that I jump back in fear. Then he gets up and storms out of the room, slamming the door behind him. Carolin and I are left behind. She leans over to me and pulls me out from under the workbench.

"Pretty loud, wasn't it?" she strokes my hair. "Well, looks like all my friends are angry with me now. But at least I still have you."

I look Carolin in the eye. Of course, it's flattering that I'm as important a friend to her as Daniel and Nina. Nevertheless, I hope things settle down quickly. I'm more of a harmony person. Carolin seems to be reading my thoughts.

"Don't worry," she reassures me. "I promise we'll all make up soon. And to make that happen as quickly as possible, I'm going to call Nina now and make plans with her." What do you think, good plan? I wag my tail. "Ah, great. We'll do it like that then."

Obviously, Nina is also eager to speak with Carolin. As soon as Carolin calls, we're already on our way to our favorite café around the corner. When we arrive, Nina is already there and waves us over. Quite friendly, I imagine.

"Hello, Nina! So glad it worked out spontaneously," Carolin greets her.

"Yes, a fortunate coincidence. I was just thinking that fate wanted us to finally talk."

Both laugh. That does indeed look like a reconciliation. The waitress approaches our table.

"I don't know how you feel, but I think our meeting calls for two glasses of champagne."

Nina nods. "Absolutely. Special situations require special measures. Bring us please two glasses?"

I'd also like one!, I want to say. But all I get is a trip to the dog water bowl they have at this café.

Two glasses with a clear liquid arrive, and the ladies reach for them immediately.

"So, my dear, to us!" Nina raises her glass to Carolin. "Yes, to us!"

They take a big sip.

"Honestly, I'm glad you finally called," Carolin says. "It was getting creepy. But tomorrow I would have picked up the phone too. I still can't believe we argued over a guy." Ugh, she shakes her head.

Carolin smiles. "But what I regret most is that I've been texting you with my bad luck all the time lately. That was wrong of me. And that's why... I promise to change! She raises her right hand. "Sworn!"

"Well, okay, but in retrospect, I must admit I also reacted quite sharply," Nina says. "After all, I had indeed told you that Marc meant nothing to me. I don't know why I flipped out in the park the other day. There was definitely a lot of wounded pride involved."

"Honestly, Nina, if I'd known how much Marc still means to you, I would have dropped the whole thing right away," Carolin replies. "And if it makes you feel any better: I canceled our date with him."

"Oh!" Nina sounds shocked. "But you shouldn't! Not just because of me. I admit I was angry, but if it really sparked something between us, then there's nothing higher than that. Fate, as they say."

"No, not only because of you," Carolin corrects her. "At least not only. Of course, I've been thinking about Marc a lot after our argument with you. And then suddenly I felt like I needed some time to calm down after the chaos of the past few weeks. A new man just doesn't fit in right now. Not even one as great as Marc."

What can I say? Mr Beck really knows his stuff. It's always chilling how well he assesses situations.

Nina thinks for a moment. "Normally, I'd agree with you. But maybe you should reconsider. Unfortunately, attractive men aren't exactly lying around everywhere. Who are the others then?"

"Well, the evening when I wanted to cook with Daniel was actually more romantic in mind," I admit. "I didn't tell you before because I thought you'd laugh at me. But lately there's been some sparks between Daniel and me. So I thought I should just give it a try."

Каролин вздыхает: «Ну, ты представляешь? Я уже сегодня почувствовала на себе его разочарование. Даниэль узнал, что я встречаюсь с Йенсом. Так что я сама ему рассказала, потому что Йенс прислал мне розы в мастерскую. Ну, Даниэль был далеко не в восторге».

Утром я действительно была немного запутанной. Но, видимо, доставка подарков, таких как собачья колбаска или цветы в больших количествах, входит в ритуалы ухаживания человека.

— Это меня не удивляет. Я всегда знала, что Даниэль в тебя влюблен. Но то, что ты всерьез обдумываешь... — она делает паузу. — Нет! Давай будем честными, Даниэль просто слишком добр!

Одна вещь ясна: если господин Бек когда-нибудь понадобится новый хозяин или хозяйка, Нина была бы идеальным выбором. Я редко встречала человека и животное, которые так часто были бы на одной волне. К сожалению, нельзя сказать то же самое о ее любви к животным. Так что этот тандем, скорее всего, не состоится.

— Ты жестока. Бедный Даниэль.

— Неверно сказано. Даниэлю пора выйти из своей раковины. Иначе у него не будет шансов с другими женщинами. Но то, что я нахожу гораздо более интересным, — продолжает она, — это то, что ты теперь еще раз встретишься с Йенсом или подпадешь под его магнетизм?

Каролин вздыхает: «Ну, сегодня вечером у меня с ним встреча запланирована, так что я, наверное, скажу ему, что не хочу больше видеть его».

Нина задыхается: «Ты сумасшедшая? Этот замечательный парень? После вашего романтического дня на Эльбе? Пожалуйста, давай измерим твой пульс, может быть, у тебя что-то не так».

— Почему? Я объяснила тебе проблему. Конечно, поездка была прекрасной, и она была очень романтичной и волнующей. Но я наслаждалась ситуацией в первую очередь. Не знаю, смогу ли я серьезно влюбиться в Йенса.

Нина качает головой: «Так что, по крайней мере, дай мне свой номер телефона, если ты будешь сообщать ему плохие новости. Я бы сейчас очень его использовала». Они смеются.

— Кстати, я хочу задать тебе один вопрос.

Каролин смотрит Нине через край стакана. — А что именно произошло с Марком?

— Ах, это глупая история. Я, наверное, все испортила.

Нина делает глубокий вдох: «Так вот почему ты не рассказывала мне об этом вечером, когда мы готовили с Даниэлем. Я хотела рассказать тебе об этом во время нашей встречи, но тогда Даниэль был вне себя из-за собаки всего несколько минут, так что у меня не было времени. Это до сих пор немного неловко для меня».

— Ты действительно не рассказывала.

— Да, я понимаю. Я хотела сказать это тогда, когда мы сидели на пледе, а Марк принес нам брокколи и картофельное пюре в имбирном баре. Там рядом села семья с двумя маленькими капризными детьми. Один ребенок постоянно плакал, а другой — маленький хулиган, лет трех. Он бегал туда-сюда между людьми и развалил на мне весь мой картофель. Ну, ты понимаешь... Я просто четко дала понять родителям, что думаю о их детях. Хорошо, возможно, я была немного громкой. Но мои нервы были на пределе.

— Марк не был в восторге от твоей реакции, верно?

Нина кивает: «Он был шокирован и закричал на меня, что я должна успокоиться. Это были маленькие дети, и это не было намеренно. Он сказал это перед другими людьми. Я встала и оставила его сидящим с горой грязного картофельного пюре. Затем я пошла в следующий автобус».

— И с тех пор ты больше не слышала от Марка?

Нина пожимает плечами: «Нет. А я тоже не пыталась связаться с ним. И потом я снова встретила его, когда он звонил мне вечером, ищя тебя.

— О боже! Это действительно ужасная история! И он больше не звал?

— Нет. И я тоже не звала. Конечно, я бы хотела... но я не могла. А потом я снова встретила его, и оказалось, что он договорился о встрече с тобой. Это было слишком для меня.

— Я понимаю, и мне очень жаль. Я даже не подозревала.

Каролин и Нина смотрят друг на друга и хихикают. Затем они отвечают одновременно: «Да, пожалуйста, еще два бокала шампанского!»

"So, then, keep being frustrated. It won't be your last frustrating experience with people, I guarantee you that." With these words, Mr Beck turns around and walks away again.

Let him be. He wasn't much of a comfort anyway.

I stand up and decide to simply ignore Mr Beck's wise advice. Of course, a dog can be part of a human family. I'm even quite sure a cat could be too, she'd just have to want it. But that brings me back to my initial thought: how do I get myself into a family? Maybe it's not too late for Jens and Carolin. After all, they're meeting up tonight. Perhaps I can somehow create a good atmosphere? After all, Carolin herself said she had a great day with Jens. In any case, I have to find some way to worm my way into this date! So, I spend the rest of the afternoon lurking. Just in case Carolin leaves without me.

In fact, she closes up earlier than usual and goes upstairs to change. I follow her heels and don't leave her side, even when she disappears into the bathroom again.

"Hey Hercules! What's going on with you today? You're so clingy." She shooes me in front of the bathroom door and locks it.

Fine, if she comes out again, she'll have to pass by me, unless she climbs out the window.

She doesn't - after a while, she appears again, now wearing that black, short dress we discussed earlier. She's also piled her hair up on top of her head in a little mountain. I gain hope - if Carolin puts so much effort into her appearance, maybe she'll reconsider going out with Jens.

The doorbell rings, and shortly after, Jens stands at our door. Carolin greets him with a kiss on the cheek, then bends down to me.

"So Hercules, you're staying here today. So be good and don't jump off the balcony again!"

No way! I'm not staying here. I run over to Jens and make doggy faces. He laughs.

"It seems like your pal has definitely decided to accompany us. He's probably taken a liking to me since the sausage incident."

"That's not even an option. He stays here."

Ugh! How unkind of you! But I'm not going to let that slide. As Carolin opens the apartment door, I run off and past her into the hallway. Downstairs, I'm lucky: Meyer the parakeet is just coming in, so I can slip outside. There's also the car without a roof waiting for me, smiling invitingly. If I didn't have such short legs, I'd jump right in, but I have to wait for Jens and Carolin.

"Hey you naughty dog!" Carolin approaches me and scolds. "If I say you stay here, then you stay here!"

She reaches out to grab me, but before she can get a hold of my collar, I run to Jens and rub myself against his legs while trying to bark as friendly as possible. Jens bends down and picks me up.

"Well little guy, I'd take you with me, but your lady is being very strict today. I'm almost afraid of her."

Carolin laughs. "Against two men in agreement, I don't stand a chance. So let's go."

He puts me in the footwell of the passenger side, then both of them get in and we drive off. Persistence does pay off!

When we arrive at the restaurant, I wonder why Carolin didn't want to take me with her. After all, it's a garden restaurant, and at first glance, I see two dogs under other tables: an old boxer and a very pretty retriever lady who winks at us as we sit down at the neighboring table. I lie down so I can see her well. Maybe I shouldn't always only think about Carolin's heart, but from time to time also consider my own. On the other hand, of course, I need to be extra attentive today, if I want to know what Carolin tells. I want to intervene when she starts with her "I-need-to-find-myself" nonsense again.

But the conversation remains innocently focused on food and drinks. Carolin orders water, Jens laughs.

"I thought we'd order something a bit fizzy to celebrate this beautiful evening. And not just the fizz in your mineral water."

"You won't believe it, but I've already had two glasses of champagne with Nina this morning. Sort of a reconciliation ritual, since we had some stress. That's why I'm sticking to non-alcoholic drinks today."

"As you wish. I don't want to pressure you. Why did you have an argument?"

"Oh, it was a stupid story. I sort of accidentally made a date with someone Nina likes very much. She got mad."

"So, so, an unplanned date. It's clear to me that you're a sought-after woman."

He laughs and takes Carolin's hand. She hesitates, but before she can clearly pull her hand away, I start barking. Carolin bends down to me.

"Shh, Hercules, what's wrong?"

"Maybe another dog?"

"Hmm, you're not usually a barker. Something must have scared you."

Yes, that's right, something did scare me. The prospect of Carolin driving off the next guy before he even got started. After all, I had successfully steered this situation around. I just hope it doesn't continue like this for the whole evening.

It does continue like this for the entire evening. Every time I sense that Carolin is about to steer the conversation in the wrong direction, I get fidgety. And that happens every ten minutes or so. Carolin is already getting quite annoyed.

"Hey, what's going on with you?" She hisses at me. "I wanted you to stay home, and it would have been better if you had. You're being incredibly naughty! If you keep this up, we'll lock you in the car."

Okay, that would be bad. Maybe I should tone it down a bit. I lie down again nicely under Carolin's chair. The retriever at the neighboring table looks me over curiously.

"Hey little guy, do you have a bladder problem?"

"No, me? Why?"

"Because you're always moving around."

How embarrassing. This beautiful woman thinks I'm incontinent. That can't be allowed to stand.

"So, I'm fidgety because I want to protect my lady from making a big mistake."

"Like the guy over there?" She points at the man. "He doesn't look so bad."

"That's true. But we have the opposite problem. I think she wants to get rid of him."

"Huh. Well, she has her reasons."

"I don't think so. She likes him, but she wants to find herself first. That's totally crazy, right?"

"Little guy, may I give you a piece of advice?"

"Of course."

I expect good advice from an attractive woman in this situation, especially since she might have been in the same spot as Carolin once.

"Stay out of human affairs. It only causes trouble and looks pretty silly."

I've had it. One could almost think that the lady has been briefed by Mr Beck. I'm humiliated and hide again under Carolyn's chair. Well, then do whatever you want! But don't say I didn't warn you later on.

For the rest of the evening, I spend almost silently under Carolyn's chair. However, Carolyn doesn't make any further attempts to deliver Jens the bad news. Does she have second thoughts? As they finally stand up to leave, Jens makes a proposal that pleases me particularly well.

"How about we take a walk with Hercules again? The last time we were by the Elbe, now maybe we can show him the Alster, right? And anyway, he's been remarkably calm for an hour." Your little curtain lecture seemed to have worked, I guess. We should reward that. What do you say?"

"Yes, why not? That's a good idea."

My heart beats faster. I'm now certain Carolyn has changed her mind. Otherwise, she would have thanked him politely.

The lake called Alster lies right next to the restaurant. We stroll along the broad path directly by the shore. Normally, I'd just take off running and explore the area, but of course, I also want to hear what they're talking about. So I stay put for now. And then - Jens puts his arm around Carolyn! My heart is racing now, so excited am I. How will Carolyn react?

She doesn't do anything at first. A good sign. The two continue walking, and I follow them.

"You know," Carolyn starts to say something but then falls silent again. Oh no! Not a good sign?

"What do you mean?" Jens stops. The two are now looking directly at each other, and he takes their hands.

"So, I find you very nice, Jens. But I think I'm not quite ready yet. And I'm afraid of giving you false hopes."

"How do you mean that?"

"Well, we had a very romantic day by the Elbe, and tonight, except for Hercules's little outbursts, it was also very beautiful. But I think you might be expecting more from me than I can offer right now. And I don't want to disappoint you. So I think it's better to be straightforward. I can't fall in love just yet. Before I consider getting back into a relationship, I need to figure some things out about myself."

Jens lets go of her hands. "Hmm."

He doesn't say anything else. Ugh. I'd rather hide under a bush than deal with this situation.

"Are you angry now?"

"No, just surprised."

"Yeah, that's what I thought. Of course, it never would have occurred to me."

"No, not like that. I'm surprised that you've seriously considered a relationship and are worried about my wanting one. "

"Then do you not want one?"

Jens laughs. "No, of course not. I already have a girlfriend."

Wow! We're obviously looking at Thomas number 2 here.

"Yeah... but... I didn't know that." Carolyn sounds completely flabbergasted. She has every right to be.

"Well, listen, girl, don't you ever read the Oder or Gala? Bunte?"

"No, honestly, not really."

"Fine," Jens says in a condescending tone, "then don't. But if you did, you'd know that I've been with Alexa von Schöning, a very successful model, for four years now."

"Yeah, but... what did you want from me? Why did you even meet up with me?"

"Because I find you adorable and because I like to have some fun. Alexa knows this, it's okay for her. I thought you knew that too."

Good thing I can't speak right now. Because I'm at a loss for words. He's worse than Thomas! At least Thomas had the decency to feel guilty. Carolyn is speechless as well.

"Come on, Carolin, laugh!" Jens pats her. I growl at him. "Get your hands off that woman right now!"

"Oh man, you're really getting on my nerves today. What's wrong with you? I thought you liked me."

"Yeah," Carolyn says almost monotone, "I thought I liked you too. You can be so mistaken. I'd like to go home now."

"Okay, let's go then. I don't know why you're upset though. Nothing has even happened yet. Which is a real shame." Jens grins. Carolyn glares at him.

We ride back in the open carriage. My ears flutter in the wind, and it feels amazing. Otherwise, nothing good feels right now. I feel like a complete idiot. I wanted to set Carolin up with this guy. Unbelievable! Apparently, I have no people skills either. Well, at least Carolin doesn't, but that's only a small consolation.

Before the house, Jens stops. Carolyn wants to say goodbye, when he leans forward and gets very close to her.

"Carolin, seriously. You and me - there's definitely some chemistry here. Let's give it a try at least. I mean, I really want you. That you're hesitating now only makes the whole thing more interesting."

Carolin says nothing and reaches for the door handle. Then Jens suddenly grabs her, holds her tight, and starts kissing her on the mouth. Carolin screams and tries to push him away, but he won't let go of her hands and continues kissing her.

I'm completely shocked - this can't be happening!

But the shock doesn't last long: I jump up from the floorboard towards them and bite Jens right where it hurts most. He yells in pain and tries to hit me, but he has to let go of Carolin first. She uses the opportunity, slams the door shut, grabs me, and jumps out of the car. Jens curls up in pain. Carolin shuts the driver's side door and walks towards the house, then changes her mind and turns back to the car.

"Give Alexa a nice greeting. Tell her not to worry. A tetanus shot lasts for at least ten years, I believe."

"Hm, that's right. But I'm sure it will sort itself out. "

At that moment the doorbell rang.

"Do you expect any visitors?"

"No, I only invited you. Strange."

"Maybe a Fleurop bouquet with an apology from Mr. Uhland?"

"At nine o'clock at night? Unlikely. Besides, he definitely doesn't have a guilty conscience."

The doorbell rang again. Carolin stood up and went to the phone, which allowed her to hear who was standing outside.

"Hello?"

Now there was knocking as well.

"It's me, Daniel. I'm already standing at your door."

Carolin gave Nina a questioning look, who had also come into the hallway by now, and then she opened the door. It was indeed Daniel. However, he looked somehow different than usual. Somehow sad. And determined.

"Hello, Carolin. Sorry for disturbing you late at night, but I really need to talk to you."

Only now does he notice Nina. "Oh, hello!"

"Hello, Daniel! Are you okay?"

"Yes, of course. But I have something important to discuss with Carolin. Would it be alright if we left you alone? I know that's not very polite, but it's really important."

I noticed my neck hairs standing on end. The tone in Daniel's voice promised nothing good. Nina thought the same. She looked at Carolin questioningly.

"That's fine, Nina."

"Well then, I'll clear out. Bye you two, see you soon."

After she left, Daniel hung up his jacket and sat down on the sofa. Carolin followed him but sat in the armchair opposite.

"What's so important?" she wanted to know.

"I won't beat around the bush: I'll be gone for a quarter year next month."

"What?!"

"Yes. Aurora had asked me some time ago if I'd like to accompany her on a concert tour. I'm supposed to assess the violins that are offered to her at the same time."

"You want to travel with Aurora for three months? That's not serious! "

"It is. I have to go. You know, I thought I could handle it. Us and you. But I was wrong. I can't stand seeing you every day. And that's why I need some distance."

Carolin swallowed. "I'm sorry. I didn't know it would be this bad."

"Don't worry, I didn't either. Besides, you can't help it if you're not in love with me as much as I am with you. That's just the way it is."

"Will you come back? After three months?"

"To be honest: I don't know yet. But right now I don't want to think about that. Of course, I'll continue paying my share of the workshop costs. Don't worry about that."

Carolin stood up and sat down next to Daniel. Then she took his hand and squeezed it tightly. "Daniel, this is really the last thing I'm thinking about right now. I'm sad that it has to end like this because you're my closest friend."

"I know. But right now I can barely tolerate being your pal."

I was already fast asleep when someone shook me by my paw. I looked up. It was the old Mr. von Eschersbach! He glared at me angrily.

"Get up, good-for-nothing! You've had it comfortable here long enough. I've decided that Carolin needs some space from you. At least for three months. So take your chew toy and get out!"

My heart started racing. I want to hide. But where? Mr. von Eschersbach grabs me, there's no escape. Fearfully I whimper and try to duck under my cozy blanket, but he has me by the scruff already. Oh no! I'm going back to the shelter!

"Wake up, Hercules! You're dreaming!"

Carefully I look up - and see Carolin's eyes staring at me in surprise. "My goodness, you're making such a racket. Are you dreaming again about rabbit hunting?"

Rabbit hunting? If only she knew. I jump out of my bed and snuggle close to Carolin.

"You're shivering, poor thing. It must have been a nightmare, right?" She sighs. I do too. After all, I've also noticed that with humans everything is always complicated. In consolation, I lick her bare toes. She laughs. "That tickles, Hercules!"

With one swift motion she picks me up and puts me on her arm. "I have a very good idea how we can both spend the rest of the night a little quieter. You get to sleep with me again tonight. I don't feel like being alone right now either. Maybe I'm totally screwing up, but that doesn't matter now. On the other hand, what else could I have done? What Daniel wants is simply out of the question. I'm not in love with him. I had even hoped I could be. But it's just not working."

For a while she stays completely silent, so I think she must be asleep. But then she continues talking.

"And probably I don't need to bother Marc anymore either. Oh man, I think I've really messed up. After all, I found him quite interesting. Why did I tell him I didn't want to see him again? "

Yes. Indeed. Why exactly? Not a very clever move. I said it or would have, if only I could speak. But anyway, no one listens to me.

"Marc is nice, right?" I lick her cheek as confirmation. "See, Hercules, I do believe you like him too. I like him too. To be honest, what really bothered me was this whole Nina thing. She's my best friend after all. And having the feeling that she's still in love with him wasn't exactly pleasant."

Suddenly hope sparks in my plan for a happy family life. Perhaps we will still achieve our goal. How exactly, I don't know, but it can't hurt to position myself as a woman's man. So Carolin tells me a little more about Marc. I make an effort to look her in the eye as loyally as possible.

"Well, that is a real doggy stare. You think this with Marc was a mistake? Well, but Nina did overreact a bit. Poor thing. Of course, she's not exactly the most child-friendly, but to scold her in front of everyone? Not okay either." I blink and sniff at her again. "That does make him somewhat unsympathetic."

No way! I shake my head and growl a little.

"Well then, we're not in agreement there. I think it makes him a bit unsympathetic. In that sense, maybe it was the right decision. After Thomas, I'm really satisfied with my supply of choleric men."

Wuff! What is she trying to tell herself again? Who knows what exactly Marc said to Nina and vice versa. If I think about it carefully, Marc suits us best of all by far. So if it's up to me, Carolin should call him right away and put off the self-discovery thing for later. She can do that later on. I poke her in the side again. No reaction. Unbelievable. Carolin has actually fallen asleep in the middle of our interesting conversation.

But I can't fall asleep yet. Not yet. My thoughts are racing in my head. What do the things I've learned about people over the past weeks tell me about Carolin and men? First: She finds Marc nice. Second: But she doesn't want to talk to him because the conversations at the café have become unpleasant for her now. And third: That's why she tells herself that it wouldn't have worked anyway. Right, that has to be! Fourth: I need to get Marc to talk to her. But how am I supposed to do that? How am I supposed to do that?!

TWENTY-FIVE

So your theory is that Marc is indeed the right man for Carolin and she just doesn't want to admit it. Because then she would have to approach him, and that makes her uncomfortable. And that's why she has constructed something that supposedly doesn't fit him. Hm. "Mr. Beck looks very thoughtful." "Wow. You've learned a lot. Which isn't surprising, considering you had an excellent teacher."

"Yes, you're amazing. But what do you think I should do now? The matter is quite complicated. Unfortunately, I can't just march up to Marc and tell him. On the other hand, if he doesn't show up, then it will never happen between them." "Come on, make a call already!"

Mr. Beck nods. "Yes. Complicated. Really."

We fall silent. Then Beck continues: "In essence, you can only do one thing: Go to the practice and hope Marc takes it as a sign."

"A sign? For what? That he's being followed by a dachshund?"

Mr. Beck chuckles. "You see! You still have a lot to learn about people. So when people really want something, they tend to see signs everywhere. Usually, it's not even a sign at all. Let's say someone wants to have children. They'll surely trip over a stroller soon and take it as a sign that they're meant to have their own kids. In reality, of course, it's just a sign that someone put a stroller in their way." "Got it."

I don't quite understand Mr. Beck. What does a stroller have to do with Marc and Carolin? Obviously, I look confused because Mr. Beck shakes his head and looks at me indulgently.

"It's simple, Hercules: If Marc yearns for Carolin and then sees you, he'll take it as a sign that he should reach out to her."

"Yes, but that's what I intend too. That would be intentional."

Mr. Beck sniffs impatiently. "Of course. But Marc doesn't know that. He won't think a dachshund has a plan. He'll just see you as a simple animal. And he'll believe it's a sign. Got it?"

Frankly, I don't get it, but I can't admit that.

"So I go to Marc now and hope he sees me somehow?"

"Exactly. That's what you do."

I arrive at the practice and realize our plan has a significant flaw: It's quite noisy around here at this time, and Marc probably won't hear a barking dog. Plus, he won't be home; he'll be working in the practice. Even if I manage to get inside, I won't be able to slip past the woman at the desk. And without a dog owner accompanying me, they'll probably kick me out. Since my chase after Bobo and Snow White, I definitely have a questionable reputation with her.

So I sit for a while on the sidewalk in front of the entrance, thinking. Should I go home? Call Beck to help? No, my only chance is to get into the waiting room and be seen by Marc there.

As a woman with a cat in her arms approaches the entrance, I prepare to make a run for it. She rings the doorbell, the door opens, and I slip behind them. The cat looks at me amusedly. "Hey, little guy? Sneaking into the vet's office? Don't want your owner to believe you're sick?

I shake my head. "Nope, I'm on a secret mission. And if we're talking about sickness, I'd say my heart is broken. But not mine, my owner's. And Dr. Wagner too."

"So, he's in love then. The news will hit this practice like a bomb. I guess half of the patients here are only brought in because their owners want to talk to the vet." I'm delighted, of course. After all, the best or best enough for Carolin is good enough, and one doesn't want to get stuck with a dud. But it also makes me realize I need to act fast. The competition is already lining up at the starting blocks.

At the desk, the cat owner finally stops to register her pet. The young woman behind the counter looks first at the cat, then at me.

"Oh, do you have a dog too, Mrs. Urbanczik?"

She shakes her head. "Why? He came in with us." She gestures to me.

"I guess I just missed him. But this isn't my dog."

The young woman in the lab coat looks into the waiting room. "Does anyone recognize this dog?"

Three people sit on the plastic chairs, all shaking their heads silently. So if Wagner doesn't show up soon, my plan is a bust. Because the assistant will definitely kick me out. I give my most determined look.

"Hmm... he looks familiar to me. But without an owner, I can't really identify most animals." She ponders. "What do we do with you then? I don't want to just set you loose in front of the door. If you're truly alone, maybe we should take you to the animal shelter until your owner is found."

WUFF! Animal shelter? No way! Ugh, I've obviously just stabbed myself in the back. Ouch, how do I get out of this mess? And where's Dr. Wagner anyway? Just as I'm about to thank my creator, the door opens and instead of Marc Wagner, a little girl walks through. Today is not going well at all. The little girl looks at me. She has big blue eyes, brown curly hair, and many small brown spots on her nose.

"Are you next? What's your name?"

"We don't even know how he got here," the assistant explains to the girl. "I'll call the animal shelter right away."

"No!" The girl cries out. "He's so cute! I want to keep him. Wait, let me ask Dad first!"

The assistant smiles. "But Luisa, it's not that simple. I'm sure he already has an owner who will miss him soon. The animal shelter just keeps him until the owners show up."

The girl named Luisa puckers her lips. "He's so cute. I want to keep him!" She speaks and dashes off towards the study. Through the half-open door, we hear her talking with someone.

"Papa, there's a cute dog sitting outside all alone. Look, I think we need to help him. Can't we keep him? Mrs Warnke wants to take him to the animal shelter."

Papa? Who is this child speaking to?

The door to the study swings open again and out comes Luisa - followed by Marc Wagner! Wagner is "Papa"? Does that mean Wagner has a child? And a wife, too? I sink back in my chair, utterly bewildered.

"Hercules! What are you doing here?"

"Do you know this animal?"

"Yes, Mrs Warnke. This is Mrs Neumann's dog. Is he really alone?"

"Yes, he just came in when Mrs Urbanczik was trying to register her cat. I thought I'd have to call the animal shelter." But if you know the dog, then I'll simply call his owner now."

Marc Wagner ponders for a moment. "Wait a minute. And you come on in, Hercules."

"I want to come too!" Luisa calls out, following Wagner. As we all enter the study, Wagner shuts the door behind us. Then he lifts me onto the examination table and studies me.

"So, Hercules. Now tell me, is someone else in need?"

Luisa giggles. "But Papa, dogs can't talk."

"You'll be surprised, my dear. This one here already has!"

Right! To confirm this, I give a short bark. Luisa widens her eyes.

"So, does Carolin know you're here?"

I shake my head as best I can. Then I carefully grab a sleeve of Wagner's jacket and tug on it.

"I'm supposed to come with? To Carolin?"

I bark twice. I don't know if that's the right way to signal, but Mr Beck is not the one I'd ask.

"So, Hercules, I don't think that's such a good idea."

Ah. He already has a wife. That must be it. I sink my head in despair.

"Come on, don't be sad. I'd love to come along. But your mom has clearly said she doesn't want to see me anymore. Believe me, defying women is not a good idea."

No other woman? Just strategy? I'm somewhat confused, but I decide not to let it distract me. There's obviously still Wagner's interest in Carolin, and that should be enough. Perhaps there's also a good explanation for everything.

"I have a much better idea. For that, you need to be honest with me now. Do you remember when I examined you after your fit the other day?"

How could I forget? So I try to nod again.

"Very good. I had the distinct impression at the time that you're in excellent shape." Is it possible that this fit was an expression of your tremendous acting ability?

Trapped. How embarrassing.

"So, Papa, I don't understand anything anymore."

"Wait a minute, Luisa," Wagner says. "Hercules, come on: have another fit!"

Please, is that supposed to be a command? "Have a fit, go ahead!"

Alright then. He gets it. I let myself sink to the left side and start twitching my forelegs and hind legs simultaneously. I writhe, foam at the mouth, yowl - while making sure not to fall off the examination table. I think this is quite a convincing performance. Luisa widens her eyes even further, and Wagner grins.

"Impressive! Our little dachshund is a state actor. I knew it." He pats me on the head. "Okay, brave dog, you can stop now."

I lie still, with Luisa stroking my belly.

"That was like a circus act, Papa!"

"Exactly."

"What happens now?"

"Well, Hercules, you're supposed to run back home. And there, Hercules, you'll have another fit for your mom. I'd be very surprised if she doesn't call me after that. Then I'll come to the rescue." Does that make sense?

It does! That's a brilliant plan. He could've come up with it himself. I jump back up and give a short bark. Then Wagner lifts me off the table and takes me outside.

"You know what you have to do. I'm waiting for Carolin's call!"

I twist in painful spasms. This has to be the most convincing performance yet. Just as Daniel left today, I practically threw myself right in front of Carolin's feet in the workshop. She seems to believe me; she's white as a sheet from shock. I hope she reacts the way Wagner predicted.

In fact, she does! She picks up the phone!

"Neumann speaking. Is Dr. Wagner there? Thank you." She pauses briefly. "Hello, Marc, this is Carolin. I'm sorry to bother you, but Hercules just had another terrible fit. Worse than the last time. Yes?" She waits. "You're coming right over? Thank you so much, that's really kind of you." We're in the workshop.

Mission accomplished! I can let my fit wind down now. It was getting a bit tiring. I lie on my back and feign being completely exhausted. Carolin sits down next to me and pets me.

"Poor Hercules. You've made me so sad. But Dr. Wagner will be here soon, and then everything will be alright."

Not long after, the doorbell rings. Wagner must have left in a hurry. He comes in, sets his doctor's case down beside me, and examines me just like last time, muttering to himself from time to time, and then sits next to Carolin. Hm, hm.

"So, I can no longer rule out epilepsy. Here's what I propose:

I'm already heading to Schloss Eschersbach tomorrow morning anyway. How about if I pick both of you up early in the morning and we drive together? Then we'll quickly find out whether Hercules is truly a purebred and whether there's hereditary epilepsy."

"Yes," Carolin says quietly, "that sounds like a very good idea. I'd love to come along, thank you."

Schloss Eschersbach? With Carolin and Marc! Amazing! I want to jump for joy, but I don't; it wouldn't look very tired.

The sky is a brilliant blue. Just as it should be on such an important day. And this is definitely an important day; I have no doubt about that. I'll see Schloss Eschersbach and my family again. And if Wagner's plan works, there's still a chance for him and Carolin. I don't quite understand how he plans to make it work, but I trust that he's thought it through. As Mr Beck so wisely said: A man who needs a dog in order to win the woman of his heart has bad odds. So from now on, I'm staying out of it.

Impatiently, I wait for Wagner to finally arrive. Carolin seems nervous too; she keeps glancing at the clock. Then the window of the French doors shatters. Wagner - and he's brought Luisa with him.

Carolin opens the door.

"Hello! Ready for our little trip?"

"Yes, I'm all set." She looks at Luisa. Wagner follows her gaze.

"I'd like to introduce you to someone today: this is my daughter, Luisa."

Now that's a strange idea. A child of foreign dachshund females isn't usually well-received by other bitches, but let's hope humans are different. One look at Carolin's face tells me there are similar power struggles in the human world too.

"Я был в браке раньше. Луиза — моя дочь. Она обычно живёт с матерью, Сабиной. Но сейчас школьные каникулы, и Луиза всегда со мной. Я глубоко вдохнул. Должно быть, я должен что-то важное сказать. Ну, и поскольку вы обе так много для меня значите, я хотел, чтобы вы познакомились."

"У тебя дочь", — повторила Каролин, как будто не совсем поняв.

"Да. И какая замечательная девочка. Луиза протянула Каролин руку. Привет!"

Теперь Каролин улыбается. Мое сердце немного облегчилось.

"Привет, Луиза. Рад тебя познакомить."

"Можно в сад? Я видела там качели", — спросила она.

"Конечно, иди".

Когда Луиза ушла, они сначала ничего не говорили. Затем Марк чихнул.

"Луиза часто бывает у меня. Я не хочу быть просто папой на выходных. Я никогда не хотел этого. Я хотел, чтобы она как можно меньше страдала от развода. Сабина и я обсуждаем возможность того, что Луиза в будущем переедет ко мне. Сабина — стюардесса, и сейчас она хочет работать больше. Честно говоря, я с нетерпением жду этого. Конечно, это будет напряженнее, но я хочу разделить повседневную жизнь своей дочери со мной. Она растёт так быстро, и потом время пройдёт, и его не вернёшь."

"Ты из-за этого поссорился с Ниной? Потому что она сказала тебе, как отвратительны ей кажутся дети?"

Вагнер кивает. "И это тоже. Но не только это. Уже во время второго свидания я понял, что искры не прыгают. Но её вспышка гнева на пляже была уже довольно сильной. Я ещё не рассказывал ей о Луизе, но собирался это сделать. Ну, знаешь историю. Для меня дети очень важны. Мне сразу стало ясно, что это бессмысленно."

"Да, я понимаю, как это тебя ударило. Но когда ты сказал, что больше не хочешь со мной видеться, я бы предпочёл, чтобы ты рассказал мне всю историю с своей точки зрения. Но ты звучал так решительно, и я тоже не хотел говорить плохо о твоей лучшей подруге."

Каролин берёт его за руку и сжимает. "Я рада, что ты пришёл. В итоге я сама себя упрекаю. Я действительно очень люблю проводить с тобой время."

Вагнер улыбается. "Ну, мы, наверное, везём, что Геркулес так слаб по здоровью. Ха-ха!"

"О здоровье: ты думаешь, это плохо для Геркулеса возвращаться туда? В конце концов, эти люди отдали его в приют для животных?"

"Нет, наоборот. У него теперь большой выход. Он, возможно, станет собакой ветеринара."

"О, действительно?"

"Да, возможно. Или нет... Я просто шучу! Я абсолютно уверен."

Затем Марк прижимает Каролин к себе и нежно целует её в нос. В этот момент Луиза снова выходит из сада.

"Папа, ты такой неловкий!"

Марк отпускает Каролин. "Я влюблён!"

Каролин встаёт на носки и шепчет Марку что-то на ухо. Но так тихо, что я бы не услышал, даже если бы мои слух был превосходным: "Я тоже".